        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS. No man has so many offers of assistance as the man who needs none. THE GREAT POlNT.—Biggs: "Where is youe family going to spend the summer ?" Miggs " don't know yet. My wife hasn't found out which place is most expensive." NOT ExPLICIT.—" Sir," said the stranger, " am an artist." "So ?" queried the other, " What sort? Rszor, fiddle, brush, pugilistic, or stage MEMORIES OF THE METROPOLIS.—The Squire : "' So you've got back from London. How did you feel in such a big city ?" Father Stumpley " felt for my purse most o' th' tirae, surr." "How savagely that cow looked at me." "It's your red hat my dear." "Well! I knew it was a little out of fashion, but I didn't suppose a country cow would notice it." "NEVER write letters, young man, that you'll regret in after-life." "You speak as from ex. perience." "I do! In early correspondence with her who is now my wife I signed myself,' Yout obedient servant." "I'm making money by selling mice." "Whom do you sell them to?" "The professor of music it the next street." "What on earth does he want mice for?" "Why, he uses them for trying the voices of the young ladies." "CAN you change a £5 note for me ?" he asked, with an important air that nettled the courteous clerk. "Yes, sir; we can change anything here." Wish you'd change my mind, then, if you're sc good at it." "Have no small change, sir." "Mrs. HOLLINS is a splendid fellow, I think," said Miss Perkins. "Yes," said Ethel ; "but he is very absent-minded. When we were first en- gaged he used to call me Alice—he said that was his mother's name, which was also forgetful, because I've found out since it was Mary." "Get out of my way !" angrily shouted the man with the diamond scarf pin. "You're half-seas over !" " Yes, I'm half-seas over," replied the webbing pedestrian, "but I'll be all right to-mor- row. But there's no hope for you. Every morn ing you will get up the same sort of a vagabond.; THEIR AUNT: "And so you're going to buy your uncle a pipe with your pennies instead of spending them on chocolate?" The Dear Children: " Yes ; then uncle will give us a shillieg, and we can spend that on sweets." THE PHILOSOPHER: "A young man should begin at the bottom and work his way up." The Youth: "I can't very well do that. lam appren. ticed to a well-digger.' GUSUER : "So you're spending Christmas with your married sister, I presume, Miss Prinker ? Did you come down by the mail train, last night ?' Miss Prinker (blushing modestly): "Well, yes— .1 did. But—but—but—in a ladies' carriage." A NEW clergyman was taking Sunday duty in a hamlet among the Yorkshire Welds. After morn log service the clerk came up to him, and ob3erved "So ye call them Sauums, do ye? Noo, we never knew what to make o' that ere 'P.' We allus called 'em 'Spasms." Mits. WiciewlßE "What ridiculous, impossible things these fashion plates are." Mr. Wickwire: "I know they used to be, but most of them are engraved from photographs nowadays." Mrs. Wickwire: "This one can't be. Here are two woman going in opposite directions, both with brand new gowns on, and neither looking back at the other." "HAVEN'T you and your friend finished with that argument yet ?" "It isn't my argument," answered tho opinionated man resentfully. "I am merely telling him the facts in the case, and he is so thick he can't understand." WIFE: "You mustn't worry so much dear ; it will make you ill." Hasband : " Not ill, but cross-eyed." Wife: " Cross-eyed? " Hushand : "Yes ; trying to look at my income and my ex- penses at the same time." TE2VIIER : "If any pupil can answer, let her raise her hand. Well, Mary, you may tell." "Please, ma'am, I don't know. " Then why did you raise your hand?" "I couldn't help it, ma'am. Uncle John gave me this bangle last night." OLD LADY (on ocean steamer): "Mercy me! 13 this all one ship?" Travelled Grand-daughter: "Why, yes, grandma, and we haven't walked a quarter the length of it yet." " Gracious : How near will we be to America when we get to the other end?" "Mv husband,' says the large lady, "has a , habit of marking all paragraphs mu the paper that ' say mean things about women." "So that you will not fail to seem theme eh? Still, that is not as Mean a trick as mine plays. He cuts them all out. The:3 I have to get another paper, only to find that I have been made a silly of." A GOOD SIGN.—Mrs. Newwed "I can't undor- stand it, mamma, but Harry is an altogether different man since we were married." Mamma : "My dear child, I should be alarmed if he were not. A man who remained the same after mar- riage as he was during courtship would be an anomaly. Roally, I should fear tnere was some- thing wrong in his intellect." NEWSBOY : "Paper, sir?" City Man: " What papers nave you?" Newsboy : "Look here, mister, I've got ter work for my livin'. I know tie names of all Ili' papers I carry, but I ain't got no time to stand here and recite'em. Coma round some Sunday afterno..m." PAPA: "You saw that big boy whipping the little one, and you didn't interfere? Suppose you had boon that little boy?" Bobbie: "I did think . of that, an' was going to part 'ern, but then I hap. to think, s'pose I Was the big boy? So I left 'am alone." "I'M sorry about this war in South Africa." "It doesn't affect you personally." " Yes it does. Half-a-dozen girls have told me it was going to make dia:nonds more expensive. P'raps it was my egotistic imagination : hut every one of theirs seemed to have a 'now-is-the time-to-buy-engage- ment-rings' look in her eye." COCICNEY (to Rural Stationmaster): "What's on at the theatre, to night ? " Rural Stationmaster: "Theayter ! Why, we ain't got any, sir." Ccckney : "Oh ! Anybody particular at the music.hall, then?" Rural Stationmaster " Moo- sik 'all! There ain't any ; but there's shunting every night on the branch line, and everybody comes tip to see that." GRANDMA had been explaining to the little girl how our earth is kept from flying off into infinite space by the attraction of the sun, which is con- stantly trying to draw the earth towards itself, while the latter always keeps its distance. " Grand- mamma," said the little girl, " I should think the sun would get discouraged after a while and let ANNETTE : "I have just been having a delight- ful stroll with Harold. Can anything ba more poetical than a walk in the moonlight ?" Jean- nette (five years older): " Poetical, no doubt, Annette: but when you have hod my experience you will know that a dark corner of the stairs is equal to ten moonlight nights." FOR Two REASONS.—" I would go with you to the end of the earth," he asserted passionately. "Not with Me you wouldn't," she replied coldly. " Why not ?" he demanded. "For two reasons," she answered. "One is that I'm not going, and the other is that there isn't-any end." When one Meets the prosaic New Woman one has to be care- ful what he says. A FARMER once sent halfm-crown for a lightning caterpillar-killer, which he saw advertised in a paper, and received by return two blocks of wood, with directions printed on them as follows: "Take this block, which is No. 1, in the right band' place the caterpillar on No. 2, and press them together. Remove the caterpillar, and pro- ceed as before." "HAS George ever hinted that he had thought of you as a possible wife?" asked the anxious mother. "No," replied the girl, a far-away look in her eyes, "and I'm afraid he never will." " Why," said the mother, "1 thought—" "It doesn't matter what you thought, mamma, dear," interrupted the daughter. "Only last night he complained of feeling drowsy, and it wasn't nine o'clock. A LAWYER was cross-examining a bad-tempered woman, who was evidently a match for any man, whilst her husband, who was obviously the weaker vessel, sat sheepishly listening. The lawyer Was pressing a question urgently, when she said, with fire flashing from her eyes: "You needn't try to catch me, sir; you tried that once before I" " Madam," replied the counsel, "I haven't the slightest desire to catch you, and your husband looks as if he was sorry he did."</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>Mir Dun Numß, Recently I saw a charming gown which was of soft cream frieze, with a border heading the volant en forme in a design of autumn leaves and berries worked in natural coloured wools. This was re- peated on the bodice, running down the broad box pleats from throat to waist a small stock collar and waistband were of purple-tinted panne. For those who can afford it, nothing is so smart as cream cloth or frieze, or the light shades which are near akin to white, for walking costumes, trimmed with dark furs and applications of black velvet. It is not so ruinous a mode as would appear at first sight, for it is astonishing how long light things last if treated with ordinary care, and the brightening of the streets, so long accustomed to black, is a reasonable excuse in its favour.</p>
        <p>Velveteen blouses are only intended for after- noon or home evening wear, but there are some decidedly attractive models to be seen. The favourite colours seem to be coppery red, rich cornflower-blue and old-rose, and the trimming consists, in most instances, of ecru lace laid on white silk, or else of ivory or white satin almost covered with strappings of black or deep-coloured velvet ribbon. For a young girl nothing is much prettier than a blouse of cream esprit net, striped with lace insertion, and having elbow-sleeves and chemisette of unlined lace run with baby-ribbon. Or the sleeves can be made long and tight, with the newly-introduced puffs at the elbow. Crepe- de-Chine is another effective material, and many blouses made of it are most elaborately tucked all over in diamonds or squares, and have deep trans- parent lace yokes with unlined neck-bands. Some of the prettiest lace blouses are ornamented with applique of white silk, while others, equally stylish, have yokes and sleeves of tucked satin, the vest of the blouse being of thick yellowish lace over glace silk. The new delicate-coloured floral ribbons are used for blouses by joining with broad insertions of lace, and very charming they are, especially if made with vests and under-sleeves of soft yellowy-cream net, the former having straps of velvet with tiny paste or gilt buttons across it.</p>
        <p>Despite the long innings they have had, fur too_ues are still popular. A charming one is of sable, in the new shape, tied up at the sides by soft bands of chiffon, ending in a large bow which apparontly forms the crown; a handsome buckle is also placed at the side. Another hat has also fur for its chief ornament, sable tails mounted on a softly-puffed velvet foundation, with large velvet bow and buckle in front. The hats seen now are either made of twisted-up felt or cloth, or they are plateau ones of black beaver or bird's feathers. The ingenuity of the milliners seems at present to stop there. Even plumage trimmings must not mar the oxceedingly flat look it is modish for all toques to possess. Should a bird be worn, it must be a brooding bird lying with wings outspread on the round flat crown, if it be lace it must do no more than drape the brim and be tied at the back in a loose knot, a rule the lace on every second hat seen observes most faithfully, though in one model a little attempt at originality was made. In that case, even the lace was flat; it looked like a d'oyley that had been accidentally dropped upon a fur toque, and was of the very oddest effect.</p>
        <p>lcashionable milliners are insistent that the hail must be worn low on the neck; the half-way com- promise which is dear to the timorous finds no favour in their eyes, for the newest millinery makes the low coiffure a necessity. The coiffure , which is neither high nor low is rarely becoming, except to the Grecian type of head, and equally -seldom does it give the wearer a smart aspect. ,The adoption of low hairdressing for day-time is .only a mattter of time; for evening many people cling to the high style on top of the head, as giving 'height and dignity; but either manner is correct ,for evening. The tight skirt is more exaggerated and extreme than ever, and so long as this excessive tightness lasts there will be unwillingness to wear short skirts, for nothing could be much uglier than a very tight short skirt. Some of the newest gowns are fully gathered from waist to hem, the fulness 'being sewn in at intervals under a band of embroi- dery or passetnenterie. This may possibly indicate a total change, and it were devoutly to be wished, for the ultra-tightness of the gown of the moment has nothing to recommend it. It is inconvenient and inartistvi, sometimes worse than either. Your loving, JENNis.</p>
        <p>2 Mc DO UrG- Al I 4'S PATENT SELF—RAISING FLOU R MEAUINED</p>
