      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <p>a WOMEN'S CHAI.</p>
        <p>An account of:the Royal tour will shortly be issued, by the King's desire, in book form. Sir Donald Mac- kerzie Wallace, who accompanied the Prince and Princess of Wales on the " Ophir " will supervise the preparation of thie interesting book, and their Royal Highnesses will not only contribute to the letterpress, but the work will also be freely illustrated from photo- graphs taken by them. There will be a popular edition at a price which will place the book within reach of all, and another of a more expensive kind, which will make a souvenir volume of the historic tour. The wives of several of the commanding officers in South Africa, have recently left, in the hope of being able to spend Christmas with their husbands. Lady Roberts; it will be remembered, set this example,which has been followed by Lady Lyttelton, Lady Methuen, Lady French. ana others. Some two months ago, Mn Kitchener, the wife of General Kitchener,went out,but news has unhappily been received of her death in South Africa. She was, of course, the sister-lc-law of the Commander-in-Chief, who is regarded as a confirmed bachelor. Though not much past fifty Lord Kitchener is said to be somewhat ageing under the tremendous responsibilities that have rested upon him for the past year or more. The Board of Education has been overwhelmed with enquiries from women teachers, who are desirous to go to South Africa: The Colonial Office it will be remem- bered, wanted 10C teachers for the Boer children in the camp, and the terms offered are £lOO salary for one year, with free passage, and board and lodging. There is an official form to be filled in, but though a great many applications have already been made, no appoint- ments will be settled until the end of the month, and priority, or lateness of application will not affect the candidates' chance. Preference will be given to those of strong physique, and who are able to teach the rudi- ments of singing. Lord Milner it seems, has specially mentioned the latter qualification, apparently with a view to teaching the Dutch children the National An- them. The government are also now arranging to give free passages to women and children, belonging to men who have been in military, or other aervice, during the war, and who are desirous of settling in South Africa. Lord Dufferin the other day, made some comparisons not wholly favourable to modern conditions of social life. Amorsgst other signs of degeneracy, he associated a relaxation of dignity and reserve in our gills. Modern conditions, it is true, have greatly altered feminine demeanour, and it is no doubt greatly due, as Lord Dufferin suggested, to the unwillingness of men to sur- renderithe luxuries of freedom for the straitened existence of the married state. Women have had to seek their own amusements, and their own means of liveli- hood, but the change of manners which these altered conditions have brought about, is Lot one that many C! us would renounce if we could. In the "Age of the Dandies," the ceremonious chivalry of the men was the counterpart of the meek simplicity of the women, but in this, as in other things, those who sigh for the " good old times," are led away by the imagination, and there is probably more real, genuine, and sincere politeness in these days, than there ever was in the obsequious- ness which did duty for these virtues, a century ago. Ermire is unquestionably the most porn fur among fashionable women, chiochillla ranks second in their affections, and sable apparently third. As a matter of fact the order ought to be reversed, if what is becoming telplaced before what is fashionable. Sable is the most becoming of all furs, while ermine is, to the generality of women, the most unbecoming. It requires the tex- ture of youth, and admirable colouring to wear ermine without being rendered sallow or soiled looking. If there is any lurking muddiness in the complexion this par- ticular fur will hunt it out, and keep it out. Acd grey fur, unless it be the silvery grey of chinchilla or silver fox, is little, if any more desirable, The fur known as blue fox, and all its imitations, is (Attie most hopelessly hard and trying shade of grey. 11 rather resembles the tint of wet slates, and only the most brilliant complex- ions can stand the proximity with impunity. For those;to whom good dark sable is an impossibility there is skunk, which comes nearest to sable in depth of tone, and is very much cheaper, and for those whose purse strings will not stretch to skunk, there are the dainty fur-edged glace silk ruffles and muffs to match, which are so much to be preferred to any sets of imitation furs. To the woman clever with her needle, and quick at grasping details, the building of the ruffle, muff, and tcque is a simple matter, and if necessary, may be an inexpensive one. Fur is appearing on many of the smartest winter gowns, wide bands forming a heading to the flounce, while finger widths of the same fur appear on the bodice. Such aTrimrning is invari- ably becoming, but should only be invested in by women whose toilettes are fairly numerous. Our seasons seem to have got mixed up of late years, and there are many days in winter and early spring when a tur-trimrce I gown would be an impossibility. By the impecunious fur should be arranged so that it can be donned and removed, as circumstances and the climate render desirable.</p>
        <p>Many women there are who possess and like white furs, yet refrain from wearing them regularly on account of the cleaner's bill. By a simple process however, white furs can be kept in such excellent condition as to need very occasianal journeys to the cleaners. Take some oatmeal—not the coarse, or floe, but the medium, damp it with cold water and rub well into the fur. Allow it to dry,then rub with dry oatmeal, and shake thorough- ly. Some women pin their faith to an equal mixture of oatmeal and table salt, applied in a dry state, this is a much quicka process, but not so cure a one when the furs are much soiled.</p>
        <p>_ There are a few, but very few women celebrities in this country,who are able to obtain their toilettes for no other consideration than the advertisement they afford any particular firm of dressmakers. In Paris, and also in America, the practice has a much wider vogue, and there are French houses which bestow the most expen- sive costumes on leading members of the social and artistic classes, for nothing else than tbe advertisement they thus obtain for their wares. No is there anything new in this custom, as, according to Mrs Bryne's " Gos- sip of the century," the famous Count D'Orsey used not only to get his things for nothing, but was paid by his tailor for wearing them. They were always sent home with a banknote, and once when the piece of paper was missing, the count, so the story runs, sent them back to the tailor with a message that he had forgotten to line the pockets. Those- of us on the look-out for Christmas gifts for , the little ones, will appreciate the many charming fairy books that have made their appearance lately. Among the most delightful may be mentioned the "Violet Fairy Book" (Longmans, Green and lCo.) Five and shirty fairy tales—all of the good old fashioned sort that will bear telling and retelling a hundred times—are to be found between the handsome blue and gold covers, and eight gorgeously colouted illustrations, net ta mention numerous less gorgeous ones. Another very fascinating took just published by this firm, and which will afford the young peaple and others more advanced delight for many a day to come, hears the title of " Old Bal'ads in Prose." What the children of tc-dilly would think of ohe books and toys that fell to their mother's lot, ic is difficult to imagine. A doll, a puzzle, a Noah's ark, and very little in the way of literature was almost all that was thought necessary for the small folks in those times nowadays they are catered for as carefully as their eldors. The most elaborate toys, the most beautiful of books, fall to their share. - -</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>It is intended to apply for Parliamentary powers for the construction of a " twopenny tube," to be known as the Manchester City Circle Railway, connectisgetahrreyfiinvge principal railway termini of the city. A steamer has been se zed and detained in the Thames contrabandb y t he Customs war. authorities When til eo n Customssu s pi c i officerso n ofboarded the vessel they found, it is stated, four field guns and a largo quantity of stores. The steamer 'lto afforded accommodation for some 500 or 600 men.</p>
      </div>
