      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>LICENSING ANOMALIES. Warrington Magistrates and the Birmingham Suggestons.</head>
        <p>LICENSING ANOMALIES. Warrington Magistrates and the Birmingham Suggestons.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Borough magistrates me in the magistrates' ro'sm at the new Police Court on Monday morning " to arrange for the attendance of magistrates at the Court House during the inurine year and for the transaction of other business." His Worship the Mayor (Mr J Charlton Parr) presided and there were altogether 40 magistrttes present. The formal business having been disposed cf. the following circular and resolutions received from the Clerk to the Licensing Justices for the city of Birming- ham were laid before the meeting :—" Victoria Courts, Birmingham' 31st October, 1901. Dear sir.—l am de- sired by the Justices of this city to forward you copy of a resolution passed by them at a meeting held on the 23rd October lost, and to ask you to be kind enough to lay the same before the Justices of your borough at an early date. The Justices of this city are of opinion Oat having regard to the recent utterances of the Colonial I Secretary on these points, the time is favourable for re- moving these anomalies, and that it is advisable that de- putations from the larger cities and boroughs should wait upon the Home Secretary with the view of inducing him to introduce a Bill into the House of Commons, which, if passed, would enable Justices to carry out the objects of the resolution. The Justices of this city hope that the Sue- tines of the different cities and boroughs who are invited to cc-operate in this matter will be willing to limit their action to these two points. An early reply will oblige. —Yours faithfully, W.• BAHNDALE, clerk to the Licenr- ing Justices." The resolutions read as follows :—" (1), That in the opinion of this meeting it is expedient that the magistrates should have the same powers and duties in regard to the grant and renewal of licences to sell wines and spirits by retail, and to beerhouses licensed before 1869, as they now have in regard to victuallers and beer off licenses." " (2) That a magistrates' licence should be required for the sale cf beer, wines,and spirits at clubs."</p>
        <p>The first resolution was carried unanimously, but an amendment was carried in place of the second resolu- tion to the effect that the justices. vhilst not going to the extreme of this resolution, are prepared to support any lrgislation for the suppression of bogus drinking clubs.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable stated that he did not know of any bogus clubs in Warrington for the specific purpose of private drinking. There were great numbers else- where,and he understood that the resolution in question was aimel at such clubs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>AN EPISODE IN THE LIFE OF A MANCHESTER GIRL.</head>
        <p>AN EPISODE IN THE LIFE OF A MANCHESTER GIRL.</p>
        <p>Comment is frequently beard on the rapid changes in some portions of Manchester. Now that portion known as Beswick, with the exception of a few manufactories, has, within a short time, been completely covered with residental property, occupied chief y by small tradesmen and working men. One of the newest streets is Cros- ton-street, filled by artisans. A rather remarkable occurrence on this street recently has created something of a sensation in the neighbourhood. For about twelve months No 45 has been tenanted by Mr Wood, an employe"; of the Great Northern Railway Co., whose daughter, Marjorie Eva, now five years of age, has, within that period, undergone an experience to which it would be difficult to find a parallel. Her story, as told by her mother, Mrs. A. E. Wood, and repeated by the neighbours, reached the ears of a Manchester re- porter, who, in order to verify what bad come to his knowledge, called upon Mrs Wood a few days ago, and took from her own lips the following facts, " Before my daughter, Marjorie, was five years of age she began to exhibit peculiar twitchings of th; muscles of the hands and arms, which in time extended to the face, the eyes, and mouth. She also used to com- plain of her legs aching, and (showed signs of languor and weariness. When in bed at nights there was a twitching of the limbs, accompanied by great restless- ness. For some time I did not think there was any- thing seriously wrong with the gill. I thought it was a bad habit she had got into, and that she would soon get over it. As the symptoms grew gradually worse I became alarmed and took her to a doctor, who told me she was afflicted with St. Vitus' Dance, and that if not checked it would soon become a serious matter. Of course he prescribed for her, and the child took his medicine, but as far as I could see it did her no good whatever. She got worse." "Did you take her to another doctor ? " "No, I did not ; I was walking out with Marjorie one day when her condition attracted the attention of a lady, who was passing. The lady asked me if I had not taken the girl to a doctor. I replied that I had done so, and that he bad told me she was suffering from St Vitus Dance; that he had prescribed for her,but his medicine; had made her no better. The lady then said : "I know a little girl who bad St Vitus' Dance, who was Cured by taking Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people.' I said I bad heard of them, but had not taken any, What the lady bad said, however, induced me to try a box, and I wrote to London for one."</p>
        <p>" London! Wbv they are sold all over _ Manchester."</p>
        <p>" fe;;flineW Icoula get them here but I wanted Dr. Williams' advice about the case, and I wrote to the firm." " Now, tell me the result of your application." " Well, they answered my letter, and sent me a box of Dr Williams' pink pills, with instructions, which I followed, and before the girl had taken half the pills I noticed a great improvement. In fact, she began to get better after the first two or three " How many boxes did she take ?" " Only one box. One box quite cured her." "It seems a remarkable cure, because young girls affected with St. Vitus Dance seldom get better before a certain stage of life is reached." And so I think," said Mrs. Wood, with fervency, " and I was so dclighted that I wrote to Dr. Williams' medicine company.; and I told thern that • words could not express the pleasure I felt in doing so.' " " Can you tell me exactly the effect of Dr. Williams' pink pills upon yrur daughter 2" "She only took one pill Pee day. The first thing I noticed was that the twitchings of the mouth ceased : then the eyes got better ; next the hands end arms ; and lastly, the shoulders and other parts of the body." " Did she take anything else besides taking Dr. 1 Williams' pink pills ?'' No ; only that after the twitchings had disappeared, according to the instructions I had received, I was, if possible, to send her somewhere for a change of air, 80 I sen; her to Didsbury for a fortnight." "And there has been no return of the disease ? "Not the slightest." Little Marjorie was present during the interview, and amused herself part of the time by playing hide and seek with a cupboard door near the parlour window, and par ly by romping about the room in the noisiest manner, showing unmistakably that she was in robust health. " Are ycu willing that the particulars of your daughter's case should be made public 2" " Yes, decidedly ; I shall only be too pleased to let others know the cure that has been effected in mv daughtei 's case. I cannot speak tco highly of Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people, and I shall always have pleasure in recommending them," Mrs Wood also pave the readiest consent to her daughter's photograph being published in connection with the account of her case.</p>
        <p>THE LIVERPOOL-MANCHESTER LIGHTNING EXPRESS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>When Operations Will be</head>
        <p>When Operations Will be</p>
        <p>Commenced.</p>
        <p>Much progress is being. made with the multifarious preliminaries connected with the scheme for the moto- rail cl lightning express " between Liverpool and Man- chester. As tar as can at present be calculated. th e actual work of constructing the line will be commenced in the spring of 1902. As is usual in such undertakings operations will begin at three or four points on the route to be traversed. The electric generating station will be at Warrington. It is not at all improbable that *tart will be made simultaneously at the two extremitiestof tbe route—Liverpool and Manchester—and at Warring- ton, which may for practical purposes be regarded as somewhere about half-way along the line.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRID Y NOVEMBER 22, 1901</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>(Copyright.) A QUEEN'S SOLDIER.</head>
        <p>(Copyright.) A QUEEN'S SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>BY WALTER. WOOD,</p>
        <p>Author of "Barrack and Battlefield," "Famous British Warships," "Through Battle to Pro- motion," "A Captain in the Fusiliers," "A Soldier of the Legion," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIII, THE ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>Bonn was a sluggish thinker, but when once his mind was made up he allowed no deviation from his purpose. After much consideration he had determined to escape; there was no going back on that resolve. Now that he had formed the idea that he might find an ally in Boota, the burgher, he was resolved to spare no effort to secure his help. But Benn was not so dull as to be unaware of the fact that he must act with extra caution, and accordingly he summoned all the craft in his nature to assist him in an interview which he had sought with Boota. The watch which was kept over Bonn was by this time far less strict than that which was maintained upon his comrades. It was felt that a cripple like Mark could not escape, even if the means to do so were afforded him, and that if he should by any possibility get clear of the capital he could be easily re-taken. He was occasionally, as an invalid, allowed to walk within the fenced enclosures when the rest of the prisoners were kept rigidly within doors, and soon after his last intimate talk with Wade, while enjoying this privilege, he was strolling aimlessly about when he noticed that the guard had been relieved, and that one of the few members of it was Boota.</p>
        <p>The burgher was leaning against one of the posts to which the barbed wire was fastened when Mark approached him, hobbling more than usual. To the Yeoman it seemed advisable to simulate help- lessness and feebleness to the best or his power. , "I suppose I oughtn't to speak to you," begal Mark, "but you're not as strict in your regulations as we are. I believe, though, that lots of our men had good times with the guards, which makes it pleasant for both sides." The burgher looked cautiously around him before replying. Seeing that they were unwatched and could not be heard, he said: _ .</p>
        <p>"We are not so very strict, ad in your case it does not matter."</p>
        <p>"You looked lonely, and I felt lonely," said Mark, "so I thought I'd come on. You must get pretty sick of work like this." "Your leg is very stiff," replied Boota, warily, but with so much sympathy that Berm shuddered Involuntarily at the thought of what Wade had said concerning this sentry, if it should have proved possible to kill him on his post. "Yes," replied Benn ; it doesn't mend. I suppose I shan't get better now till our own doctors have had a go at me. That can't be till the war's done; unless some of the prisoners are exchanged." He looked at the burgher as he spoke of an exchange, but the man took no apparent notice of his words or glance. "The war will not be over yet," said the sentry, quietly. "I wish you'd give it up," said Mark, earnestly. "And why ? " asked the sentry in astonishment. "Why should we give it up? it is you who have brought it on us, and the issue is with God. He will give victory to the side of the oppressed and we shall conquer." Mark was surprised, but quick to see his mistake. He set to work at once to rectify it, and it seemed he could best do this by letting his companion talk. "You have made us to be dragged from our homes, our farms, our crops, to be forced to leave the plough and seize the rifle. Our crops are rotting, and our men are being killed and dying. But you will pay a terrible price for it. You have already paid a big one. Look at your people here and think of your dead." The burgher spoke with solemn emphasis. "Yes, it s pretty awful," Mark admitted. "But you like the fighting, don't you ? " , "Like the fighting," repeated Boota, pas- sionately. "I am a farmer, nothing else. What can fighting be for me? It has torn me away, and my father, too, from home, and the farm is left to be looked after by my mother. She is strong, she is able, and she loves the work, but it is terrible that the war should take us both like this. And we do not know how things go. It is three months since we saw her, and two since we heard. At times I feel as if I would risk all and go." "You can understand, then," said Mark,' feeling that if he meant.to speak at all it must be now, "how sick us poor devils are to get away? " "Yes, yes," the burgher admitted hastily. "But I cannot help it." "You are only a few score miles from home, you can pretty well tell where it is when you look the right way; but we are thousands." "It is so, said the burgher. "My heart would break if I were held like that. I love my home so well that I would never leave it, only for the trips we make to this place twice a year." Mark began to breathe more freely. He had sounded the sentry and felt that he was sympa- thetic. He could now touch upon his tenderest chord. _</p>
        <p>..,...„...... "You say you haven't been home for three - months?" he said.</p>
        <p>"That or longer," answered the burgher. "I was there sin weeks since," said the Yeoman, quietly. "What!" exclaimed the burgher. "You at my home—at the farm ? " .</p>
        <p>"Yes," answered Mark. '"Anyway, that was the name. Yours is Boots, isn't it?"</p>
        <p>"Yes, yes," said the burgher, excitedly. "And it's exactly over there, by the railway 2," said Mark, pointing to the south-east. •It wag a guess, but he had his reasons for wishing to be correct. "That is true," said the burgher. "Straight as the crow flies and you get there." "And your mother—is she a dark, short woman, with braided hair and gold earrings? She speaks good English, and almost looks like an English- woman ? " ' -</p>
        <p>"It is so," exclaimed the burgher, his imagina- tion supplying the details which Mark had failed to give. "But how do you know her ? " "I was taken there when I was caught. It was Mrs. Boota, your mother, who bandaged my shoulder and had a go 'at the leg. She was there when I was lying on the couch where they'd planked me. We'd had a fearful long ride, and I must have gone silly. I was to tell you, if ever I met you, that everything was going on well, as well as could be. I should have told you before only I didn't know who you were." . "Ah, how thankful I am," exclaimed the burgher. "It will not be long, if God is good, before we are back again. He is giving you into our hands. To-night we have coming nearly a thousand more of your officers and men that have surrendered. I ought not to tell you, but it will cheer you, for it will bring the day of your deliverance nearer. I hope you will soon be at your own home. Now you must go away. It will be dark very soon and you must not be out. I will talk with you again. See, there is a small present ' for you. It will help you to pass your idle hours. There are many little things you can do with it, if they let you; but do not say I gave it." He slipped a many-bladed knife into Mark's hand •fts be spoke, and then walked a few paces away. Berm saw that he must look upon the interview as ended, but he felt that he had been almost more than successful. Whether the burgher knew the value of his gift or not Mark could not tell, but a glance had shewn him that amongst the tiny implements in the knife was a nipper—the means of enabling him to cut through his most formidable obstacle, the wire fencing, if he could get away from his prison and take advantage of the commotion which the arrival of new prisoners would create. By some means the news of the arrival of more prisoners had become known to the captives. The intelligence was received with varied emotions. Wade frankly expressed satisfaction on the ground that the more British prisoners the Boers held the less was the disgrace attaching to the capture of Yeomen. ;lc ilars are nailed like this," he</p>
        <p>"If the crack regu. said, "I don't see why us raw chaps should worry our heads at being caught. All the same, we've been badly packed enough so far; what will it be like when the fresh lot comes? If anything like disease breaks out it'll have pretty fair field, won't it? However, as I've said before, it's the fortune of var." Mark, to whom he spoke, did not answer. He was too full of his own scheme to be interested even in the coming of the new captives, except in so far as their arrival helped his own escape. He determined that he would now attempt to gat away at the very earliest moment, that he would allow no reasonable chance to slip past. It seemed to him that there must be opportunities a elie,aping I.2efopikielt passed.</p>
        <p>It was lake when it became generally known that a big batch of prisoners was on its vr ay to Waterfall. The burgher guards were quite as much excited as the captives; and some of them, in view of this fresh access of good fortune, were disposed to allow more latitude than usual. "There are cavalrymen coming," said Boota to Mark and Wade. "You can be in the enclosure to see them. You are cavalry, too. It will be like welcoming broth ers. " Mark's heart thumped with pleasure. He almost wondered whether Boots had done this deliberately, whether it was the natural sequence to his presentation of the knife. He looked sharply at the burgher, but gathered nothing from the dark, impassive face. Without saying anything even to Wade he hobbled into the enclosure, keeping near the Vuildings, the dwellings which hummed with the round of human voices and the clang and clatter of making ready for the new -comers. Listening acutely, he heard the tramp of feet in the distance, mingled with the thud of hoofs. The sounds became clearer, and amid them he could distinguish unmistakable English voices. "It's the fresh lot!" exclaimed Wade. "I'll scoot round here, so that I can get a glimpse of 'em. You'll join me, won't you ? " "Yes—don't wait," said Mark. "Hurry on; don't miss any of the sport on my account. His comrade disappeared, eager to get the first sight of the new arrivals. "Now," murmured Bean, taking a deep breath. "Now for it. In ten minutes I shall either be a free man or a dead man."</p>
        <p>He began to walk across the enclosure and made his way, stealthily, to the spot at which Boota had been on sentry. He stooped and listened, and peered into the darkness. But he saw no form, he heard no movement near him—only the loud commotion of the marching of the captives. They were almost at the rows of dwellings. He heard an order given, and the prisoners halted. Evidently they were to be sorted and removed in small bodies to their prisons. Again Mark listened and looked, but there was no sign of anyone being near him. l3oof a was helping with the prisoners. Then lie set to work with his little wire-cutters, which he had opened from the blade, and snipped viciously at the fencing near the post. The wire was cut with the nippers almost as easily as string is cut with scissors, and he stepped warily through the fence. Still with the help of his stick he hobbled across broken ground until he came to a second fence. This also he cut, and so got free of the enclosure and the prison. By this time he heard the tramping afresh, and judged that the prisoners had been sorted and were being marched off to their quarters. He took a long, careful look at the buildings he had just left, then set his face resolutely towards the exact point which he knew to be the south-east, and, with a feeling of intense exhilaration, began his journey. His clothing was in rags, his boots were shreds of leather, and his headdress was such as he might have used as a cleaning-cloth at home. But he had his compass, his knife and his freedom, and he was happy. Besides the compass and knife, Mark possessed a small box of meat lozenges and a larger box of chocolate. These he had secured with great difficulty, and, at Wade's suggestion, had fastened them firmly on the inside of his jacket. Ile, therefore, had them with him at all times, so that if his chance of escape should come unex- pectedly he would be prepared to take it. Gaol was behind him ; liberty and Lizzie were ahead. So keen was his delight that it was not until he was a mile from the prison that he remembered he was an injured man, and that to the perils and privations of an escape across the country must be added the heavy drawback of a serious wound. In spite of his courage, there arose before him that picture of himself dying on the veld from starvation and exhaustion which Wade had drawn in lurid colours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>CHAPTER. XIV.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER. XIV.</p>
        <p>LIBERTY AND LICK.</p>
        <p>But B'enn did not allow this recollection to trouble him for long. He was fairly committed now to his scheme, and there was no going back upon it. One thing only rPmained to be done, and that was to get as far away as possible from the capital before the day broke. There were still several hours of darkness, and Benn hoped that he might hobble on at the rate of two miles an hour. If he could do that, or a little more, he might cover fifteen miles. Then perhaps he could find some hole or crevice in which he could hide and I. sleep until the night came again, and he could resume his flight. Just outside the enclosure, he kicked something with his foot. Bending down, he found that he had come in contact with a small branch which in idle moments a burgher or prisoner had whittled into the semblance of a walk- ing-stick, and had then thrown away. The stick was rough but strong, unlovely but useful ; and, as it had something in the nature of a handle, Mark found that it would be an excellent help for:him in walking. The stick would also, to some extent, aerve as a defensive weapon. The greatest fear that the fugitive had was that his absence would be discovered at once, and that he would be pursued and re-captured before he had gone more than a few hundred yards away. But as the minutes passed, and he heard no sign that he was followed, his courage grew greater, and be made his way sturdily towards the pointj which he had set for himself. He had been walking for an hour, as it seemed to him, well away from the prison buildings and towards the spot which Boots had indicated, when he suddenly found that be had returned on his track, and was actually going back to Waterfall. The dis- covery stunned him for a moment, but a moment only. In that brief space of time a new and better plan had come to him; instead of drearily making his way dangerously across the open country until he should have the good fortune to inset a British outpost, he would strike the Delagoa Bay Railway and follow the line until friends were reached. Accordingly lie made his way cautiously towards the metals, and, having reached them, felt that his chance of getting clear away was greater than before.</p>
        <p>It began to be apparent to him now that if his escape had been discovered, no hurry was being sliewn in following him ; for that matter, the people would be so fully occupied with the new prisoners that they could afford to let one, and that a cripple, get away. In truth, it was not until next day that the fact was evident that Benn had escaped. There was the proof of the cut fence, the non-appearance of the prisoner. But the responsible officer, on finding out, merely laughed. With so many new captives, he said, there could be no time to search for and bring in a wounded prisoner. Besides, famine and suffering would be his greatest foes in case the Boer commandos should let him slip through their cordons.</p>
        <p>By the time it was known that Berm had escaped, he had got a dozen miles from the capital, and the day being then breaking, he sank into a 'dry ditch, ate a tablet of chocolate and a meat lozenge, and, with his thirst heavy upon him, stretched his aching limbs and fell asleep. He was thoroughly exhausted, but his heart did not fail him. He had heard many stories of the wonderful endurance of wounded men in this war, and saw no reason why he should not do as well as any of them had done. -</p>
        <p>It was high noon when he awoke. He raised himself on his elbow and looked about. The railway, he saw, was very near him ; he saw, too, that the point was considered to be a vulnerable one, for it was grded carefully. Within a few hundred yards of him was a Boer encampment. Benn, on making tins discovery, lay eat in the ditch, suffering intensely from thirst and the heat. A few dry branches were near him, and blades of lifeless grass. These he slowly gathered, -tud with them made a slender shelter for his head. 4;ith his face and head thus covered, it seemed almost as if his body formed part of the dry earth itself, for the tattered khaki was scarcely to be distinguished. from the bed and sides of the trooper's hiding-place. Throughout the daylight Benn remained almost motionless, sometimes dozing, but never ceasing to listen for any sounds from the railway or the encmnpment. From time to time trains went past, some to the capital, some from it. One train, which seemed both long and heavy, was stopped for several minutes abreast of him. He heard the chatter of many voices, and was hardly surprised to discover that the speakers were Englishmen. The explanation was simple: here was another load of prisoners of war for the capital. Benn could not resist the temptation to raise his head very cautiously and peer through the shelter of branches. He saw that from the trucks and carriages of which the train was made men were thrusting their heads for air and a sight of the landscape. One of the trucks nearest him, he saw, was packed with prisoners who had been his companions on board the transport. They were cheerful to the point of hilarity, and were energeti- cally. discussing the extent to which they could shower ironical compliments on the President of the Transvaal when they had the pleasure of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>beholding him. They made repeated allusions to his whiskers, and the joy of pulling them. "Hullo! I saw rabbit in that ditch over there !" cried a soldfEr in the truck. "Rats!" exclaimed a comrade, contemptuously. "There aren't any rabbits in these parts." "It was something, I tell you something movin', an' khaki-coloured, too." "Which ditch ? " asked the comrade, becoming</p>
        <p>interested. "That straight opposite—just over there, with the bundle o' sticks at one end of it. That one that looks just like a grave." "Come, we've had enough o' graves," rejoined the comrade. "Every ditch an' hole's been one in this confounded country, so far. Ugh! Fancy fightin' for a land like this! Just look. Is it worth it? It seems to me there'd be as much sense in fightin' for one or two hundred thousand miles of sandy beach." "Never mind that," persisted the man who had spoken first. "I tell you I saw something move in that ditch, an' I'm goin' to rout it out. Look out, make way, I'm goin 'to have a shot at it wi' this." "Three shies a penny," jeered the comrade. "If you knock the cocoanut off, or strike old Krooje.d on the beak, you get three shots more for nothing. If you're goin' to aim at the ditch, Cocky, sight yourself for fifty yards either way, then you very likely won't miss." There was a roar of laughter. Cocky was notoriously incompetent at the range, and had his own extraordinary theories as to shooting. He was generally supposed to possess some unknown obliquity of vision, which could be rectified only by aiming at some object quite apart and distinct from thAarget. "Duck your heads, boys, or hell brain the lot," said the comrade, warningly. "A fair field an' no favour," cried a man who made a great pretence of being terrified at Cocky's preparations. "I should feel safer in the boiler of the injun, but I'll risk it. If he doesn't hit the rabbit, boys, we'll wipe the truck with him an' give him a bath in the coal-dust." Cocky, goaded to desperation by these taunts and jeers, prepared his missile, whatever it was, and Calculated the distance to Bean's hiding-place. Mark, who had involuntarily made the movement of his body which had attracted the soldier's sharp eye, held himself rigidly, powerless to help himself in case the soldier should by any chance have in his hand a deadly weapon. "Now for it I" cried several voices. There was a whistling sound in the air, then a thud in the very ditch, and Benn knew that he had been struck, but not hurt, by some heavy article. What it was he could not see; nor dared he move hand or foot to touch it. He kept perfectly motionless. • "He's ackshally done it!" shouted a soldier. "I knew he'd hit it, for I saw 'itu aimin' for the injun right ahead." "Who saw the rabbit ? " cried another, and there was a fresh shout of laughter. "The bottle killed it," exclaimed a third soldier, "By George! they'll stop it out of your pay, Cocky." "You'll be court-martialled for it." "On active service it's a big crime for a man to chuck his kit away." "I've got an idea," roared a red-haired private, breaking in upon these comments. "We'll promote Cocky from third-class shot to marksman, an' call him Bunny for the future. Eh, boys? " "Give him his badge on the spot," exclaimed a soldier. They fell upon the hero of the moment, dragged him to the floor of the truck, and while he was held there made a couple of marks with coal-dust on the left sleeve of his khaki jacket, to indicate the crossed rifles of the marksman. Cocky, hot and cursing, struggled to his feet and fell upon the soldier nearest to him. While the uproar was at its height the train started with a jerk. The combatants were thrown off their feet, and in a final roar of laughter the last stage of the prisoners' journey to the capital was resumed. For some time after the rumbling of the train had ceased to reach him, Mark remained perfectly still. Then he slowly put an arm down to where the object that had struck him lay. A thrill of delight passed through him when he found that the missile was nothing more deadly :than a water- bottle. He pulled thevessel up, knowing from it weight that it was full of liquid, and having removed the stopper he placed the neck to his mouth and took a draught. The liquid was tepid, thick, muddy water, but Mark found it delicious. But even with his thirst upon him he was frugal and thoughtful'of the morrow, and having carefully. stoppered the bottle he put it inside his jacket. It was now easier for him to lie still, although his cramped position caused him discomfort that bordered closely on pain. Throughout that long, hot afternoon he kept to the ditch, sometimes dozing, sometimes with his eyes open, staring at the dry, cracked earth a few inches from his face, but never ceasing to be on the alert. His position was one of extreme peril, for at any moment a patrol might start from the neighbouring encampment and discover him. Even if the eye of the enemy failed to detect him a slip of a horse's foot might make further concealment impossible. As the day wore on Mark noticenhat darkness was coming more quickly than usual, and that the air was intensely oppressive. He felt that a thunderstorm was gathering overhead, and soon there were appalling flashes 'and crashes, with a deluge of rain. Very quickly the ditch was so well filled with water that to savd himself from being drowned Mark was forced to raise himself and rest his head on the edge. Even then he was lying in what was really a bath, his body up to the neck being completely immersed. The huge drops of rain beat mercilessly upon his face and head, and it seemed as if it would be impossible to escape being struck by lightning. One fearful flash seemed to rend the very earth a few yards from him, and involuntarily Benn closed his eyes and clasped his hands over them, unable to look longer on the hideous light. By the time the storm had spent itself night had come, and with it a cold as intense as the heat of the day had been. Benn shivered in his watery hiding-place, longing to get out of it and continue his journey. He was no longer thirsty, during the storm he had drunk his fill of the rain, but a fierce hunger possessed him, and he could not resist the temptation to take one of his sodden tablets of chocolate. Both this food and the meat lozenges were in a thick liquid condition, despite all the care that he had taken of them. With his provisions in this state, threatening to dribble uselessly away, Mark considered it prudent to consume the rest of the tablets and the lozenges. This he did, and at the end of the meal felt greatly refreshed and much stronger than at any time during his imprisonment. His water-bottle he emptied on the veld beside him, refilling it from the ditch. Here the water,i though very muddy, was much sweeter than that whichj had ballasted the bottle well enough ,enable Cocky to make the most successful shot of his life. Bain was still falling heavily when Mari crawled from the ditch on to the veld. Hifi wounded leg was more stiff and swollen them before, but he could manage well enough to crawt on his hands and knees to the metals of the railway.. Having reached the line and risen so that he might hobble down it away from the capital, he was about to stand upright. But he was startled to hear voices and the sound of hoofs. • Looking into the darkness he could just see fa party of mounted horsemen. They were a patrol from the Boer camp, and were riding past spot where Berm had been in hiding. Even as he watched he heard a , anger, followed by a splash and a struggh was a short halt, a little commotion, im rude laughter. Then the patrol rode away before the fugitive understood that one bad been thrown from the saddle and ha, a ducking in the bath-like ditch. "Just in time!" murmured Berm. God!"</p>
        <p>the Teri</p>
        <p>cry of • Thera Lnd sumo y, but not of the receiie3</p>
        <p>gruff</p>
        <p>"Thanic 11 height, down till _ _1</p>
        <p>He set his teeth hard, rose to his fi and, with the help of his stick, hobbled line after the patrol. (To be continued.)</p>
