      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>MUNICIPAL TRADING. THE CONTROVERSY</p>
        <p>WIDENING.</p>
        <p>More Criticisms and Explanations.</p>
        <p>Mr. Norbury Williams Replies Further to Alderman Monks.</p>
        <p>We have been asked to publish Mr Norbury Williams' first reply to Alderman F W Monks,' but as it is now second-hand we cannot do so. q'Singe Saturday last Mr Williams has added a postscript to his letter which reads as follows : F.S.—For long years, New York Cty has done as Warringtm has been doing, only a trifle worse perhaps. Through the apathy and backbonelessness of its muni- cipal rulers, it has long been " bossed" by men of the Croker type. A year ago, the New York papers were writing articles on the Manchester fligginbottom case, and a.king, " Why cannot we do what Mane.hester has done ? " lam profoundly thankful to say that there has corns news this very day that Boss Croker, with all his seething corruption, has boon overthrown. I say: Let Warringtons do likewise, and may Alderman F W 'Alotiks shake himself from his lethargy, and help in the purifying and reforming procps,, (Signed)</p>
        <p>Mr. Norbury Williams Offers to make an Inquiry. The following further reply to Alderman Monks' let- ters has been sent to us for publication. Shakespeare-street, C.- on-M, Manchester Friday, 15th November, 1901. . To Alderman F. W. Monks, Warrington. Dear Sir.—l received your letter of the 7th instant. I have not however, made any haste to reply to it, as I found that whale the correspondence was incomplete, and almost before your second letter was in my hands, it was put by you into the hands of the press and ap- peared in type. Why this haste was necessary I cannot tell. I have been engaged in many controversies but never before have I found a publin man in such a des- derate baste to have his letters prir tod. In this district May say our custom Is to await the completion of the correspondence before giving it to the press, but I pre- sume yeuhave other notions of propriety in Warrington. 18.—In yours of the 7th instant you Ray you have not the necessary information to reply to my seventeen questions and that you have not any means of obtain- ing it. To this I reply that as a member of the Town Council if you have not the information you ought to have, and you certainly have ample m sans of obtaining it if you would give your mind to doing so, and devote the necessary time to it. 19--Your statement that I " evidently do not wish the public te get at the truth " if one of those insulting observations which only an Alderman, with no shred of representative authority, would venture to make. Long before you entered the Warrin.ton Council I was engag- ed In helping the public to get at the truth, and I have Probably devoted to this one object alone fifty times as Much trouble and time as yourself. u 20--As for your statement that I have suggested that ueelelle your uncle is a shareholder in J Buckley and Co Limited, you should give up membership of the rn "ells I can only speak of it as childish. I simply entioned the circumstance in order to bring out the lact, that Warrington seems to be honeycombed with Limited Companies, and that the most extraordinary •-lx`c4s-55n of Council-men, their uncles and other rela- tions and these Companies has been the result. 21.-1 have not stated that I should decline, if asked to make the er (pairs, suggested by you. tarn wishful that Warringtee ratepayers should be elle to get at the truth,and if you can guarantee that I should have access to all necessary documents, and that full protection should be given to officials and others wile might give information or evidence, and further, that members of the Council would give evidencl if called upon, I should be quite prepared to place my services at the dienosal the ratepayers of your Borough, and to endeavour to elicit the whole truth and nothing but the 'truth. I cannot see that you could give any such guarantee, and 'I therefore say thet you should support the memorial adopted at the recent meeting of ratepayers' which asked for an enquiry by the Local Government Board. Will you do this ? 22.—The only other peeßsage in your letter that cane for reply is that in which you tell me and the public ' that" on the general epeetion of imunicipal trading you think you have as high an ideal as anyone, and you - Would not hesitate to publicly denounce anything that was improper." Later on you sae you see nothing lac- Proper in the trading of your Corporation with limited companies in which members of your Corperation are shareholders. I endeavoured at the public meeting in Parr Ball to show how utterly demoralizing this practice Was, both to councillers, officials and all concerned. Norbury Williams here goes on to make statements ,serionsly reflecting on " H. Ingram Limited's " method, of trading in a certain instance, the accuracy of which state- ments we have no means of verifying, and which if in- eccurate are Quite 00.511015' libellous. Paragraphs 23, 24, 25. 27,and 23 and past ot 26.re.f erring to this matter are therefore omitted—ED. “Exeratense."] Continuing, Mr Williams seys, in referenee to "B. Ingram Limited" : I find that there are thirteen shareholders, and that to twelve of them the shares were allotted on March 14th last. Among these twelve I find the name of Councillor F R Roberts. brother of the ex-Mayor. But to my mind the thirteenth and last name is the most remarkable and noteworthy of all. His 25 shares were allotted to him so recently as October 3-d last, and his name is Edwin Bradshaw, Aeonntant. Elective Auditor for the Borough of Warrington ! ! This gentleman has been elected as the ratepayere' Watch-ing There is an old Latin say- ing " QUiS custodiet custodes ? " This MEW he roughly transleted " Woo is to watch the watch-flog Pe, halvperte- Mime this gentleman will, in the course of tirn Pass the invoices cf " to Ingram, Limited." ding is eating like a gangrene into is Warrington Town Council. It is sense of the members of the Cone ag the officials. Will you enquire Will you help those who are trying</p>
        <p>S. N. W.</p>
        <p>_ _ This improper tra the very vitals of th deadening the moral it is demoralizint 'lnto the things ?</p>
        <p>to expose them and put an:end to them ? Talk leas about a "high moral tone" and do more to bring it about. Wake up, my deaf sir, wake up ! yours f*ithfully, S. NORBURY WILLIAMS.</p>
        <p>Councillor Forshaw's Rejoinder to Councillor F. R. Roberts.</p>
        <p>To THE EDITOR OF THE " EXAMINER ": Sir,—lC would not be necessary to deal with the letter of Mr. Councillor F. R. Roberts, which appeared in your issue of the 9th instant, but that it contains so many mis-statements as to render an answer from me desir- able. first place he says that his membership of the Corporation costs him the sum of £25 a year. Hew Does this sum include his contribution of one penny to the "No more Municipal Trading Fund," or is the cog of his membership of the Council £25 and one penny annually ? Perham he will interect the ratepayers by giving full patticulars, and by detailing his expenditure of this £25 a year. With the first pert of his latter I have little or nothing to do, it is in the latter part in which be refers to me, and which I deeire to answer. He complains of the inspection of Ingram's register of shareholders. Even Mr F R Roberts is not above the law, and by section 32 of the Companies Act, 1862, any person on the payment of one shilling may inspect the register of shareholders of any limited liability company A client of mine came to see me on Wednesday,the 30th ulti:no, and asked me on his behalf to inspect the regis- ter of H Ingram, Limited. I did not know the company before that day, nor did I know that Mr F B Roberts was a shareholder,nor did I know until I had ascertained on that Wednesday that the registered office was Mr F R Roberts' office. In'the usual course of business I sent my clerk to inspect the register. He paid the shilling which Mr Roberts gratefully accepted and my client obtained the information he required. The same par- , ticalars could have been obteined from Smnerset House. The use of the information did not concern me. A , solicitor should carry out his client's proper instructions and I du not feel constrained to ask Mr F R Roberts how I should conduct my business. He refers to any being a shareholder in Fletcher, Russell and Co , Limited. I am oaly sorry I have not more sharer, but I am pleased to be a shareholder in such a firm, which has done se much to provide work in the town. The capital of Metiers Fletcher, Russell, and Co., Limited, is a quarter of a million of money and there are bundteds of I shareholders. If that company contracted with the Corporation any interestthat I might have in such con- tracts would be too small to discuss. I did not know that Messrs Fletcher,Ruesell and Co.,Limited contracted with the Corporation. Such contracts (if any), as is usual, have never, to my kuowledg,e, appeared on any council summons during the time I have been in the Cmencil. The sale of gas stoves to the Warrington Cor- poration for resale, no doubt at a profit, to eonsumers cf gas, benefi*s the ratepayers of Warringtor. Contrast this condition of things with that of H Ingram,Limited, with a capital of about £6OO and probably 15 or 16 shareholdere. The small capital and the small number of shareholders make contracts by H Ingram, Limited with the Corporation of far greater interest to share- holders such as Mr F R 10bertsen such contrects,than I should have in the case of Mews Fletcher, Russell and Co., Limited. Messra Fletcher Russell and Co., Limited, employ in their world renowned works at Warrington. over 600 workmen, most of whom are tatepayets, and I am sure that Warrington is proud of such an industry and would like to see more of such firms in the town. As I interpret the agitation of the ratepayers with reference to limited liability companies trading with Municipal Corporations it seems to be directed more to what one might call "family " companies (in some cases made into limited liability companies for the purpose of trading with municipal corporations), that is to say where a shareholder has the predominant inter- est in the company, taking an active management in the affairs of the Company and also holding a position as a member of the TowniCoutecil. The ratepayers may rightly consider that a person in this position has a de- cided advantage over competitors in any contracts, and that, therefore, it is against public policy. Again, it may be forgiven if I remind you that goods bought from Messrs Fletcher Russell and C Limited by the Corporation are resold, and a profit is derived for the benefit of the ratepayers, both from the consumption of gas and probably from the article sold, but in the case of contracts for the supply of materials to buildings a very d.fferent state of affairs existo. Mr F R Roberts refers to the resolution as to muni- cipal traling, Bze., which I proposed and which was passed by the Town Cowen, he supporting its con- firmation. If be had any objection to it why did he not state it at the proper time when it was being passed, and in the proper place, at the Council Meeting at which he was then present. My resolution dealt with members of the Council or their partners trading with the Corporation, but it also afforded seine prc- tection (which is important) to the Corporate officiale. Mr F R Roberts states that I was present when chal- lenged by Mr Norbury Williams not to support direct or indirect trading with the Council. He further states that I have given the agitators considerable help. Both these statements are incorrect.'ell was net present when Mr Norbary Williams made the challenge nor when my name was called out. I cannot help it if he is ashamed of beine a share- holder of H Ingram, Limited, otherwise I do not know why he should object to his name bring announced as Such. If he is inclined to centirme hie rush into print with- out first ascertaining fee's, I not only cannot lonlp it hut it is not my intention to follow him.—Yours faith- , W. J. FORellaW. November 15Th, 1901.</p>
        <p>Letters between Mr. F. Monks and Mr. James Dodds. A Difference of Opinion. We are requested to publish the following correspond- ence, which speaks for itself. Walton old Ilan near Warrington, 7th November 1.901. Dear Mr. Dodds,—ln a letter my son has received from Mr N. Williams, he makes a statement in your authority, and which lam sure I have never made to you. What I did say wag: " That both of us agreed with the principle of no Municipal Trading on the pert of members of the Council."</p>
        <p>4&amp;_</p>
      </div>
