      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WOMAN WITH A LOOSE SKIN.</head>
        <p>A WOMAN WITH A LOOSE SKIN.</p>
        <p>A " London Sun" reporter recently discovered a woman whose skin was so loose that when drawn be- tween the thumb and finger it would lay hanging. Chas. Forde's Bag Beans for Biliousness have since re- moved the disease which caused this state of affairs ,and in gratitude for her cure, the woman gave the follow- ing particulars for publication. See is Mrs Sarah Mary Harvey, the proprietress of a confectionery business at 345, Southwark Park-road, and speaking to the reporter in the presence of her mother and daughter she said :—" Although I now look so well, 1 was very different a couple of months ago. I had become so thin that my skin when pulled between my finger and thumb lay hangir,g loose, and 1 was Fo incapable of energy that I began to feel that death would be a relief. My friends despaired, and more than one expressed the opinion—to use their own words,—that I was not long for this world. For more I than twelve months I had suffered from j iundice. My colour was as deep a yellow as if I had been dyed ; and my limbs refused to obey my will.A skin eruption was a ceaseless torture, depriving me of sleep night and day, though the exhausting pairs I endured made it almost impossible to move. My life was, in fact, one prolonged misery. I had meantime been attended by three doctors leach in turn for months. The change from one to the other was made as each failed to do me the slightest good. I consumed their medicines, used their ointments and obeyed their instructions, but it was all to no good. They treated me for liver-disorder, biliousness and con- stipation, but notwithstanding their treatment I con- tinued a great sufferer. Eventually my appetite left me completely. When 1 did force a little food down it gave me great pain from indigestion and palpitation, and when my flesh fell away I completely despaired of ever getting wolf again. My friends took me to a hospital where I was treated for a fortnight, but felt not the slightest bit of good from it. Just at this time a neighbour read of a case like mine that Chas. Forde's Bile Beans had completely cured, and suggested that I should give them a trial. Though a little sceptical as to the result, I got a box of the beans. In a very short time I felt that they were giving me relief, and so persevered with their use. Before I had been taking them very long, I began to feel genuinely hungry. I also found that, not only could I eat heartily, but ' my food was properly digested ; so naturally my spirits I revived. At the end of a few weeks I saw that thel yellowness was disappearing from my face, and in a short time my flesh resumed its natural colour. The loose skin is there no longer. I have regained all the flesh I lost, and am growing quite stout. I can also rest well at nights. It is now only a comparatively ,hort time since I started taking the Beans yet I am quite cured, and I can g 3 about my work with enjoyment such as I haven% known for years." " And you believe you owe this entirely to Chas. Forde's Bile Beans ?" queried the reporter :—"I am quite sure of it " was the reply. " Nothing but Chas. Forde's Bile Beans cured me. My case is talked about all over the neighbourhood, and by people who come into my shop. I am so grateful for my cure that I want to publicly express my thanks ' • and shall be glad if you will publish my case. No doubt there will be others—perhaps widows with families like me—who would be glad to know of something which will cure them." Such is the gratitude of Mrs Harvey for her wonder- ful cure. The medicine whieh effected it is now recognised as the world's speciflo for jaudice and all liver-disorders, indigestion, loss of flesh, weakness, anmnia, debility, rheumatism, colds, liver-chit', sleep- lessness, headache, constipation, piles, female, ailments, pains in the side and chest, palpitation, and all blood impurities. All chemists stock Bile Beans, or they may be had poet tree from the Bile Bean Manufacturing Co., 119 and 120, London Wall, London, E.C., upon receipt of price, one and three halt-pence, or two and nine. They are sold only in sealed boxes ; never loose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S NEW MAYOR. Biographical Sketch.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S NEW MAYOR. Biographical Sketch.</p>
        <p>Mr J C Parr, is the son of the late Mr Thomas Parr, a welt-known local banker, and was birn at Grappenhall, in 1837. He married in 1872, Jessie, daughter of the late Colonel Lister-Keye, of the 10th Royal Hussars, York, by whom he has four children, Mr Roger Charlton Parr, Miss Catherine Parr, Miss Elinor Parr, and Miss Margaret Parr. Mr Parr is a justice of the peace for the county of Che - ter and a justice of the peace for Heref-rd- shire and he is also Deputy-Lieutenant of Herefordshire. Be represented the Apple 7 ton Division on the Cheshire County Coun- cil since its formation, and quite recently was elevated to an Aldermanship, his son being elected Councillor in his stead without opposition Mr Parr succeeded the late Mr Edward Greene!' as Chairman of the Daresbury Petty Sessions and the now defunct District. Highway Board. He holds the office of President of the Young Men's Christian Association, in the welfare of which he takes a deep interest. He has also taken a prominent part in the management of the Infirmary. Mr. Parr is a member of the Carlon Club and Junior Carlton and Constitutional Clubs, London. In April, 1897, ; the freedom of the Borough was conferred upon I him, and in returning thanks for the scroll which was enclosed in a silver casket, Mr. Parr said he need hardly say that no casket was required to hold the scroll; it would be enshrined in his heart, and would be handed down by those who came after him, from generation to generation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRI') AY , NOVEMBER 22 1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>GREA7 EST THING OF</head>
        <p>THE GREA7 EST THING OF</p>
        <p>ALL.</p>
        <p>[By Mr. H. Whitham.[ The heading I have adopted for the article I now pur- pose writing may seem some chat ambitious, yet judg- ing from the feelings that have pervaded my whole being during the past few months, I am convinced it is not so. Ido not envy the man who can quietly stand by, and, unmoved, watch the stirring events of current 1 history, as they unfold themselves. Nor can I under- stand how anyone can fail to conclude, as I have done, 1 that the greatest question before the civilized world to-day is undoubtedly the relationship of capital to labour. Labour is revolting against the conditions under which it is performed, and all the academical preachments that may be hurled at it from press o pulpit will be of no avail. The only true solvent will be found when we succeed in bringing together in friendly discussion, a permanent and public board composed of the various interested elements. Euring a great portion of the past twelve months the United States has been the scene of flumes us labour disturbances. Some of them have been attended with riot and bloodshed, and all have shown that a fierce hatred exists between the two parties concerned. More than this, we have seen an utter want of desire to understand the ground, or arguments each party to the conflicts took as their base for entering into the modern phase of the duels. I In San Francisco we are just emerging from the most severe fights between, labour and capital the city has ever seen. Indeed the struggle has been so intense and far reaching that city,state and nation have been forced to recognize in it a something that had in it the material out of which, throwing aside theory, must be formu- lated plans for future guidance. The pulpit has, as usual, been astride the fence. Standing in the middle of the road, it has waved a feeble reprimand to capital, not neglecting at the same time to take all the profit and pleasure that could come from riding across the continent in palace cars, eating and drinking at the expense of the greatest labour exploiter the world has ever seen, Pierpoint Morgan. The press has, in this unequal contest,been, as a rule true to its traditions. Papers owned by merchants and business men have not disdained to use every vile epithet the English language afforded them in order to blacken the characters of labour leaders, and to bolster up for a short tine longer the cause of Capital. One paper charged them that "by their clamours and false- hoods these leaders and demagogues managed to pro- long the strike for weeks after the more reasonable and intelligent workingmen were willing to settle it. They also provoked the lawless to commit those assaults that have in so many cases resulted in death," The press and the pulpit so far Jail to perceive the true conditions upon which peace may reign in the indus- trial world. When Pierpoint Morgan and Bishop Potter "suggest," the labour world is deaf, although all the old ladies among the "unco guid " may stand open mouthed in admiration. As in the press the exception was found willing to aid labor in its struggle, so in the pulpit an able defender was found, but it was in the Catholic Church. Now what are the conditions surrounding this the greatest question of all? The man who attempts to answer this question and, at the same time confesses that he is unacquainted with the literature dealing with the early days of steam and machinery will stand self condemned as to ability. More than this, it must be enforced upon the public conscience that the horrors of the labour question can only be known to the man who is willing to study and to pore over its records which date back to the time when the Israelites were commanded to make bricks without straw. Right on through the centuries we have in various forms the records of the servile wars. We read of the long ages through which the labourer was not a man, merely a thing. The lowly worker not having a hereafter to look forward to,liad no rights in the present life that need be respected. And yet in those old Pagan days the " thing " would assert its manhood, and no law or provision could be made strong enough to prevent the labour union from being formed or the labourer from meeting in secret, or the strike from following dissatisfaction at the condition of things. It is on record that a labour union existed, amons, the carpenters at the time Jesus Christ appeared, and that his preaching of the equality of man before God and the law was but the open statement of a secret ritual that had been growing with the ages. It was a direct blow to a superstition that had made a class call itself 'the people and had refused to recognise in the lowly worker a human being. So through the ages the question of labour and capital has come down to us. In various disguised forms it has always been I with us, and until the enactment of the laws of settle- ment, and the appointment of a Royal Commission in 1832, when these laws were repealed, the struggle for freedom has .been incessant, and will be until we gain a truer insight into the rights and duties of man. lOur chief troubles exist because we make no honest endeavour to understand each other. We persistently refuse to acknowledge the right of our brothers and sisters to partake of nature's bounties. In America millionaires are not only growing in number, but they are growinc, in power. They endow universities and prevent the 'Professors from giving forth to the world the results of their studies. They consort with high dignitaries of the church,and by so doing help to make wider the gulf that separates the classes from the masses. In England and America the labour leaders recognize the cause of the under-swell that keeps us in a state of unrest. Education is doing its work, but doing it slowly and very roughly. Men clamour for shorter hours with increased pay. They know that one of the rights of man is that he sh,all not only have a sufficiency of the good things of life for himself, but that those near and dear to him, his wife and his children, shall have enough and to spare, also. Cracked-brained fools look on at the struggle and fancy they see the panacea to all our troubles in the bomb, the pistol, or the knife. When a strike happens special policemen are enlisted into the service of capital, and, sworn to defend a false liberty, make it possible for scabs to hinder the honest worker in gaining his own. Employers now organize, and, in explanation, say they must de. fend themselves. They strap the pistol pouch to the specials side, put in his hand a club, and around his middle a well filled cartridge belt, and then turn him out to strut around the city as the proud and patriotic defender of the people's liberties. In England a decree of the House of Lords makes it possible to claim and obtain damages from a trades union, thus making it impossible to raise a fund for strike purposes. In America the courts issue injunctions to prevent men from speaking to each other, or from taking action on questions affecting their relationship with their em- ployers. Employers demand that a workman shall give up either his union or his job. They go further than this,and demand that he shall give up his rights of citizenship and stay away from the polling booth. In both countries an effort is being made to down and squeeze out the labour organizations. Opponents to labour organizations have failed to ina,d the signs of the times aright. Trusts are seeking by every means to put a stop to competition that is ruinous to profits. Labour Unions are trying to put a stop to competition among the workmen. Were it not for the drag of labour unions on the wheels of irresponsible competition, we should have almost universal revolution, and rivers of blood in our streets within six months time, As it is, what do we see ? America never before had the pros- perous times she is having now. Her productions go into all nooks of the world, and almost the last words of the murdered President were a demand for increased activity' in seeking out for fresh markets. During the past six years of business activity strikes, and rioting have been weekly occurrences. A man elected by a nation's vote to the position of chief magistrate, has met his death by the hand of a cowardly fool. A world has gone wild at this, and, as I have said above, blind to the cause of the under-swell proposes all kinds of foolish and outrageous methods for pulling down what it is pleased to call anarchism. It maybe that socialism.proposes the impossible, and the socialist is a mere theoretical dreamer, none the less the fact remains that our cities slums are teeming with poverty and misery. We can read of young women, 17 and 18 years of age, working in the brick fields of Staffordshire' and we can read of children of tender age working the long nieht through in the cotton factories of the Southern States of America. If the methods of the Socialist are impossible there is surely room for the educated man, the man of study, the man of sweetness and light to step forward and • help to bring the opposing forces together, and with Ithe help of friendly talk and sympathetic work bring order out of chaos. aln Europe the workman is restless and starving. In</p>
      </div>
