        <p>FOR LANCASHIRE</p>
        <p>PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>and melancholy, and she also suffered from heavy pains in the head, while her heart seemed affected. A very skilful doctor said she had an anemic heart, and what made her worse people who came to the shop used to remark how pale she looked. During the busy times she assists me in our dining-room business at 59, Bankhall-street. Although she was able to do some of the housework she had to take things very quietly for if she hurried or went upstairs she gasped for breath, and her heart throbbed awfully. For about four months she was treated at the Stanley Hos- pital as an out-patient, and they did her good there, particularly for the pains in the head, but she could not get rid of her com- plaint, so we decided it would be best for her to try her father's remedy. Mr. Wood suffered a great deal with rheumatism and sciatica, but he was cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and when we found that the pills were also recommended for anemia we felt that at last there was a chance of my daughter being restored to health." •4 And your expectations have been realised ? " "Yes, I am thankful to say." "Did she soon feel benefited after taking the pills 2" "Yes, she was much better after the first two boxes, and she was all right when she had taken six."</p>
        <p>Miss Wood, a robust-looking young lady of 21, added—" Before I began the pills I used to be nervous and mopish, and did not care to go into company. but now I can always enjoy myself. I have no palpitation, and have not needed to take any of the pills for two or three months."</p>
        <p>medicines can this be said:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Absolute and Unquestionable</head>
        <p>Absolute and Unquestionable</p>
        <p>THOUSANDS OF GREAT CURES.—Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are praised by all classes of people for the way in which they have cured paralysis, locomotor ataxy. rheumatism, and sciatica; also all diseases arising from impoverishment of the blood, scrofula, rickets, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the bowels and lungs, anaemia, pale and sallow complexion, general muscular weakness, loss of appetite,palpitations, pains in the back, nervous headache and neuralgia, early decay, all forms of female weakness and hysteria, These pills are a tonic not a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and are sold by Chemists, and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, Holborn Viaduct, London, at 2s. 9d. a box; six boxes for 18s. 9d.</p>
        <p>England trade depression is once more gaining ground In America a high tariff seems to keep wages high, but it does not keep out poverty. What it really does, is to enable a surplus production to be dumped on a foreign soil.</p>
        <p>H. WHITHAM.</p>
        <p>San Francisco.</p>
        <p>St. Mary's Church, Latchford.</p>
        <p>Tie annual sermons in aid of the schools connected with S. Mary's, Latchford, were preached on Sunday, by the Rev Fattier Mark, C.F., Rector of Sutton</p>
        <p>_ Monastery. The congregations were not so large as might have been expected. The musical portion of tbe Services was a special feature, having been arranged by Mr Peter Bate, organist and choir master to whom much praise is du.. There was an augmented choir and orchestra and in the morning they rendered "Merse des Anger," composed by S. Moorat, with thwriginal accompaniments for strings, by permission of the composer's widow. Additional accompaniments for wind instruments were arranged by Mr Peter Bate. The offertory piece Raff's " Cavatina " was admirably played by Mr R H Pearson, and the concluding volun- tary was " Allegro " from M•zart'a 32nd Synsphonv. At the evening service Mr R B Pearson played the violin solo " Romance " B Tours and afterwards the Benedic- tion service number 6, composed by P von Tugginer and orchestrated by Mr P Bate was rendered. A hymn for chorus and orchestra " Hail Queen of Heaven "com- posed by Mr P Bate was very effectively given. At both services Mr Peter Bate acted as conductor, Mr R H Pearson as leader of the orchestra, and Mr J Broad- bent, presided at the organ. The soloists were Mrs Peter Bare, the Misses Hazlehursc, Fell and Rots and Messrs J R,lobitt and J W Bibby. The Rev Father Mark preached two eloquent sermons, the morning discourse being based on the words of St Paul "Brethren we have ceased not to pray to God for you that you may walk worthily in God, in all things blessing Him, being fruit- ful in every good work and increasing in the knowledge of God." During the course of his sermon he said that judging from the indifferentism of the present day, he could not help thinking that, with those outside the Catholic Church faith was of very little importance and that with them philanthropy and natural goodness were the only keys required to open the Kingdom of Heaven. The spirit that was actuating the present age did not care a jot about what a man's religion might be. Toe world looked more to what a man did. The world regarded creeds as mere apples of disscord, dogmas as mere bones of contention. So long as a man was truthful and honest, or if he did not possess these qualities, so long as he was not found out, he was a hero with his fellow-countrymen, and his sal- vation was regarded as secure. The Catholic Church very firm'y maintained that faith and good works were necessary for salvation. At the conclusion of his dis- course the preacher appealed for their support on behalf of the schools. He said he knew perfectly well that many calls bad been made upon the people of that parish and thelr generosity had perhaps been of late over-taxed. They had come forward nobly and given handscmely In aid of the erection of the beautiful new church, but he asked them to make one more effort, this time in aid of the schools, tie was appeal- to them on behalf of little children and assuredly what ever they did for these little noes God would regard as being done for Himself.</p>
        <p>Christening of Sir Gilbert Greenall's Heir. At St John's Church, Walton, on Sunday after- noon the ceremony of baptizing the heir to the Walton estates, the son of Sir Gilbert. and Lady Greenall, took place. The event aroused consider- able interest in the village and there was a large congregation. Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall as well as a number of relatives were pre- sent. The font had been very artistically adorned with beautiful flowers, and the west end of the church had been decorated with palms. The officiating clergy were the Revs L J T Darwall (vicar of the parish), Clifton-Smith (vicar of Dares- bury),and H V Pigot (vicar of St Thomas', Stockton Beath). The choir assembled round the font and sang the responses and several hymns. "Gilbert " was the name bestowed upon the child,which is of course,t he name of his father aid late grandfather. The service concluded with the pronouncing of the Benediction by the Vicar of Walton. Afterwards the children in the village who were anxious to tee the baby wee given an opportunity of doing so. There is Security is I A rev drop. on the. toothbrush every morning</p>
      </div>
