      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>.14 OP</head>
        <p>.14 OP</p>
        <p>D-FWILILL</p>
        <p>twitted with the inconsistencies of professing Christian people. Be had had a good deal to do with the temperance cause,but be found it was almost hopeless,to try and save a man or woman, especially a woman who was addicted to drink. He did not know a single case where a woman had been absolutely reclaimed. Peoplesometimestold them that they made random statements, but it was impossible to exaggerate the evils of strong drink. Referring to the erection of the lunatic asylum of Winwick, which will provide accommodation for 1,200 lunatics, he said there were more lunatics in Lancashire ready to go into the institution than it would hold. And yet temperance advocates were called extreme people when they denounced the strong drink which was largely responsible for lunacy. Be advised all young people to have nothing t do with drink, a3d said he bad been amazod and ashamed , to see large numbers of respectable young men and young women trooping into public houses. In referring to temperance reform, Councillor Roberts said they would have to be moderate in their demands and content with a little at a time. Since he entered the Town Council he had load many opportunities of conversing with men connected with the "trade" and he believed tbs.; publicans were anxious hr shorter hours. It was the wealthy brewers who stood in the way of legislation for a reduction of the hours of the sale of drink, especially on Sunday. He believed the publicans would be satisfied if their , premises were opened for only an hour at noon and an r hour at night. It they as Temperance workers could get that it would be a great step forward and be believed if it was pressed forward there was a reasonable pros- pect of it being carried. (Hear, hear.) In conclusion. he encouraged the members of the society to continue I in their good work. .</p>
        <p>During the evening, songs were efficiently sung by Miss Kershaw and Miss Mason, and recitations were given by Miss Furness. Mr C T Owen accompanied on the piano. A vote of thanks concluded the proceed- ingS.</p>
