      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY MEN'S FIND.—On Sunday afternoon, some men in charge of the Padgate ballast train of the Cheshire Lines Railway, were working in the vicinity of the Central Station at Manchester, when, in remov- ing a heap of debris, one of their nurnbr found a card- board box, which, on examination, was found to con- tain four keyless watches. They were handed over ta Inspector Bate, of the station Staff, and an effort will be made to find their owner.</p>
        <p>DEDICATION OF A. LITANY DESK.—At Christ Church, Padgate, on Sunday morning, tie Rector of Warring- ton, dedicated a handsome oak Litany desk, which has recently been presented..to the church by a member of the congregation, who, for the present, wishes to re- main anonymous. The dedicatory prayers were said by the Rector, at the Litany desk, direct's/ after the third collect, the rest of the service being as usual, The Rec- tor gave a brief summary of the history of the Litany, from the eartiest times dawn to 1661. He pointed out the peculiar beauties of the service itself, and how much those lost who never sincerely joined in it. He also stated the reason fir a special place from which to say the Litany. The Vicar, preaching in the evening, also treated the subj,ct of the Litany, its history, and place in Christian devotional exercises. The desk, which is of massive oak and handsomely carved, was supplied by Messrs Jones and Willis, of Liverpool.—A beautifu'ly bound Litany book has alsn been presented by a lady parishoner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Harriers.</head>
        <p>Harriers.</p>
        <p>Saturday's fixture of the Sankey Club was an inter. club run with St Helens Sacred Heart Harriers, at St Helens, and a large number of members turned up at headquarters, the Sloop Hotel, prepared to make the journey. Owing to the dense fog which prevailed through out the afternoon it was decided to declare the run off„ and a telegram was sent to St Helens to that effect. Not to be denied, a fair muster turned out and indulged in a short spin in the direction of Bold. To-day Seftoa will pay a visit, and a good turnout is expected.</p>
