      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>TEMPERANCE MEETING AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE MEETING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Addresses by Councillor William Roberts and Miss Whitfield.</p>
        <p>A temperance meeting and entertainment under the auspices of the Wycliffe Temperance Society took thee in Wycliffe Hall on Wednesday evening. The,Rev John Yonge presided over a small attendance. Amongst those present were Councillor William Roberts, Mr R I Wyllie, Mrs Henry Roberts, Miss Whitfield (Superin- tendent of the District Nursing Association), and Mr F Morrell (secretary of the Temperance Society). Miss WHITFIELD read an interesting paper describing ; her experiences of the ravages of strong drink on the ; lives and homes of the people. Her twelve years ; e perience as a nurse had led her to the opinion that I surely it was the love of strong drink that was the root . of all evil. Miss Whitfield proceeded to describe some of the distressing scenes she had witnessed in a London hospital which were entirely due to drink. Were it not for the large number of cases attri- butable to drink a much smaller staff of nurses and doctors would have been sufficient. Her experience as a district nurse had intensified her hatred I of drink and increased her resolve to condemn it at every opportunity. Only those who lived and moved among the poor pe,pple could form a true conception of the ravages of strong drink. The daily life of the dis- trict nurse was made sad by the tales they heard from the lips of the sufferers and the sights they witnessed, If the people realised individually and collectively the deadly nature of the poison which surely if slowly, des- troyed its victirns,they would endeavour more earnestly te remove the temptation. Miss Whitfield pioceeded to describe an Imaginary round of a district nurse and gave some typical instances which bad come under her own observation, showing the evil effects of drink on the lives and homes of the patients. She urged the members of the temperance society to undertake their work with earnestness. They had a golden opportunity of weaning the children from the drunken:habits of their parents. Dn not let them lose sight of the main thing; teach them earnestly and faithtully to shun the drink as they would shun the deadly serpent and teach them that their bodies were temples of the living God. In conclusion she asked them to be more consistent and more whole hearted in the temperate cause. The CHAIRMAN said they were indebted to Miss Whit- field for the exceedingly interesting but painful paper which she had just read. It was a paper lull of sugges- tion and gave them an insight into the scenes which were being enacted all around them every day. What he had said was perfectly true. Many people had very ittle idea of the terrible evils of the drinking habits of a large portion of the community. They sometimes saw a drunken scene in the street and were disgusted,whilst in somelpersons a drunken scene only excited amuse- ment and laughter. To anyone looking npon a scene of that kind with true reflection and with the smallest insight into what it meant, they could not be amused by it. They ought to check anyone who looked uroa it as a joke. It ought to be taken seriously for it was not only a public scandal that scenes of that kind were enacted but it was external evidence of a great deal of distress and misery which went on where they did not see it and inflicted agony and anguish in many lives. He hoped what they had heard would encourage them to discourage the drinking habit in themselves, in their families and homes and by their example and precept wherever their influence might be. Councillor WILLIAM ROBERTS said he would like to take the first opportunity of congratulating Mr Yonge upon having completed 25 years of service at Wycliffe Church. It was an event which had given pleasure and satisfaction to the whole of the church and congrega- tion. lie could say with truthfulness and sincerity that during the long years of service which Mr Yonge had honoured them with,at no time had he stood higher in their estimation than he did to-day. He trusted God would give Mr Yonge health and strength to remain w4th them for many years to come and that the happi- ness of all the people ccnnected with the church would be to him a source of satisfaction and reward. (Hear, bear.) Proceeding to refer to total abstinence, Coun- cillor Roberts said the longer be lived, the more con- firmed lie was in the faith of total aostinenee. He wished to impress upon all connected with their churches and Sunday schools that those in the outside world were mere critical of them than ever. Personal conduct was the standard by which they were judged by those outside the churches,and men of the world were only too ready to point the finger of scorn if any of their number happened to stumble or fall. He would advise all Christian people to have nothing whatever to do with intoxicating drink. If there was anything which had given him pain it had been to be</p>
      </div>
