        <p>NOVEMBER 22ND 1901.</p>
        <p>If the Hon. ALffi.z/7 The Government's LTXTE Lrox is 0 be b6Education Policy. lieved, the policy education which the Government will follow the coming session of Parliament will not be fined to suppressing the School Boards, but WI continue the line of policy already Introduced 01 diverting to the so-called Voluntary schools ths funds which have been voted by Parliament for national purposes. Speaking at Leamington, the hon gentleman referred to the important educa- tion measures which would be passed next sesslo°' There was a growing feeling, he said, that while the Voluntary schools must be continued, it we's impossible to expect the supporters of Voluntail schools to continue to subscribe as largely as f' present. The audacity of this statement is sr. tounding. It has already been pointed out these columns that when the grant was increass" to Voluntary schools it was on the distinct understanding that it was for the purpose el raising the low standard of education Church schools, and not to relieve subscribers of the responsibilities they undertaken. It was, therefore, stipulated tbs" the subscriptions be maintained at the level st which they then were. But the subscriptions Icee? falling off, and instead of accepting the logical CO elusion which this involves, that those interest°, in these schools were ceasing to regard their exist ence as necessary, or at least worth the expense 'of maintaining, the Church party demand that tl3,° schools should be maintained, and that the funds, either from grants or rates, shall be used t- maintain them. This seems to be the basis uP°: which the Government are to fram3 their policy, so far as primary education is conceroo",' A strong claim, Mr. LYTTELTON informs Usti! about to be put forward, that, provided the b ings were maintained by the subscribers, till: bducational expenditure should be be horn entirely by the nation, either by rate or otherwise' That certainly is a strong claim, however it be put forward; and if the Government acceo they will lay down an entirely new princiPl: in education, that any body of people Oh.' choose to erect a school building and unOn take its maintenance will be in a position% claim national funds to carry on educatl°,l/) practically on any system they choose, and with any kind of teaching which they may Cl to call religious. Surely, if it has come to tioe' that "the expenditure should be borne entirol9 the nation," it is only rational to suppose that t" system of education should be national, with alie/a system of religious teaching that may be insist on grafted on by those who desire it and are Pre; pared to pay for it. But that is quite anotbed I matter. Our Church friends put th 3 cart bef°C, I the horse; and, while desirous of relieving 0431, selves from financial r3sponsibility, it is quite 01 a ent that they wish to retain the control sijka management of the schools :in the hands of te'll clergy as at present. Mr LYTTELTON is prePe! g to admit that a consequence of applra rates to Voluntary schools will be to Per on the boards of management represen.ttil tives of the ratepayers to act along WI representatives of the parents—whatever th 4; may mean. He admits that this is thOly point upon which his friends are not caltir,lo agreed. Probably we shall next hear that'rtv dissentients are to be pacified by an arrangsnlerr. which shall render the aforesaid ratepayers' ref, a sentatives quite harmless. By " co-ePting,toci sufficient number of members—a favourite Ea" with the Goverment at present—the clerical p.avrova-' on a board of management may easily be ,gt such a majority as to leave the representativ..o a the ratepayers and the ratepayers' mic/neY to nominal and ineffective force. The facts seenlra of syste be that an extensicon of the National rate-aided education—much as it abused</p>
