        <p>becomes</p>
        <p>being admitted as the only effective basis for primary education; and that instead of frankly accepting the consequences and retiring from the field, the friends of the Church are determined to continue the control they enjoy, but which they are no longer prepared to pay for. Apparently the Government are going to try the re-casting of elementary education on this plan; but if the country is alive to its best interests they will find it a matter of some difficulty.</p>
        <p>THE report on Trades British irades Unions in 1900, made by Unions. the Chief Labour Cur-</p>
        <p>respondent to the Board of Trade, shews how much the unions have benefited by the industrial prosperity which the country has enjoyed up till this year. Its record is one of almost uninter- rupted prosperity during the years from 1896 to 1900, in which period the membership increased by 314 per cent., or nearly a third of the whole. Previous to 1896 decreases were registered of 1.5 per cent. in 1893, 2.9 in 1891, and 20 in 1895. The year 1896 seems to have been a year of active pro- paganda, for the decrease of the previous year was changed into an increase of 6 per cent., which grow to 8 per cent. in 1897. The increase fell off in 1898 to 2 per cent., but took a leap the follow-</p>
        <p>ing year to 9 per cent.; and in 1900 it was stil 5.8, or rather more than -54 per cent., and more than the annual average of growth since 1892. 'The increase last year, however, was mainly owing to the growth of the coal-mining unions which sprang up as the result of the pheno- menal prosperity of that industry. With a mem- bership now reaching close upon two millons, it</p>
        <p>_ may be easily understood that there is an enor-</p>
        <p>.. _ mous turnover of union funds; and it is to be regretted that in all cases the income, expendi- ture, and balance are not given. This indicates that what has always been a weak point and a source of trouble in trades unionism still exists—</p>
        <p>namely, that there is a difficulty in getting the financial management of the smaller unions placed upon a proper business footing. Most of the union members have availed themselves of the security which is afforded by registration under the statute ; but many of the smaller unions still neglect this precaution, which must be a cause of itequent loss and other abuses. Of 1,272 separate organisations, as a matter of fact less than a-half are reported as being registered ; but those regis- tered embrace the larger unions, and account for 79 per cent., or over three-fourths of the total membership. Taking a hundred of the largest unions, with a membership of 1,158,909, an income is returned of £1,957,000, being £lOO,OOO more than the previous year. But the</p>
        <p>expenditure showed a larger increase, having risen from £1,203,60S to £1,490,582. The accu- mulated funds, howaver,shovv a gratifying increase Of aboat half a million the figures being .-C3,282, 59t3 in 1899 and £3,766,625 in 1900. Perhaps the most satisfactory feature of the whole report is the large amount of friendly society work now being done by the trades unions. Thus the hundred</p>
        <p>largest -unions last year paid nearly £1,000,000 to their sick, out-of-work, and superannuated mem- bers. Only 10 per cent, went in dispute pay. The working expenses are perhaps high at 25 per cent. but considering that the contributions have to be collected in frequent payments of small sums it</p>
        <p>need not be regarded as excessive. The figures work out at 5s in the £ for working expenses, 23 tor strike pay, and 13s for assistance rendered to the -unfortunate in the shape of sick and accidents aliment, superannuation and funeral allowances, other grants and benefits. It is thus seen -t a t the full liberty of organisation given to labour ilas resulted in the building up of a beneficent social structure, where much-needed benefits are bestowed upon the workers as the result ef their own thrift; and the trades unions have justified their existence quite apart from the doubtful nefits of agitation and trade disputes.</p>
        <p>. An object lesson as to the importan 23 of bring- lhg the British working-man up to date has just been furnished in the East End of London. A rril of bottle manufacturers has deemed it neces- Bat" to discharge a number of employs and dispossess them of their dwellings in order to 0. btain the services of Germans. Whole streets, it ls. said, are being bought up for the accommoda- tion of the invaders who are landing daily in Parties of thirty to seventy with their wives and children,while the Britishers are being sent adrift. The defence of the masters is straightforward enough. They say that the bottle-making tradeis fast going to Germany becanse of the superior Workmanship there, and it would have been useless -0 prolong the struggle under the old conditions. If that statement is accurate, their decision is for the benefit of the country. The German workmen will have to spend their money round about their 411," homes, and, like most German immigrants of ,- artisan type, they will probably become good bieciztriens. Meanwhile our own working classes will the ade to realise by this concrete example that outYjaust wake up. The Britisher can still turn when heb.est goods in every department of trade mad is inclined to. But past successes have e qui the e .ic lw.conservative, and he is being beaten by ,rermanker. American and the more systematic state of things will have to be altered. It is satisfactory to learn that the masters are Paying just as much wages, s.o clearly the step has not been taken to beat down prices.</p>
        <p>---- We are reminded of the great place which Lord Morris and Killanin really deserves among forensic humorists by a collection of some of his sayings in the quarterly Law Magazine. The wit of the late Chief Justice of Ireland was not always of the literary kind, it is true; but it was none the less an effective weapon when Lord Morris was displeased. There is something Johnson ian in his remark, on being told that "the people" were in BYmpathy with certain offenders: "I never yet knew a small town in Ireland that had not a blackguard in it who called himself the ; people ! " Again, he almost rivalled the great / ' "master of dry wit" when he told a counsel ' Who "assumed his lordship was acquainted - with the statutes and authorities on Public Health": "Assume nothing of the sort; I • yield to no man in my utter ignorance of the ' sanitary law." Even Irish politics Lord , Morris illuminated with some stray glints of . humour. It was he who said, for instance, that the real injury and injustice was done to Ireland in the glacial period, when the great boul- ders scraped off her valuable minerals into the e, sea, "just as you'd scrape the butter off a slice of bread with a knife." He wondered "why Irish- men who were partners in a great concern like , Guinness's brewery,with one hand in the till,should , think of breaking away and setting up a little . ehebeen of their own." "Ireland,"was another of his Uyings, "is not like any other island that ever 'was made; instead of a hill it has, like a saucer, , a great hollow in the centre of it where all the Water lodges." We have only space for one more anecdote. Once when a prisoner shouted in the , Qek, " God save Ireland !" the Chief Justice . "replied : "With all my heart ; but it is the men 110 are shouting 'God save Ireland' who make Iwo it init,„ 13rovidence to perform that opera-</p>
        <p>dly likely to boom in Warring - comes cheaper. Shopkeepers ',pensive luxury, but they fully</p>
        <p>realise that luxuries have to be paid for, and they go on paying more or less philosophically. Still, a charge of 19s. 6d. per hour weuld stagger even them—and it has already staggered the committee of the Warrington Musical Society, who were in- formed last week that they would have to pay the above sum for the electric light installation over and above the fee for renting Parr Hall. Naturally they struck, and finally matters were compromised by the Society using as little of the electric light as possible for the first subscription concert the illumination being completed with the help of the gas which is included in the charge for the Hall. The new lighting arrangements in Parr Hall are really splendid, but we rather imagine most people will dispense with the splendour if it is to cost an extra 19s 611 per hour. Such a charge seems both exorbitant and impolitic, especially as the avowed object of the Corporation is to popu- larise the new illuminant as much as possible. The fact will have to be brought home to those responsible that popularity and cheapness rut. I together in such matters as these. Electric light retailed at nearly a sovereign an hour will not do at all, as long as the alternative of gas or oil re maiL s.</p>
        <p>6FC. Stront 3URN, • At, DINE. 00D, tediein6 lo clots' ~ead Ly 1320:::1 becon,'"' er, POb' IbliShe' Feld° , R. ALFOP lii - ors con' of the watan at as' in tcreased 'lot of ' in had that keel" 0013; ‘tereste "r :pen° i:tbe , to uPelj 3ir ne,f wort's"' us/ i° 'bore° terWise" thi* • bi be ci</p>
