      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>krid thai</head>
        <p>krid thai</p>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Arrangements, says the Exchange Telegraph Company are being made at Westminster with a View to the Opening of Parliament on January 16th. Education is to.be given the premier place in the Government programme, it is believed, and if what the Hon A Lyttelton announced at Leam- ington as to one of the proposals of ministers should prove to be correct, the public may expect as lively times in the provinces as some of us remember to have marked the introduction of Mr Forster's Bill,some thirty years ago. If Mr Lyttel- ton is rightly informed, the Government have made an important capitulation to those who may be classed as educational Progressives throughout the country, and will propose next session that, ' provided the buildings are maintained by the sub- scribers, the educational expenditure shall be borne entirely by the nation, either by rate or otherwise, a concession being made to popular feeling by re- presentatives of parents and of ratepayers being admitted on the Board of Management. Such is an inkling as to the propoml which Mr Lyttelton very properly described as most important. But the Government will find that it is also very con- tentious, and with this great question on hand, together with arrears of debate on the war in South Africa, and other matters, it may well be believed that ministers propose to settle down in earnest, and to commence work early. The first Parliament of King Edward VII was op,med on February last by his Majesty in person, and his second session is expected to be commenced in the same way.</p>
        <p>Climatic Asperities and foretastes of the rigours of winter, as if insufficient in themselves to try the patience of the very poor, and make human kind generally keep within doors and feel somewhat uncomfortable, have been emphasised during the week by decisions on coal exchanges to advance the price of household coal a shilling or more per ton, the rise having already taken effect. There may be those, however sincere their sympathy with men compelled to earn a living by their labour underground, who will read without a pang the statement that at Aberdare this week fifteen colliers were ordered to pay a penalty of 14s fid each for absenting themselves from work on three "atop days." The object of these men being purely and simply that of causing the price of coal to be increased in order to enrich themselves, as they believe, while " taking their ease at their inn" or elsewhere, is not one that is calculated to improve the disposition of the public towards the miners, who, however, are very much under the thumb of their more interested "leaders,</p>
        <p>Health Statistics published a fortnight since under the head-line, "The Towns that Kill," were intended to show that the three great town i of the Noe,h-east of England held the reputation of being the most unhealthy places in the country. Thus Sunderland was crelited with a death-rate of 25.4 per thoussui, Newcastle of 255, and Gateshead of 27.7. In this dark list, then, it is seen that Gates- head was the worst. But, according to a state- ment which has this week appeared in the "Times," ' as bearing upon the mortality of the South African Concentration Camps there are worse places still, both in London and the provinces. Mr Paul Swain, F.ll, C.S.,points out that last August in Westminster the deaths of children under one year old numbered 213 per thousand births, figures sufficiently terrible to attract attention. But in Battersea the mor- tality reached toe almost incredible number of 384 per thousand, while in Fulham it was 373. In the quarterly return of the Registrar-Ueneral ending September 28th the same fearful rate of infant mortality is shown in many of the larger towns of the provinces generally. For instance, Birkenhead, and Blackburn stand at 362 per thousand, and Sal- ford at 331. Gateshead here figures at 343 per thousand.</p>
        <p>-- The death-rate in the Concentration Camps, as every newspaper reader knows, was terribly high, but information contained in the Blue book which has just appeared, a bulky tome of 387 pages, seemed to call for something nearer home with which we might be supposed to be more familiartso as to be able to draw a camparison, and in the figures relating to our own country, as given above, there are the means for doing so. Deaths in the refugee camps no doubt included people of all ages, but the mortality was almost entirely confined to young children. It was these who died wholesale of measles, and it is astonishing, not so much that they did die in vast numbers, but that the number was not even greater ; so dense in their stupid- ity, and so filthy in their habits, are the average Boer women shown to be. It is not necessary to quote the words of the English doctors as to the remedies the Boer women insisted upon trying ; they are simply filthy,and fully account for enteric fever abounding to the extent it did. The rooted objection of the Boers to soap and water and fresh air is fairly well-known by this time, and the greatest brutal oppressor in the eyes of both Boer men and women has been the doctor or the officer who has insisted upon the observance of cleanliness. People will think as they please as to the origin of the war and the policy to be pursued in the future. With these things one has nothing to do in this letter, but in the matter of the concentration camp, gross and sensational charges have been made which are proved by the Blue Book and external evidence to be absolutely untrue; and for the sake of the country's good name it is right that the truth should be told and that the causes of the terrible mortality against which our doctors struggled heroically should be known as set forth in this well- I timed official publication.</p>
        <p>While the Colliers in South Wales continue to take the law into their own hands, or their repre- sentatives on the Sliding Scale Committee do it for them, as if they knew no other law, the French Miners are said to be still considering the question of a general strike; but the various districts in France are not in harmony on the subject. So far as the English public are concerned they would, perhaps, prefer French methods to those now adopted in South Wale. There was no " play" day in the Welsh coalfield last week, but the fiat has gone forth from the headquarters at Cardiff that there shall be another" stop day "on Wednes- day next. A suggestion for a whole week's holiday at Christmas has not yet been entertained by the Committee. Meanwhile, the outlook is, on the whole,no more encouraging than it was a fortnight I ago. The sole object of the miners is admitted to raise the price of coal, no other cause for their pre- sent action beitg even mentioned.</p>
        <p>Except in the Welsh coal area, the Industrial Outlook in all parts of the provinces has the ap- pearance of an unwonted calm. As already sug- gested, it would be unwise to attempt to minimise the importance of the exception. Since there are obvious possibilities opened out by the action of the miners' representatives which no thoughtful individual can. afford to disregard. It may, however, be said with truth ' that at the present moment this country has less to fear from ferment among the toilers than any other nation in the world would have in similar circum- stances. As a matter of fact,we have already under- gone many of the experiences that have yet to be suffered by certain ot our trade rivals. Reference has been made to France, and it might be added that at the present moment there are no fewer than fifty thousand men out of employment in Germany. In their past experience, probably, may be found the secret of the comparative restfulness of British</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S WESLEY GUILD.</p>
        <p>The monthly social and musical gathering was held in the guild parlour, Brunswick School, last Thursday week. There was a good attendance of members and friends, Mr E Taylor presided. The following programme had been arranged by Miss Norman :—Violin solo, Miss N Harrison; song. Mr Hinds; song, Miss 14 Harrison ; song, Miss N Temperley ; violin solo, Miss N Harrison; piano- forte solo, Mrs Morrison (encored) ; song, Miss E Harrison ; song, " Mary," Mr T Hinds (encored) ; recitation, Miss Agnes Davies; song, Miss N 'tem- perley. MiSS Wells was the accompanist. The vari- ous items were well received. During an interval light refreshments were served, a most, enjoyable evening being spent. A LANDLADY PLEDGES HER LODGER'S CLITHES.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court on Wednesday be- fore Messrs F J Norman (in the chair), and W Tim- mins, Agnes Smith, 55, Suffolk-street, was charged with unlawfully having pawned with Henry Dodd. pawnbroker, Egerton-street certain goods the pro- perty of Peter Crank without authority on the 18th inst.—The evidence showed that Crank went up- stairs to get his things when the prisoner informed him that she had pawned them. She proffered to get them out for the following Monday and he waited until that time. As she did not keep to her promise he issued a warrant against her. It was stated that the woman had X 2 in her possession and as the prosecutor did not wish to press the charge the magistrates allowed the charge to be withdrawn. The woman promised to pay all expenses incurred and to take the clothes out of pledge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>WESTON CHURCH -SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>WESTON CHURCH -SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A married men's tea party and concert was held in the above school on Monday evening the pro- ceeds beidg devoted to defray the expenses of the recent mission. Mr A Guest occupied the chair an exceedingly good company being present. Tne duet (piano), entitled" Symphony,' Miss K Tovvena Thomas AVC M, gold medalist, and Professor Ainsley F S CM, was listened to with rapt atten- tion. Song, " Mona,' Mr E Worrall ; song, " Dolly and a Cock," Miss Nellie Temperley was also encored to which she responded with "Bevoni the Shadow-land "; solo (violin), " Concerto," Miss L IsmPerloY song," The skipper," Mr W shaw who gave a very pleasing rendering and which was highly appreciated an encore following with the song, "For King and country "; song, "The lost chord," Miss Thomas: comic song, "It hadn't the desired effect," Mr Ellison, Mr Ellison proving so great a success in hie inimitable style convulsed the audience with ladghter. He gave "Three Girls in a Boat as an encore ; duet (vocal), " Excelsior," Messrs Worrall and Shaw : song, "Queen of the Angels." Miss Nellie Temperley ; solo (mandoline), " Maritana." Miss L Temperley; song. "The Holy City,' Mr E Worrall ; song, " Eileen Allannah," quartette, Miss Thomas, Miss Nellie Temperley, Mr W Shaw. Mr E Worrall; comic song, "I couldn't say I hadn't for I had "Mr Ellison.--The RevW H Stables said he felt it _</p>
        <p>incumbent upon him to explain his enforced absence during the early part of the meeting. It was unnecessary to point out that a very serious call demanded his services elsewhere. They all knew the work of the Mission was God's work. Each and every one threw themselves wholly into the work because it was God's work. In exactly the same proportion, exactly in the same way, exactly in the same degree, he (Mr Stables) believed the work now engaged upon was God's work. Hence the Guilds, evening preaching teachers classes had to be temporarily relinquished for nearly a month and remove to another sphere of labour, viz that ot electioneering in the coming School' Board election in Runcorn. He was quite sure they would not begrudge him the work, especially so seeing there was so much at stake. The Church party were standing up for the little children and in doing so they were standing up for religion. The Church party were fighting for their schools. The party which called itself kprogressive hoped to capture all Voluntary schools in Runcorn. They could make it very difficult indeed for them to exist and possibly had they their way. volun- tary schools would become extinct—and Universal Board Schools lubstituted. They—the Church— believed religious instruction should be given day after day not in a sort of diluted form but in the very best form in the work to which Gad had called them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>SONS OF IEMPERANCE. —_— Opening of a New Division at Halton. '</head>
        <p>SONS OF IEMPERANCE. —_— Opening of a New Division at Halton. '</p>
        <p>-- On Saturday evening the officers of the Lifeboat Crew Division of the Sons of Temperance met in the school- room of Trinity Wesleyan Church, Halton, for the pur- pose of opening a new division of the order. There was a large attendance. Mr Thomas Heesom presided, and he was supported by other officers, including—Messrs D Bisbrown, Thomas R Whyte, Thomas J Holt, James Johnson, Thomas Clarke, Alfred nden, G Powell, John Fowldes, and Samuel Tomlinson. After the usual formalities 16 new members were Initiated. Another member was not able to attend, so that there are 17 chart3r members. The election of officers was then procerdsi with: The following was the result :—Mr F Hanky (patriarch), presiding officer ; Mr Oawald Baguley, worthy 01103i- ate ; Mr Charles P Bradbury, financial secretary Mr Jo,eph Aatbury, recording scribe ; Mr Samuel Tomlin- son treasurer. The following members were appointed to 'the remaining offices i—Samuel Tomlinson, jun.. George Smith, Harold Gamon, John Hankey, Albert E Sanders. William Spruce, Arthur Gamon and Hubert Higginson. Mr Thomas Heesorn, in an impressive man- ner, installed the newly-elected officers. It was decided to call it the "Rose of Halton Division" and Mr D Bisbrown, G.W.P. of Cheshire,then instituted ?be division in the name and by the authority of the Grand Lodge. Mr Bisbrown said he was proud to have the honour of instituting a new division at Elalton. He was full of hope that they would have a bright future. It was not possible for one member to run it—they must have the co-operation of all. Unitrd they would stand, divided they would fall. Their object should he to promote the harmony, advance the interests, and preserve unsullied the reputation of the Sons of Temperance. iRe was sure that God's blessing was resting on their efforts, and he was confident they would be crowned with success. (Applause.) Interesting and encouraging addresses were gi von by the following officers :—Messrs Thomas J Holt, Samuel Tomlinson, Alfred Noden, Thomas Heesom, Thomas Clarke, and Thomas R Whyte. The speakers pointed out that the object of the Order was the promotion of teetotalism and thrift, and whilst carried on, on Chris- tian principles it was of a strictly unssctarian charac- ter.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. St. Paul's Day Schools. The following repert has been received from M's Inspector of Schools :—Boys' School : "The school is very carefully organized and well discip- lined. Records are honestly kept and show good steady work throughout the year.' Girls' School: "The work in the girls' school is carried on with much vigour and brightness, and generally good effect." Infants' School: "The infants are in good order and their instruction is in most respects very satisfactory." The grant earned is the highest possible per child in each case. STOP THAT OPERATION 1 Eye Diseases are being :cured daily, without operation, by Mellor's New System of Treatment. Particulars Free.—MeLtou, Eye Special- list, 168, Oxford-street. Manchester, (13).—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>111 E EXAMINER FRIDAY NOVEMBER</head>
        <p>111 E EXAMINER FRIDAY NOVEMBER</p>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD ELECTION.</p>
        <p>The Progressives' Campaign.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a meeting was held in support of 11 the Progressives. Mr J Wilson was in the chair, and • he was supported by Mr J R Frith, S Taylor, F Norman, J Pritchard, J M Frith, R EL Posnett, W Handley, and the Rev A Wells and others. The Chairman said that he hoped they would return all the members who had previously represented them , with the addition of Mr Posnett whose speeches had the ' true ring. The clerical party who opposed them were violently opposed to Bourd Schools, but they had come to stay. The Voluntary schools were not capable ot dealing adequately with the education at the youth of England. Hence the springing up of the Board School system. The c:erical party always applauded anything which militated against the Board system. They were delighted with the Cocketton judg- ment. They said' they bad no right to educate the children of the poor out of the rates and therefore cried " down with the ratese' That cry won some of the allegiance of their party for nobody liked heavy • rates. The rate had gone up instead of down as could easily be proved. Whe• should the party which was always opposed to popular education want to centrol not only their own scuools but the Board as well ? He appealed to the people to return the progressive candi- dates. (Applause.) Mr Taylor said he would say something about a speech delivered by Mr unison hisitco-overseer. They as overseers met some time ago to decide on wnae amount of rate they should levy for the coming half year. The assistant overseer had an intimation from • the School Board officer that the requirements would be £1,050 for the coming half year. The question was what amount of poor rate abouldebe levied, He Mr Taster asked what amount led in the £ brought in for the last half year. He got the amount and reckoned it out. Last half year id in the £ brought £217 113 8.1. He said that was more than 40 ' in the £ and it really amounted to £l7 more then qd. Be, the speaker, did not tell Mr Sutton to put the 4-1 I there. Afterwards the Finance Committee of the School Board met to decide what amount was required. They decided only to have £l,OOO. Why ? That £5O made the difference of the id. At the meeting of the school Board he asked how it was that after the overseers had been intimated that £lO5O would be required who had altered it. The balance sheet this year showed a balance ofe£432 193 21 which would have to be reduced by £lO5. It should realLy have been £l,lOO to keep the balance what it had been last year. As an overseer after levying the rate to Eupply the School Board with £1053 it was not gratifying to see it altered without his authority. (Applause). Mr Handley said their friends on the other side at the lase election took as their cry " Down with the rates." Whatever was the sense in which they meant it the ratepayers undoubtedly understood that the rates would be lowered if the Church party were elected. Such a lowering of the rates had not come off, so this time they had inscribed on the standard" Keep down the rates." The progessivee said give the best education at the sole cost of the State, but let the State through a statutory and adequately appointed authority ex ,rcise an effective administrative csntrol. Without suggesting wrongful- ness in the matter of finance by the managers of volun- tary Schools did it not seem anomalous that Board Schools receiving the smaller share of money from the national taxes should have their aceounts audited by a Government official, while voluntary schools, receiving the larger amount from the public taxes should not have their accounts similarly publicly audited. In view of the impending Education Bill it seemed impera- tive that their School Board be so constituted as to be able to represent on their behalf the broadest and meet enlightened views on the education question. Toe election of the other aide would mean the pewee to re- °resent the narrower and restricted view as the opinion of Runcorn. They (the ratepayeri) would decide as to which it should be. Voluntary effort was even now in- adequate for the mainteaance of accommodation. Was the only alternative then to be an un-educated Runcorn? Mr Posnett said they were met on • serious errand. It should be perfectly clear that he was fighting for princi- ples. It was far bigger than anything between Canon Wood and Robert Poscett. The assets of the country were intelligence and brains, which should be cultivated to the best of their ability. But the spirit of Canon Wood's recent speech was entirely false. The party 'opposing them said they would have nothing to do with 'increasing the rates. There was no necessity to in- crease the rates. But that party were putting their hands down to the very deepest to get the best education for their own church children. But the poor , man lied as much right to develop his brains as thee rich man. He claimed that it was the cheapest in th long run to educate every child as much as possible. He had no personal feeling in the matter. He was speaking for principle and was opposed to clericalism. Clerical- ism meant Anglican Isolation. At two meetings at which Sir John Brunner took part the Church party boycotted the meetings. lie appealed for their vote on the broad principles of the best education for everyone. Mr Norman,after dealing with the literature published last year by the Clerical party, said that the Ceckerton judgments seriously imperilled important prerogatives of the School Board. He noticed that all Beards con- trolled by educationists or broad-minded Churchmen, were immediately summoned to see that their tater ssts were protected and safeguarded as far as possible. Tee Board Schools Enabling Bill received the support of ,such Board, but in Runcorn no aotion was taken. The Runcorn majority were perfectly satisfied that whatever the Government might do would b 3 the correct thing, and when the minority did attempt to support the Bill they were defeated. How Ii thi dad they heard from the Chairman of the Board or the Chairman of the School Management Committee of the splendid success of their School Board children in the various local examination. And yet it was only natural. Could they wonder at it? Time would not permit te deal with the financial aspect of affairs bet on 801219 other occasion be trusted to prove that the majority had not hesitated to circulate the most misleading figures in their uncurbe'd z ial to prejudice School Board education in the eyes of the people. It was suggested Chit this eleetion wit; altogether unnec sssary, that next year important changes in connection with education al affairs sheuld be introduced, and that therefore things might remain as they were. He arrived at a totally different conclusion. Within the past few years very determined efforts had been made by the clerical party to control the School Boards. Where they had succeeded in some cases they had come near a violation of the Cowper-Temple clause. It was of vital importance that the Scho ii Boards should be held by independent men so that when changes took place they might See no retrograde movement was made, and abev3 all prevent an extension of that sec- tarianism that had had such a baleful influence on our educational policy up to the present time. He earnestly wished, in view of this transitiou stage, on which they were about to enter, the clerical party would adont higher and nobler platform, drop the sordid question nf "down with the rates " ; rather endorse Mr Punch's nobler appeal, Come open your mine, let the least in the nation Be trained in the use of his hand and his eve ; Fling open your ;schools, for in them is salvation, Bv them is the country to live or to die. Let them emancipate themselves from parochialism and sectarianism, and do their utmost to perfect a national system of education on broad Bible Principles, leaving to each religious denomina- tion in Sunday schools to take the necessary measures for the conservation of the faith of its children. (Applause.) Such a proposal was not chimerical. The establishment of School boards through- out Scotland found religious bodies anxious and willing to cc-operate, and he would be a bold men who would dare to say that the cause of religion or education had suffered thereby. Ho feared that such a happy consum- mation was too much to expect and without doubt the outlook for educational matters was dark in- deed. (Hear, hear.) The present Government appeared to be completely under the heel of the clerical party. Every regulation, every ;reform had to be suited to their interest, or it" wee withdrawn or stamped out. That noble Englishman, John Bright, speaking on clericalism and education said " I am sur- prised that Churchmen themselves do not see that nothing tends mere to impede the progress of liberty, nothing is more fatal to independence of spirit in the public:than to add to the:povvers of the clergy in matters of education." (Applause.) 'The danger was that in the coming re-arrangement, the success that had crowned their efforts during the last two or three years would make them more ambitious than ever, and that their demands would mean the practical monopoly of the educational machinery of the country. Those who had made a study of the question ant knew what was being done in America and on the Continent were agreed chat a system of education run on clerical lines would not meet the demands of the future. Your British lad might know every line of the Church catechiem and every sermon of John Wesley, but they counted for nothing in the competition he had to face from his foreign competitors. Ia the meantime the mettle of educationists arid of Nonconfor- mists alike', would be tested, bet there was</p>
        <p>cRAwFoRD's "ALEX&amp;NDRA " BISCUITS. JI7ST</p>
