      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>no need to despair. In the dark days of the American constitution, old Abri.ham Lincoln encouraged his followers with the declaration that their opponents might gull the whole of the people part of the time they might gull some of the people all the time,but they coul I not gull the whole of the people all the time. And no power living could make him (Mr Norman) believe that a great nation like this when it realized the import- ance of education and realized tint it could only be properly educated when emancipated from the trammels ef sectarianism, would fail to rise to the occa- sion and bring itself lobo line with the other civilized countries of the world. (Applause.) The meeting concluded with a vote of thanks t 3 the ' Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Meeting of the Church Party.</head>
        <p>Meeting of the Church Party.</p>
        <p>Oa Thursday evening last week, at the Newtown Schools, Surrey-street, a meeting was held under the presidency of Mr W Shaw in favour of the ratepayers candidates. Present on the platform were Canon A. Wood, Rev W H Stables, Messrs E Imison, J Littler and Cunningham. The Chairman said they were heavily rated already, and did not need any more rates adding at all. It was not a false cry to say " Down with the rates." It had been said by the other side that the Church candidates got in on the understanding that they would pull the rates down. He did not understand that. They simply meant they could keep down the future rates as much as possible consistent with efficiency and economy. Mr John Littler appealed to the workingmen of Run- corn to give them fair Play. They had not abused the confidence reposed in them three years ago when they were returned by large majorities. They had done their best and had not taken advantage of their position' which was a strong one, but throughout the work of the Board had treated their opponents with absolute fair- ness. They were now charged with not being friends of education. They said look at the 14,000 schools, but they did not add they were built at the expense of mil- lions of money which had not come out of the rates but from the friends of the voluntary schools. (Applause.) Long before the state took any interest in educiatioa the voluntary school took upon itself the education largely of the law of the land. Plenty people would have no edoeation to-day but for the party which were now charged with not being friends of education. Mr Imison, replying to a statement by tin e Rev J Hirst Hollowell, said that they as managers did nor at'empt to use their position on the Board to drive chddran into their own [schools.. The Rev Hirst Hollowell also said that the aid grant was used to put a premiu non inefficiency. That was untrue. It was given to Board Schools in poor districts where Via School Board rate did not return a certain sum. That they were going to keep the rates down gave the impression that they ware not q site able to do that, bat had risen them tI. in the • £. As a matter of feet they bad not raised them the smallest part of a farthing. When they cams into office they formal the rate was 93 in the R. Tney proved that at the , meeting ,held at the School Board the other evening. Niw that they had left it they had left it at 91 in the ' £. Proceeding to review educational matters generally Mr Liaison deprecated educating persons for positions they were not likely to occupy which was a neeiless waste of the child' time an i the public motley. Toe wise thing for the ratepayers to do was to send men to the Board who were not inbued with fantastic notions They had nothing to be ashamed of and they would prove as worthy of their confidence at the end of the three years as at the present time. (Applause.) Father Chambers said the Catholic and Churchman were at one in the matter of education no matter how they might differ religously. The parents had the right to choose whateverschools they liked—Roman Catholic or Church. The Church of England had been for many years the great pioneer of the education of the country. (Applause.) Though a Catholic he was as good an Englishman as any in the room. (applause.) Ile had been educated at one of the Church of England grammar schools and thus owed a debt of gratitude to them. If they wanted to study their own interests they could not do bitter than stan 1 solid for their own m.embers. (Applause.) Canon Wood afterwards addressed the meeting,devot- ing himself principally to the answering to two letters which appeared in the " Examiner," those of Mr Peter Iltadlev, and "Imperialist." Mr Cunningham also addresssd the meeting and sail that while he admired Mr Well's courage on preaching on " Nonconformity not Schism" he did not agree with his attack on a fellow cleric. Toe Rev W U Scabies said he had been questioned 'whether he was toe gentleman who was in correspona- erica with Mr Wells. Mr Wells had stated he was cor- responding with someone re the recent mission at Weston Point and the impression had got abroad that he (Mr Stables) was the gentleman in question. He (the speaker) denied all knowledge of any correspond- ence on the subject and wished that statement to go forth to the public.</p>
        <p>Meeting in Trinity Schools. On Monday evening another well attended meeting wa.,, held at the 'Malty Church Schools, Runcorn, in support of the candidature of the Church candidates, Messrs W J Cunnin4ham, E Imixon, 3 I,i:ter and the ! R3v Canon Maitland Wood. The Rev C R Killick (vicar of tioly Trinity) presided, and said he thought it was a misnomer to call those who were on the opposite side " Progressives." and he could never see why they should do so. lie feit that their friends there that evening were rather the true Progressives, and ho objected to the other side rnonopazing the title. Mr Imison then addressed the meeting. He thought he ought to address himself to one of the questions that: was agitating the public mind, and that was, were they ! as ratepayers' candidates in favour of giving the work- ing man' s child the very best education possitl.!? As the Chairman had rernind‘,d the:n they belonged to a</p>
        <p>party which had for years and years taken an interest hi the education of the people. and which had done so at 'a time when there wag porphaps no; the same induce- ment; to take part in this work as at the present. He contended that they were very deeply indebted to the Church party, for had they not selected the re3ponsi- bility and established schools in almost every village throuzhout the land ?</p>
        <p>Mi. Littler,answering the question as to whether they, I as the " ratepayers' caw-bastes," still deserv-d their confidence, he said he thought they still deserved every support. As Mr liaison pointed out, they had kept all their promises, and they had dine their very beat to ' carry on the work efEcien ly. He thought, moreover, they hadfsucceeled. The WY. Cation Wood r4erred to the statement in the Progressives' address that the Board elected in 1E93 had increased the ratepayers' burdens by £1,200 in three years. It was necessary to say that this • was a compete misrepresentatior. The new Board had never, added anything additional to the expenditure which they met gfinar, 01. Canon Wood also read a letter he had received from Mr F Nor- man, to which he replied in an exhaustive manner. and i whilst admitting that instead of causing an increase of , 43 on the rate by the taking into the Board Schools of the children who had attended the Wesleyan School, an ' increase of 33 and a fraction oily had been caused. He proceeded to point out that, to give a generous limit, some 500 children had attended the Wesleyan School, whilst upwards of 2,000 attended the Voluntary Schools. Tnen pointing out that 2000, was four times 530, he emphasized his contention that if they followed the policy of the " Progressive " party and brought the Velantary Schools under the School Beard, it would mean an increase on the rates of at least is., and then he pointed out that such a course would mean a rate of Is 93 instead of 93.</p>
        <p>Meeting at St. Luke's. Oa Wednesday evening at the S. Luke's Mission a meeting was held in support of the ratepayers candi- dates. Mr Priestly occupied the chair. and he was sup- ported by Messrs J Littler, E Icon, Cuoninghara, the Rev Canon Wood and the Rev W fi Stahl The Chairman said that ths ratepayers' candidates had done their duty to them, and hoped the elector tte would return them. The Board Schools were equally as much thdird as the Progressives. He asked them not to cease their efforts in canvassing until nev.; week, and then their candidates would be at the top of the poll. (Applause).</p>
        <p>sMr John'son said he did not intend to say much, but he was awry so much bitter feeling had been imported in the cunt nt. It bad boen dune on tooth sides but much might have been left u Waif. Canon Wood oeQrned to bear the brunt of the whole attack. He WAS quite able to do so. Tans, were prepared to stand or fah by him. (Applause.) It seemed a strange thing that the rare- , payers were uumindiul of the great man they had in the Canon. He was an authority on educatign. [be Arch- bishop of Canterbury,in forming a committee on educa- tion, put Canon Wood on that committee as he was one man short. That was a great honour to Runcorn. (Applause.) He did good work on the committee %ill reduced chaos to order. Throughout the length and breadth of the diocese of Chester the man who stood pre-eminentlr head and shoulders above every one else was Canon Wood. He always spoke the troth and would not say anything that he did not irlieve to be true. He was sorry that personal friends of his (the speaker) had made such severe attacks on the Vicar. Mr Imioon was the Rupert of debate and could put the educational matter in a nutshell. Mr Littler was Canon Wood's right hand man and Mr Cunningham WAS well9cnown to all of them. His efforts to ameliorate the condition of the working men were most successful. All of them had their sympathy and they would go in by leaps and bounds or he would ho disappointed. ToPy were the four best men. Why? Because during the last three years they had proved It. Their opponents were &amp;ever men hut he hoped the Church party would be elected with Father C,hansbers. (Applause.) Mr Littler:said that the subject was almoct exhausted but he would like to thank Mr Johnson for his kind remarks. They should nut think of them as individuals but as the embodiment of a certain policy which they would carry our. The Pro- gressives would carry out another policy. The question to decide was which was the beEt policy for them and their children. There could be no truer friends of edu- cation than the voluntary party. They claimed their</p>
        <p>I support not only on account of the work done on the Board but for what they had done for education gener- ally. (Applause). Mr Cuenireresetarer said he was anxious for every one to know that they would carry on education as cheaply as possible. If they were not satisfied or thought they had not done their duty then they should vote for their opponents. He sympathised with those who bad to pay the rate and it often meant going short of other things. They would do all they coald to give them the cheapest education. This was essentially their policy. The keeping of the Voluntary schools was a great saving to the ratepayers and in them they gave a clear and deauite reiigious instruction which was the foundation of character. This was the best asset the nation could have. They in no way impaired the secular education which equipped them for the battle of life, Their friends bad a policy which was most difficult for them to put into words for it seemed so far above them. They had heard of foreign competition and this had given the Progressives a fright. They said they wanted a policy which would enable the children of the workingmen t ogo te the University. He hoped some- one would ask the other side who was to pay for this advanced education. Canon Wood said that many of the things he had to talk about were personal things, and none of the elections had been so had as this one. He wished to refer to something which was told him by people In whom he bad the greatest confid- ence. They were present at the meeting on Friday at Camden when Mr Posaett said that the Vioar said that " men were born to sweep the streets." This Mr Poe- nett said was a filthy untruth. He, the speaker, was advised that if that went all over the town, that it would be understood to mean that he had declared that statement, and that they ought not to get to anything better and that he was accused of having said some- thing that was absolutely and "filthily untrue." Ac- cordingly he put:Belled the letter to the people to the effect that kir Posnett had misunderstood the report in the local paper. Then Mr Posnett put out a paper that he had never used the word " filthy " demanding an apology and the name of the informant That day the infurmants had called on Mr Posnett and they had declared to him positively that they bad heard him use the very words. (Applause.) Mr Posoett had written to him as he (tae speaker) had written to him before, admitting that he said all except the word ee filthy." (Applause.) Then there was disagreement and some misunderstanding. Toe two ladies say he said the word, he said be did not. He.hoped he did not say it, and was glad it was, after all, only a mitNer of one word, Mr Posnet: had withdrawn the imputa- tion upon to s veracity. The taking over of the Wesleyan scrmols, he said, had cost the ratepayers I great deal of money which any chartered accountant would say and if this amount was multiplied four times by the Voluntary schools being taken on there would be snore on the rates than the people would care for. Tae Progressives had given them a bad time for the last three years which had at last Culmivated into the present attack. He protested against the trumpery letter published in the " Examiner " from " Imperia- Pat." He would take no notice of it nor of the foul abuse it contained. Toe other side reprinted it and sent it round:the town.Sech an action was absolutely shame- ful. At the last meeting of the School Board, Mr Norman brought two motions, the first imputing a want of truth to Mr Imisoa ! Second, want of truth to the whole piny. He then s -item lie appealed to his opp on- en's as chairman and then as Vicar to conduct flee busi- ness without imputing want of trot's CD the opposite party. lie found nearly a column of abuse and the person who wrote it and the persons who put their names at the bottom should be thoroughly ashamed of themselves. Dealing with the closing of the voluntary schools, the Vicar said it would be quite possible to compel them to close if Board schools were opened in competition. It looked suspicious why all this work should be done to get the newer on the Board unless some move like this were contemplated. He propounded the q iestion to the Progressives " Wnat fault did they fled with the work of the Board," and " What alterations did they intend to make provided they were put at the top of the poll:" (Applause). Votes at thanks concluded the meeting. On Tuesday and Wednesday. meetings were held in support of the respective psrties at the Church Selo the Greenway-road school, and Bethesda schoolroom. All the candidates were well received.</p>
      </div>
