      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Challenge to Canon Wood by Mr.</head>
        <p>A Challenge to Canon Wood by Mr.</p>
        <p>F. J. Norman.</p>
        <p>Si stroi!p: has become lecal feeling M the School Board election that its latest phase is rather a novel one. Toe great argument of the Clerical Party has been that the taking over of the Vlluntary Schools cost the ratepayers over 41 in the £. This Mr Norman, on behalf of the progressives, has denied in season and out of season, and it has been as resolutely asser:ed by the Vicar and his followers. This argument, a most telling one against the Beard system, if tru., Mr Nor- man has determined, if possible, to knock the bottom out of, Accordingly on Menclay last the following challenge was sent privately to the Rev Canon Wood. The rev gentletnan did not reply to tho communication, brit at a meeting on Monday gave the text of the letter together with a reply t it he hadigone into the Wesleyan Finance and found that the taking over of the Wesleyan scoools put a fraction over 31 in the £ and I le-,t 41 as stated cost School Board election and last Board meeting. On Wednesday the following bias were issued from the Progressive Cernmitttee room containing the text of the challenge and a further bill</p>
        <p>CHALLENGE T 3 CANON WOOD. Runcorn, November 18M, 1901. DEAR CANON WOOD.</p>
        <p>In November, 1893, speaking at Holy Trinity, you stated :—" The church party had printed and posted on the walls (facts for voters) a statement about the Wes- leyan Schools. They had only just taken facts. They had an object lesson. That one voluntary school was given up three years ago, and it now cost 41 in the £ on the rates and increased the debt of the town between £4,000 and £5,000. What you assert as a "fact estab- lished beyond dispute " I have described as a grossly exaggerated and misleading statement. In support of this I have explained that in your zeal to discredit School Board Elucation you load the transfer of the Wesleyan Schools with the total difference in the rate of 51 in '95 and 91 in '93, whereas you know that the accounts of the Board shew amongst other causes. lit—That a con- siderable portion of this difference arises from the reduced rateable value of the two periods. 2 cd— Chat a further considerable portion is in the increased cost of the old section of tie Board Schools, altogether apart I from the Wesleyan Schools. 3rd—Tnat in your desire I to shock the poor people you introduce capital outlay after having fully provided for its it rest and repay- ment in the increased rate. 4:h—That the accounts of , the Wesleyan Schools which were kept separate under alt. Pritchard's business like chairmanship falsify your assertion. You will agree with ma that it is most unsatisfactory that two members of the same I Board each possessed of the authentic documents should confuse the public with such conflicting statements. I, therefore, propose that you name some chartered accountant of standing to whom we will jointly submit the official figures, and if he will certify the accuracy of your statement I will pay the cost of such reference and give £25 t, the Cottage Hospital, while if you fail to obtain such a certificate you stand mulct in the same penalty. The matter is of public im- port, and I requ.st the favour of your rep!y before Wednesday at the latest. After that date I shall reserve the liberty of publishing this note. Yours' f9ithf ally, FREDERICK JOHN NORM &amp;N.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>CANON WOOD CLIMB I DOWN.</head>
        <p>CANON WOOD CLIMB I DOWN.</p>
        <p>Canon Wood has during three yeBri Persistently ex- cited the prejudices of the people against School Board Elueation on the qulstion of oost by publishing figures which he pledged himself were true, " established be- yond doubt." Why then does he not face the business- like challenge of Mr Norman to prove his assertions be- fore a chartered accountant, the only comp,tent auth- ority to deal with them ? At Holy Trinity on Monday night, after receiving this challenge, Canon Wood ad- mitted he had exaggerated the figures to the extent of 25 per cent His " Corrected assertion is still a grossly exaggerated and misleading statement. Re is attempting to evade the real issue by a great mass of figures which no one can follow. There is only one straight-. forward and honourable course for Canon Wood to pur- sue. He must either accept the challenge which would enable the ratepayers to have the truth and nothing but the truth, or he must withdraw his statement. Other- wise, straightforward men of all creeds must of neces- sity dissociate themselves from such tactics, and vote for business men who have no interest in confounding the ratepayers and will rot do 60.—EI4NDLEY, NORMAN, POSNETT, PRITCUARD, and TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>Truth, justice, and an equal chance for every child. Speaking at Sr. Luke's on Wednesday evening, the Vicar in a further reply to the challenge sail that their great argument was that when the Wesleyan Schools were given up s.s they were on the Ist of July, 1895,and handed over to the Board they became a source of expense to the ratepayers. They were under the im- pression that the rate had gone up 41 in the £ in con- sequence of those sohoole. Mr Norman had sent him a hug letter pointing out different things to him and he had therefore gone very carefully into the figures. There was no doubt but in saying 41 they were saying too much. He believed the amount—as he said on Monday night was a trillo of a fraction over 31 in the £. Be thow,ht that having admitted so much that he would have-been let off but this was not to be the case and a further bil! had tren put out which said that Canon Wood had admitted exaggerating his figures. He the speaker bad not exaggerated them. He was only mis- taken about them. The bill went on to say that what he the speaker said on Monday was a grossly exagger- ated and mi.leading statement. He did not agree with that. Tht bill went on that the onlv way to settle the dispute was to put the accounts in the hands of a char- tered accountant. He had no objection whatever to a chartered accountant'provided theyknew what they were going to decide. Mr Norman had never said bow much they cost, and as soon as he said the amount he would submit to tbe accountan: being called in end see who t was right in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>Reply by Canon Wood to Mr: F. J.</p>
        <p>Norman.</p>
        <p>The Vicarage, Runcorn, 21st November 1901. Dear sir,—la reply to your letter of the 16th inst if you will make a statement as to the amount by whicix you think the taking over of the Wesleyan Schools has increased the rates I am willing to submit our difference to a chartered accountant on either side with some expert in elementary school matters as umpire. I make no further reference to your letter because I know from a handbill that you are aware of what I said- at Trinity Scodols on Monday night.—Faithfully yours,</p>
        <p>A. MAITLAND WOOD.</p>
        <p>THE NOMINATIONS. The last hour tor the nominations ot candidates for the School Board was tour o'clock on Friday afternoon, at the offices of Mr Geo. F Ashton, the returning officer. Tile following ger.itemen were nominated : Joseph Chambers, St Edward's, Irwell-lane, clerk in holy orders. Nominated on two p-ipere. William James Cunningham, 135, Greenway-road, 'err. Nominated on six papers. William Handley, Aldersyde, Norman-road, savings in actuary. Nominated on four papers. Edwin Raison, Heatherlsa,Wesoon-road, jeweller, &amp;I. ;ominated on six papers. John Littler, 77, Greenway-road, quarry owner. rominated on five papers. Frederick John Norman, Lyndhurst, justice of Vas ?cane. Nominated on two papers. Robert Harold Posnett, The Mount, Moughland-lane, justice of the peace, tanner. Nominated on two papers. James Pritchard, the &amp;me, Highlands-road, justice of the peace. Nominated on t.o papers. Samuel Taylor, Hyde Mount. Halton-road, Runcorn, justice of the peace. Nominated on two papers. Alfred Maitland Wood, Tae Victrage,Higher Runcorn. clerk in holy orders. No:ninated on eight papers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES. [By • CORRESPONDENTj ,</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES. [By • CORRESPONDENTj ,</p>
        <p>The electors on Wednesday will have to decide a knotty problem. The last stage of the School Board election is now entered upon. During the last fortnight the views of both parties—" the ratepayers candidates" and the Progressive party— have been promulgated on the platform and by the handbill, the canvasser and the press. It is,there- fore, quite time that the ratepayer should make up his mind to which party be belongs, and which principles be espouses. Knowing the hard-headed- ness of the Runcorn workingman, I canno,, think that he will be led away as he was three years ago by the specious cry of "Down with the rates.' To a very large extent the last election hinged on the question of cost, and the Voluntaryist put the imagined cost of a School Board educated town in the most lurid light possible. The Wesleyan schools, it was contended, made the rates rise to the extent of at least fourpence in the ..C. This cry has been again used, but Mr Norman, with a tenacity eminently praiseworthy has lost no time in nailing the false coin to the counter. Hence the novel and almost unique challenge which appears in another portion oi our paper. Obviously the opponents of the Board system are on the horns of a dilemma. They must either withdraw the state- ment and acknowledge their error, and accept the gage of battle thrown down by Mr Norman, or maintain an assumed indifference which would seriously militate against their chances on polling day.</p>
        <p>--- On Wednesday the Vicar apparently acceyted Mr Norman's challenge to send the accounts and books to a chartered accountant, but be added a proviso which be knew could not possibly be entertained for on moment. He asked Mr Norman to state what amount, in his opinion, the rate had been increased by the taking over of the Wesleyan schools. Now the question is one of fact. The Canon has made a statement which Mr Norman denies. The onus of proof rests entirely with the one who asserts, not with the one who maintains the negative. Suppose Mr Nor- man fixes an amount and the Chartered Accountant finds that both the Canon and the business man are wrong who will pay the £25 at stake to the Cottage Hospital Fund? What Mr Norman's opinion is as to the amount of rate imposed on the ratepayers has absolutely nothing to do with the question. The Canon pledged his word of honour that the cost was 4d and a fraction. The challenge has drawn from him that it i 9 in reality only 31 and a fraction. Both statements Mr Norman maintains are grossly exaggerated, and without further equivocation it is incumbent in face of the challenze, for the Canon to stand by his guns or candidly admit that he has been in the wrong in both instances.</p>
        <p>Already there has been too much of the personal element introduced in the election. It should , not be forgotten that we are fighting not for men ' but priaciples—principles which mean, the salva- tion or ruination of the Empire. It must be ad- mitted that the principle we sapport is the system which puts the management of local education into the hands of the people, and not into the hands of managers who, while receiving their sole support from the state are not governed by the ratepayers r taxpayers at all. Toe Board system is essentially the workman's system As its ramifications extend there is no reason why the coming generations will nor be better equipped, educationally, far beyond the dreams of the narrow Voluntaryist, "Knowledge is power and the more technical knowledge the workingman possesses the better for his own chances in life and the better for the prosperity of this once " Morrie England "of ours.</p>
        <p>Last election the total number of votes polled for the church candidates, including Father II B Gore. totalled 11,953 which roughly speaking means 265 voters per candidate. The Progressives, including Mr Walter Callier, polled 10,054 votes equivalent to 223 voters reckoning as is the case this year live .candidates. The difference then per candidate is but 40 voters and this on a division means that but 20 electors have to change their minds and vote for the oppoite side and the Progressives are in. Surely this is u3t an inirossible task and I venture to hope and predict that this time next week the Progressives will be in power.</p>
        <p>can</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>FGOTTIALL</head>
        <p>FGOTTIALL</p>
        <p>- ----- On Saturday Runcorn met Batley at home. Both teams turned out pretty strong, and the earlier part of the day gave promise of a fine struggle. A short time before 3 o'clock the ground was examined and pronounced tit for play. Strangely enough, how- ever, a heavy blinding mist arose and completely enshrouded the football ground, and at the time advertised little could be seen save the linesmen on the side of the press stand. There were but few spectators, and these few when'they saw thsre was but little chance of the fog lilting, got disgusted at the weather and wisely went home.</p>
        <p>The game,judging from the referee's whistle and the shouts of the spectators at either end of the field, was a fast and furivs one. 0114 o!caston- ally, however, could any of the play be seen and the players seemed more like ghoQtly phantoms looming in the mist than real football players. But . a short time after the start a burst of cheering announced that something was on the carpet, and a Iscout in the shape of a boy sent out to reconnoitre broug,ht back word that one of the Runcorn players had succeeded in getting over the line. This was repeated more than once sod eventually Runcorn romped home the winners by a substantial lead. Doubtless the bestleam won but the game could not be criticised for reporters as well as players were, for once, it a fog.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>PENKETH</head>
        <p>PENKETH</p>
        <p>WE9LEYAN BAND OF HOPE —An entertainment in connection with the above S'iciety was held in the Wesleyan day school on Friday night last. The Rear John Gunnell occupied the chair. The programme VMS well rendered and was sustained by the following : Cornet duet, G and W Carman ; recitation, H Ambler ; pianoforte solo,Miss Hughes, euphoniutn solo, H Plump - ton. Songs were also rendered by Misses Dd Hamer and E Plimpton, and the choir sang two choruses. Au amusing dialogue " Troubles of an Editor " wasa dmir- ably,rendered by the following artistes Messrs B Ireland G Carmari,ll Plumpton and H Ambler.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Local War Items,</head>
        <p>Local War Items,</p>
        <p>- - ccordiug to the casualty lists the following are dangerously U , suffering from enteric fever : Lancashire Regimenc.-4,732 Privato J Wormsley, Mafeteng, Basutoland, November 14:h. 14 South Lancashire Ref/in:mt.-5,963 Private A. Duncan, Vryheid. November 14th. Th. fallowing is extracted from the casualty lista issued on Monday night :- 2nd Cheshire Regiment, Captain C W Collins died from wounds at El tudefontein, November 14. Ist South Lancashire Mounted Infantry, 542 Private T Turton was dangerously wounded near Scandertom November 14th. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S RIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERI.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
