      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>MINOR OASES. -__ Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES. -__ Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FalDAY.—Eefore the Deputy Mayor, Alderman Roberts (in the chair). Councillors William Pierpoint. Smethurst and John Roberts, Messrs Hepherd and Thornton. Tmasta.3:s —Jane Johnson, Turner-street was summonei fcr making use of threats towards Sarah Jane Lee on the 11th inst.--Uomplainant steed that defendant threatened to stick a knife in her.—Defendant who made her 6.lth appearance was bound over to keep the peace for six months and ordered to pay 4s 6d costs. MONDLY.—B4ore the Mayor, Mr J. Charlton Parr (in th chair), Mr F Monks, Aldermen Sutton, Hatchinson and Heaton. Councillors Smethurst and Pierpoint and Messrs Birchall, Hayward, Guest. Wright and Thornton. MAISTENANCE CASEs.—Wehard Britton. 6 Wotaley-street. was ordered to pay forth with £2 18s. arrears under an order for the maintenance of his son on the Ship Clio and, also the costs of the ease or go to p-ison for a month.—Joseph , Maher 345, Winwick-road. whose son is in the Blackley Reformatory School was under similar circumstances ordered to pay £1 19s and the costs or undergo a month's imoriron- mant.—Chief Inspector Phillips proved the cases. FIGHTING —John Bibby, labourer. 253, Winwick-road, and Edward Bretherton, labourer, 347. Winwick-road were ordered to pay the costs and were bound over to keep the peace for fighting in West-street on the gth inst. Sergeant Evans Droved the case. Defendants admitted that they foaght, but said it Was over when the officer arrived on the scene. William Darbyshire. moulder. 4, 3utton-street, and Henry Taylor, labourer, 16. Dial-strset, were similarly dealt with for fighting in Pcotland-road,on the 10th inst. Evidence was given by Inspector Rowbotham Boys MISCoNDITCT—FINES INFLICTED.—Thomas Pember- ton (14). 4. Owen-strest ; William Massey, (13), 61, Wattrin- street ; Thomas Broadhurst (18). 122, Winwick-rold ; James Lawrenson (14). 4, Stamford-street ; Charles Mackey, (17). 14, Stamford-street ; Daniel Johnson. (14). 11, Stamford- street ; Norman Newton (13). 2, Baxter's-yard. were sum- moned for committing a breach of the peace by being dis- orderly in Winwick road on the 7th inst.—P.O. Lewis said that on the evening of the date rnentione4 ha saw the lads on the footpath in Winwick-road jostling one another and shouting. He moved them away and they hooted him. He returned to the place later and they were again there conducting themselves in a most disorderly mauner. He had had numerous complaints about this sort of miscon- duct, and had several times cautioned these very boys.— Joseph Harrison. butcher, 124 Winwick-road, said the defendants were near his shop. He had suffered much annoyance and inconvenience and had made repeated corn-s plaints. He toad even threatened to leave the place unlese this disorderly behaviour were put a stop to. On on occasion he went out to move some boys. They brough several young men to him and he sustained a black eye.— The Mayor said the defendants' conduct was shocking and must be stopped. It was the first time they had been be- fore the Court, and they would each have to pay the costs. 2s 6d. R.S.P.C.A. PROSECUTION.—Luke Hampson, 11, Brookland- street, and Harry Whitney, 10. Hanover-street were sum- moned at the instance of the Royal Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals, the first named for working a horse which was ita an nnfit condition and the latter for allowing it to be worked.—lnspector Powell, of the Sanitary Department of the Corporation, said he was in Horsemarket-street at noon on the 9th inst. and noticed a horse standing near to Parr's Bank. He saw on the animal's near shoulder a large raw wound about the size of a half- crown piece. He drew the attention of a policeman to the . - horse. He lifted the saddle, and underneath was another wound fully the size of a five shilling piece. The horse was in poor condition and not fit for work. The wound under the saddle was pressed very tightly by some padding.—P 0' Stewart corrohorated.—lnspector Print said he saw Mr Maginn, who said that if he had seen it he would not have allowed it out. Whitney admitted his responsibility. He said the horse was harnessed when he saw it In the morning and he did not examine it. Hampton said the horse had been resting for a week. He had driven it to Wigan. and took it in as soon as the sore was pointed out to him.— Hampton now said that he knew of the sore under the saddle. The ostler at Wigan said it was all right. He was surprised when the other sore was pointed out to him.and at once took the horse to the stable.—Whitney said the horse had been resting for a week. It was harnessed whm he saw it in the morning, and he just casually glanced over it and palmed it. There was no sore the size of a half-a-crown piece: A six- penny piece would have covered it. There was certainly an old bruise under the saddle, but the horse had rested, and he thought it was quite better.—The Chief Constable said that Whitney was fined in august for a similar off aice.—Defend- ants were each fined 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Warrington County Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY —Before 31r a Comber (In the chair), and Mr d</p>
        <p>J Bleck ly. ALLEGED TRESPASSI‘G AT SANRET.--John Holden. John Bider, Ric Mud Rider, John Martin, William Martin, Thomas Wright and Marshall Whitfield were summoned for trespassing on land in the occupation of John Knowles, Sankey, in search of game on November 35d.—Mr It Rears aoeared for the prosecutor and the defendants were represented by Mr Browne.—The case for the prosecution waslthat George Lee, a keener in the employ of Mr C U Parker. who had the right of shooting over several farms, was croaiing a field in company with his son in the direction of Mr Knowles' land when he saw the defeadants on lir Knowles' land. They had a couple of dogs and ferrets with them and Lee siw them " bolt " a rabbit. The rabbit ran across the field and prisoners and the dogs pursued it. Ul- timately it ran into a drain or a hole. When defendants saw Lee coming they ran away but were pursued by Lees soq and caught in Widnes-rosd.—ln cross- examination Lee admitted that the defendants bad a rat cage cmtainlng two rats, Mr. Wh tdeld, the father of one of the defendants, said he had given defendants permis- sion to go ratting on his farm. Delendants, however, were on Mr. Knowles' land when the offence took place.—The defence was that the defendants only went ratting on Mr Whitfleld's land, and never saw a rabbit.—Without hearing any evidence for the defence, the Bench dismissed the case. THEFT oF A GEN AND Sum AT SAIIKET.—Jacob Platt Wail Charged with stealing a gun, pouch, flask and quantity of shot,yalue aced the'propertylof John Knowles.Sankey.—He pleaded gailty.—Eyidence was given to the effect that the gun and snot were missed from the 'barn in which they were kept, and subseqnently pris met. was seen to hide the gun in some long grass in a field. 'the pouch and disk cuntaining the shot were found at his residenze.—A fine of 10s and costs was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MONDAY—Before Messrs Alfred Thomas In the chair), and 11 Reynolds. A SUSPICIOUS CHARACTER.—Joseph Basnett. of no fixed abode, was charged with loitering with unlaveril intention on the premises of Thomas Carr on Friday.—Thomas Carr, jeweller, said that at 9.30 on the night in question he found the prisoner on his premises in Norman-road. He had no right to be there.—Sergeant Gregory stated that the prisoner when arrested denied being thsre for an unlawful purpose. —Committed for 2 L days. DRUNK msagae.—Bliz ibeth Baton and Frederick Harrison were fined 5s and cots for being drunk.—P.C. Gilpin and Sergeant Gregory proved the cases. QUARRELSISIE WONIEN.—EIIen Wainwright Hanley-street. summoned Alice Bradshaw Rutland-street, for using thre it- erring language towards her.—Thn'e was also a cross sum- mons.—The evidence showed that both persons had been using bad language towards the other and both were bound over for six month with sureties of 4Cs. PROPANE LANGUAGE —For using profane language Thomas Woods was fined 5s and costs:--r C Edwards prove tn.-.) offence. EDUCATION. -Emma Watersoa and David Walker were fined 6d and coats for neglecting to send their children to chap!. Messrs T Rowyer and G Howard proved the cases.</p>
        <p>The contract to build thirty bridges for the Uganda Railway has been obtained by tbe American Briige Com- pany, who underbid all competitors. A farme.'s named John Crotty, was on Tuesday :morning found na irdered at Ballingrane, County Cork. Your arrests have been made.</p>
      </div>
