      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Escaped Convict's Trading</head>
        <p>The Escaped Convict's Trading</p>
        <p>Transactions.</p>
        <p>Public Examination in Bankruptcy.</p>
        <p>The public ezarnication in bankruptcy of John Brooks, or Garlick, who escaped from S:afford prison whilit under sentence of five years' penal servitude, in February, 1899, and subsequently commenced business in Hull as • sawmill proprietor, was held at the Hull Bankruptcy Court on Monday, before Mr Registrar Rollit. The debtor is well known in Lancashire and Cheshire, having undergone terms of imprisonment for offances at Liverpool, Manchester, Warrington, Bolton, and ‘Vigan. He appeared in custody of two warders from Stafford prison, where he has been since his re- capture.</p>
        <p>Examined by the Official Receiver, the debtor said that his .• real and true " name was John Garlick. He was a joiner by trade. He bad traded as John Grant, John Dixon, John Henry, John Brooks, and the Ormskirk Ginger Bread Company. He started business in Hull in April last, when be brought with him between £4OO and £5OO ha cash and about £2OO in materials. He came from Leeds, where be had been staying. Before that he had been in business at Ashtor-in-Makerfield and in Scotland as a metal broker. In 1895 or 1896 be com- menced business at Warrington. He could not say whether he Waa adjudicated bankrupt there in 1896, be- cause he was in gaol, but there were some proceedings, and be underwent an examination in court. He was surprised to hear that his liabilitiea there amounted to £9OO ;he thought they would be about £3OO. He denied that he ever opened in South Castle-street, Liver- pool, under the style of the Commercia Debt Collecting, and Business Agency. His principal business in Hull ' was conducted at tne Albion Sawmills, but he had five branches. At the time of his stoppage his turnover was between £l5O alci £250 per week. He chiefly devoted himself to box making and cutting up box stuff. He arranged for the purchase of ten acres of land on the outskirts of the ci'y for the extension of the bu•ine3s, and he started making preparationa there. Altogether he employed about 120 hands. He admitted that be bad an intention of turn- ing the business into a limited liability company, and if be bad done an he would have wanted snmething for his own inventions, which he valued at £7OO. The amount he owed to uoaecured creditors in Hull was lbetween £2,000 and £2.200, not £3,000. He never told any of ITs Hull creastors that he an undischarged bankrupt a Warrington, because he did nit know he was. He thought the stock he lett behind him when be went away Was %yeah £2,000. At that time his workmen wanted £l2O fvm him tor Wa,V9. He left on Saturday October 12, and he expected lois workpelple to get their pay out of the Wee. He left Hull because he feared something had cune out about his escape from prison. Three writs had alFio been served upon him. He took £3O or £4O with him and wEnt to Car- diff, where be intended to make firewood under the name of" John Stuart." A week before he went away he was advised that it was improbable he could turn his business int a limited liability company. He did ! not make up his mind to leave Hull until the day of his departure. He was not surprised to hear there was nearly a riot am )ng hi 3 w )rkpenple when they found they cc uld not get their wczes.—The Official Receiver : And you went away perfeetly contented to let them get their wages oat of the estate' as and when they could F—Tne Debtor : I went away diseontented. I could not help myself. If I had stopped I could not have mended matters. .</p>
        <p>Further examined, the debtor said that he did not know whether his wife was alive or not. Ho bad not seen her for 15 years. His insolvency was duo to delaTs and hindrances on the part of other people. Taking the buiness as a going concern he was not insolvent. Questioned by Mr A M Jackson for the petitioning creditor, the debtor said that he was 42 years of age, and was by trade a joiner, having served his time at Bolton and Manchester. Be started business on his own acc=unt when he was lo,tween 17 and 13 Telrs of age. At that time he contracted for two chapels and built them, and then returned to the bench. He had been in Orison on five or six occasions. The last time he was in business was in 1396, and thst was at Ashton-in-Makel- field. He was in prison a large portion of the time between ihat year and his coming to Hull. He came to hull because he wanted a solace to settle thmn in. He strongly denied that his objett was " make a purse " and leave on the first opportunity. He wished to carry oat a bona-fide business and to mike money honestly. Toe examination was then closed, and the debtor removed in custody of his gaolers.</p>
        <p>Meeting of the Parochial Committee.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee was held in the schoolroom, Stockton Heath, on Thurs- day last week. Mr J Clarke presided and_there were also present: Messrs. G. Pilling J. Whitley, Pearson, Fair- hurst, Mason, with W. E. dough (assistant clerk), J. Swinton (surveyor), and J. Farrington (inspector), Mr. Ashton(the clerk), was absent owing to the illness of his brother.</p>
        <p>ADDITIONAL POWERS SOINITIT. Mr. Hough submitted a letter from the Local Govern- ment Board dated the ldt November referring to the application of the Rural District Council for an order investing them with the powers contained in section 28 of the Towns Police Clauses Act for Stockton Heath, The Board inquired whether the Council had considered each of the matters contained in the section and were satisfied that the powers were necessary. The Chairman said each member of the committee re- ceived a copy of the section referred to. Stockton Heath was now an urban district and such powers were absolutely necessary. It was decided to inform the Local Government Board that the powers were necessary in such a pbpu • bons district as Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>Sere ofFootball Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches played on Saturday must re,ch the Head Office, Bewsey-street Warrington, before 10 o'clock on Thursday morning, otherwise they eannot be inserted.</p>
        <p>A METHODIST MINISTER of 40 years' standing, says, "For years I was troubled with Indigestion and consti- pation, and suffered greatly frcm giddiness, nervous de. bility and sleeplessness. 5161151 "The best medical skill seemed , of no avail, and I quite thought • : my ministerial work was coming il'" to a close. However, a friend ,• ' u,rged me to try Seigel's Syrup,• . • and four bottles sufficed to make my cure complete." (Rev.) J. SrEarstsy, Winterton, Doncaster, 5,1,01. 4.: • •</p>
        <p>S 1 OCKTON HEATH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>TIPPING OF REFUSE.</head>
        <p>THE TIPPING OF REFUSE.</p>
        <p>Mr Richard Miller, nightsoil contractor, wrote stating that the ashpit refuse tipped on Mr Mounfield's land was deposited there at Mr Mounfield's request. Messrs GrPenall, Whitley and Co., the owners, had written to him asking him to atop tipping there and no further refuse had been deposited. Be had had nothing to do with the burning of the rubbish complained of. The Chairman said Messrs Greensll's received a notice from the Nuisance Inspector, but it was clear from Mr Miller's letter that they were not responsible. The firm were not going to allow the land to be a rubbish tip.</p>
        <p>Till PAYING OF BOSTOCFC AND GASKELL-STREETS. lsr Whitley asked what was being done in connection with the proposed paving of Bostock street and Gaskell- street.</p>
        <p>------- Mr Hough said he was instructed to say that tenders were to be advertised for on Saturday for the making and paving of Bostock-street. With reference to Gas- kell-street, the opposition had not been withdrawn and therefore the Clerk could not advertise for tenders.</p>
        <p>" Scenes from Shakespeare." A pleasant and profi,able time was spent, at the meeting of the lVycliffe reung People's society on Monday evening when a recital ot several scenes from Shakespeare's plays was given by EOM e of the members. No attempt was made at stage effect but the recital was given in a manner which showed that the performers had entered into the sPirit of. the scenes depicted, and the applause which followed each scene was evidence of the satisfaction of the audience. There were four scenes in all, taken from Shakespeare's " Two I gentlemen of Verona," " The Merchant of Venice." "King Lear,' and " A midsummer night's I dream." The parts were well sustained and con- siJerable pains must have been taken in commit- ting the pieces to memory.At the close,the efforts of the members were acknowledged on the proposition of the Chrorman (Mr C Eccles) seconded by Mr C H Hibbert. Mr Gauld responded and the proceed- ings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. BATLEY.</p>
        <p>Thls math was played at Runcorn on Saturday, and was the only Northern Leinfue game possible. There had been some frost during the night, but an inspec- tion was made of the ground, which was declared quite fit for the game. Then the fog, which had cleared off with the noonday lan, again began to form, and with the exception of a fringe of about ten yards from the touch line opposite the stand not a pitch was visible. Even the kick-off was unseen. and then there came a rush past, the players loom- ing:up like giants in a shadograph show. The linesman was very active, rushing hithar and thither, and thus the repor- ters knew to some extent wnere she game was proceeding, but not a thing could be seen through the thick veil of fog. Williams (Runcorn) left the field with a damaged ankle. Runcorn kept play in Batley quarters. A touch kick was quite an incident, but th s players were quickly lost to view. great shout at tte Batley end testified that Runcorn bad scored, and a scout brought word that Butterworth had scored a try, and that Farnacr had failed at goal. Williams returned to the field, and made a good dribble past tile stand. Flay continued in the Batley half. Then there were opetations in the centre, and eioughton put in a big kick, which caused the players to disappear again, followed by two successive shouts, and again the people on the stand were informed that Walker had crossed the line, and that Farmer had added a goal. The stand spectators now had a view of several scrummages and some kicking and then Batley rushed to the Runcorn line, but not a movement could be seen. Ralf-time : Runcorn. 1 goal; Batley, nothing. Resuming, the fog fiend still reigned, and it was only momentary glimpses of the players that the stand splattors were privileged to see, but, as the touch judge travelled from end to end pretty frequently.the game was evidently more even. Richardson added another try, and Moulton kicked a goal. Spurr scared for Batley, Fisher kicking a goal, Runcorn 13 points, Batley 5 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
