      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>CHRISTMAS CARDS</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS CARDS</p>
        <p>Art Novelties, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>"There is nothing new under the sun" may be a saying generally accepted as true but there is something inconsistent in the attitude of people who believe it and yet see something new every day of their lives. However the saying is not in- tended to be understood in its literal meaning. For all of Us there is plenty that is new under the sun. We are living in a new century and under a new reign, both ot which have been signally com- memorated by Messrs Raphael Tuck and Sons, the famous art publishers, who contrive to be always issuing "something new." As Christmas approaches they astonish the public with the multitudinous variety of novelties and new things in the line of Christmas cards, booklets, calendars, and the like which issue from Raphael House. Every year an art wave flows from Messrs. Tuck and Sons well- known headquarters, which wave spreads all over the world to the delight and not to the dismay of the people who dwell therein. One would imagine that Messrs Tuck's fund of originality would become• exhausted, but such does not seem to be the case, for this year's productions exceed in beauty and newness anything we have seen from Raphael House for many years past—which is high praise. First and foremost we have some- thing which is not in itself new, i.e., a reproduction of the last of the loyal special I Christmas cards the firm had the honour of annually' preparing for Her late Majesty Queen Victoria, and which was issued in 1900. The subject treats of the ever-absorbing theme of the Madonna and the Infant Child, tenderly rendered in her own inimi- table style by Harriet M Bennett, the grace, treat- ment, and colouring being exquisite. Her late Majesty herself, only a month before her death, pronounced the card "beautiful," which indeed it is, and the British public should be grateful to Messrs Tuck and Sons for affording them the opportunity of acquiring so unique a souvenir of their dead Queen: The price of the reproduction ranges from 2s 61 to 53. In Xmas and New Year earns Messrs Tuck have a record collection comprising no less than 1,000 sets de- signed in upwards of 100 differ,mt styles, and composed of 3,000 distinct designs. Either a casual or a critical inspection of this remarkable line will charm and delight. Boved cards and humorous cards are special sorts in addition to the others and here again there is endless variety. Calendars bave always been a speciality of Messrs Tuck's and this year the 3 are to the fore with some exceedingly choice and useful designs, numbering over 200. The most famous masterpieces of Turner, Landseer, Constable, Herring, Bonheur, Lord Leighton and others are reproduced in this form. A series of daily tear-off block calendars have been introduced for the first time, and should be a popular feature. In juvenile toy and gilt books there is an unparalleled selection, nursery rhymes and fables being presented in the most charming guise. Father Tuck's Annual takes pride of place in the books for the young and is indeed a treasure trove of anecdote, verse, and , picture. Mention must also be made of the carbon and platino panel eerie, a variation of the ever- popular black and white panel, and, we think, even lan improvement on the old style, charming though it was. Finally there are the picture post cards, containing 2,000 separate design, and in connec- tion with which there is a .£l,OOO collector's prize competition which does not close until February 25th. 1902 in every respect Messrs Tuck have more than done themselves justice this year,special though it is, and, we have not the least doubt, that they will obtain the patronage they so eminently merit from a discriminating public.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>FAM!LIAR SYMPTOMS.</head>
        <p>FAM!LIAR SYMPTOMS.</p>
        <p>Cramp, sleeplessness, rheumatic pains, twitchings, shortness of breath and !blood disorders, are common symptoms of ono cause—failure of the kidneys, to act properly. Mr. Muer Green, of West Floughhain, Dover, had all these afflictions, together with pains in the loins, a stifling feeling in the he'd and throat, and blood so badly polluted that little hard spots formed on his face and body. After suffering for alc ng time he decided to adopt the natural treatment for expelling such diseases, and commenced to take Dodd's Kidney Pills. Dodd's Kidney Pills acted directly on the kidneys, and so puri- fied the blood. The waste sabstancas that were clogging the system and were responsible for the symptoms were removed tnrough nature's safely valve, the kidneys. which had hitherto f Ailed, in their duty and so caused ' the distressing troubles. It you have one or more of nese symptoms your kidneys are not doing their duty. If you would be well, see to the kidneys. If you would have the kidneys healthy, take Dodd's Kidney Pills, the only certain remedy for:weak and diseased kidneys. By their use your body will he fortified against the inroad of disease. Dodd's Kidney Pills are 2s 91 per box,of all Chemists. or post free on receipt of price by The Dodd's Medicine Co., 23, Farringdon Avenue, London, E.C. Remember the name, D-3-1-d-'s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>"Curiosities of Vision."</p>
        <p>This was the subject of the lecture delivered by Professor Stirling in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo- street, on Tuesday evening. It was usual, he said, to look at an object, with two eyes. Two distinct impressions were mad d on the retina but the brain recorded it as one. When two images were seen, there was no complete cb-ordination between the muscles and the eyeball, and this was responsible for double vision. When an image fell on the retina it did not follow that our juagment of the image was absolutely sound. We did not come into the world with a knowledge of size and distance ; this was acquired afterwards ; and therefore our judgment might be fallacious. A person might see something with his o vvn eyes and form a judgment upon it but still his judgment might be absolutely wrong. The lecturer proceeded to show by a series of optical illueians that our judgment of size, direction,movemene and colour contrast was wrong. During a fog, individuals seemed much larger to the eye than under ordinary circumstances. Then a brightly illuminated object looked larger than one whico was not illuminated although it was exactly the same size. The lecturer demonstrated this by placing two globes of the same size together. On lighting one up by means of a candle it ap- peared to the eye a little larger than the other. This was owing to the halo or irradiation which was produced. This irradiation was also percep— tible in the wearing of clothes. A portly lady would never think of advertising her bulkiness by dressing in white. White appeared to make the object larger whilst black hal the opposite effect and that was why black gloves were prefer- red to white. The lecturer humorously suggested that those who had large pedal extremities should stick to black if they wished to diminish the appar- ent size. The lecturer showed how vertical lines appeared to increase the size of an object whilst horizontal lines had an opposite effect. In speak- ing of colour contrast, the lecturer said that a particular colour varied according to the nature of its surroeedings. In proof of this, the lecturer gave several dernonscratious showing that colours changed in hue according to the colour of the back- ' ground. By revolving a disc coloured green and white a pink colour was produced although there was no pink abut it. Several other* optical illu- sions concluded an interesting lecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LITCHFORD 2ND V. SEFTON "B."</p>
        <p>At Latchford on Saturday in dense fog. The first half Was rather in favour of the home team, goali being scored by Smith, Spence, and Cliffe. S3fton played much better after the interval, and netted the ball on two occasion. Smith acain scored for Latchford. The final result of a very god game was a win for Latch- ford by four goals to two.</p>
      </div>
