      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>BER 22 1901</head>
        <p>BER 22 1901</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of this society onf,Sunday afternoon the chair was occupied by Mr T L Robins, of Warring- ton, who spoke briefly on the improvement of the mind. Mr A Perkins, of Warrington, delivered the address, the sutject of which was " Obedience." The service was also taken part in by Mr A Downes, Mr A Healey, and Mr B Farrar. The good attendance which characterises the mee!ings of the society was well maintained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Organ Recital at Stretton Church.</head>
        <p>Organ Recital at Stretton Church.</p>
        <p>A large congregation assembled in Stretton Church on Sunday afternoon, when an organ recital was given by. Mr Percy Randall (organist of Holy Trinity Church, Warrington), as well as a musical service by the choir, assisted by Mrs G Hoult, Mr G Parr, and Mr T Amos, of Warrington. The following was the programme:—Organ solo, " Song without Words," Mr Randall ; hymn 166, A. and M., " All people that on earth " ; solo, " The Holy City," Mrs Moult ; solo, " Consider the Lilies." Mr Parr ; organ solo, " Adeste Fidelis," (variations for different stops), Mr Randall ; anthem, " 0 worship the Ring," choir (solos by Messrs Parr and Amos) ; solo. " Abide with me," Mrs Hoult ; solo. "Nazareth," Mr T Amos ; organ solo, " Simple Avue," Mr Randall: hymn 391, A. and M , " Onward Christian Soldiers " ; Nunc Dimittis to chant by Barnby (kneeling); march, "Tann- hauser," Mr P Randall. All the items were effectively rendered, praise being due to all who took part in the service.</p>
        <p>Choir Festival at Bold-street Wesleyan Church.—Visit of the Rev. H. G.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Roberts.</head>
        <p>Roberts.</p>
        <p>There were large congregations at Bold-street Wes- leyan Church on Sunday when the annual choir festival services commenced. The preacher at both the morn- ing and evening services was the R:v B G Roberts, M.A., who recently left this circuit to take up an im- portant appointment at Leicester. Needless to say, the rev gentleman was heartily welcomed by his numerous I Warriogton friends. Special attention had been de- , voted to the musical portion of the services and from the capable manner in which the mem- bers of the choir rendetel the parts allotted to them, it was evident that no pains had been spared to attain their present efficiency. The choir was augmented by a number of wer-known artistes arid the music throughout the festival was all that could be desired. Sunday morning's service was commenced with an organ selection, " Sing ye praise,all ye redeem- ed of the Lord," by Mr Frank Hickman, the organist at the church. Mr S H Oilier and the choir then sang Sphor'a Sanctus. After hymns, psalms and prayer the choir sang Dr Dyke's Festival 're Deum in F in a very creditable manner. The Rev H G Roberts preached a sermon appropriate to the occasion from the words "It is a good tiling to give thank unto the Lord, and to sing praises unto Thy name.' After the sermon Miss Dora Gilpin, A.R.C.M., of Bir- mingham, who potsess a very sweet soprano voice gave a pleasing interpretation of " With verdure el td." The choir sung the Hallelujah chorus with precision and the service concluded with a voluntary "Allegro from Symphony v " (Widor) by Mr Hickman. In the after- noon an organ recital was given by Mr W H Payton, Mus. Sac. and miscellaneous iitems were contributed by other artistes. Mr John Taylor, presided and in the course of his remarks touched upon the importance of the musical service in the church being as bright as possible. Be expressed the hope that the choir would acquit themselves as well in the future as they had done in the part. The Warrinrton Male Voice Choir was in attendance, and under the conductorship of Mr S Hassell, sang " Perfect in Christ " and "The Emigrants " in an effective manner. Mr R H Pearson played two violin solos with his usual taste and skill, and Miss Doris Gilpin was heard to advantage in "0 let eternal honours crown His name " and "From mighty kings." The evening ser- vice opened with Sullivan's beautiful composition " The chorister " which was rendered by the junior choir. Mr S H Oilier and the choir again sang the Sanctus, and the anthem " Hear my prayer" was I rendered,Miss Gilpin taking the solo. Mr W H Walling- ton's excellent baritone voice was beard to advantage in "The Singers." Mr Hickman again uresided at the organ andf played Sullivan's "Andante Pastorale " and the march from " Tannhauser " in capital style. The Rev H G Roberts preached from the text "Put off thy shoes from off thy feet'. for the place on which thou standest is holy ground." The collections for the day amount to £2B 14s.</p>
        <p>Oia Monday evening the festival was brought to a close with a musical service. The congregation was not large, this no doubt being due to the wretched weather which prevailed. Mr R A Naylor had been announced to preside, but as he was Linable to attend the chair was occupied by Mr W. Silcock. The foil owing letter dated from Pwellheli, was received from Mr. Naylor, explaining the cause of his absence. "My dear sir,—Yours of November 9th. When I promised to preside at 3 our gathering on the lath it was conditional that we were at home at the time. Unfor- tunately, we have been compelled to close our house through a vile nuisance near—in fact while it exists the aeighbourhood is practically unfit to live in. You will, perhaps explain the cause of nay absence, as it would have given me great pleasure to have been present had this been possible." The principal feature of the pro- gramme was the cantata "Bless the Lord, 0 my soul " (Adams) which was very ably rendered. The cantata comprised the choruses " Bless the Lord, 0 my soul " and " All His works in all places bless the Lord " by the choir, solo (tenor) l" The Lord is merciful " Mr A Griffith., solo and chorus, " He will not always chide," Miss E Rawlings and the choir ; solo (baritone), " Llke as a father pitieth his children," Mr W H Wallington ; quartette, "As for man, his days are as grass." Misses Rawlinss and Lucy Davies and Messrs Griffiths and Wallington solo (soprano), " But the mercy of the Lord,"Miss Lucy Davies.—The Chairman then delivered a brief address in the course of which he said he would like to say in all honetty and sineerity,as a trustee of the church and as holding some official position, that they gratefully recognized the services of the choir who had so efficiently led the devotion of the church and had so ft ithfully served them for so many years. He thought the services of the church ought to be made beautiful, and if he had his way he would have the chapel decorated and lighted by electricity, and the old pews taken out. (Hear, hear.) He wondered whether they would say " Hear, hear," to the next remark. namely, that if he had his way he would have vases of beautiful flowers placed on the table every Sunday. (Hear, hear, and applause.) They could not do wrong In doing as the Master did, who took for his text the lilies of the field and told them to cast all their cares upon God. In the service of Ged they ought to have everything of the best. In the olden days, in the old Temple worship, the best music the nation could supply was introduced, and in their own service, however bumble it might he, whether in the barn or in the cathedral with its fretted roof and its long-irawn aisles, they ought to give the Almighty their best ; and if they did so in all honesty and sincerity His blessing would rest upon them. (Applause )—The second part of the programme was of a miscellaneous character. Miss Dora Gilpin gave a very pleasing rendering of " Voices of the Angels," for which she was heartily applauded. Later shn sang by request Liddle's beauti- ful " Ab:cle with me," in a manner which delighted her hearers. Mr WII Wallington sang " Cleansing Fires," and Mr Arthur Griffiths rendered "The promise Divine." Mr Frank Hickman played in a very effective manner Smith's "Chanson Busse." and Thomas's " Eatracte Mignon," and presided at the organ during the whole of the festival in a most praiseworthy manner, The total collections for Sunday and Monday amounted to £32 12s 31, and since the beginning of the week £137 has been promised in aid of the organ fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Foreign Missions.</head>
        <p>Foreign Missions.</p>
        <p>Anniversary Services at Wycliffe Church. At Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sunday, ser- vices in connection with the Foreign Missionary anni- versary were held, and collections taken in behalf of the Londou Missionary Society. The Pastor, Rev. John Yonge, preached at the morning servile, and based his discourse on Isaiah xal c., part of the 11 and 12 verses. In referring to the first centurs 's work of foreign mis- sions, he said sheen was progress throughout the mission field, and although progress was slow it V 7.18 sure. In 'connection with the London Missionary Society, during the past ten years the number of European ' missionaries had increased from 196 to 270, the medical miss-onaries having been doubled in number. The financial difficulties of the Society had arisen, not from any decrease of general contributions but fro:n the conetantly growing cost of the work itself. Last year the total income was £141,000 and the outlay £150,000 so that the actual deficiency was nearly £lO,OOO and it would have been very much greater if the receipts from legacies had not been more than usual. Alluding to the recent crisis in China, the preacher said there were some people who censured missionaries for any trouble which arose. It was therefore necessary to state clearly that the Chinese antipathy was directed, not against missionaries, but against the growing power of foreigners throughout the Chinese empire. Allusion was made to the hopefulness of mission work in China, Toe work Of the society in New Gninea where the society had recently lost two able missionaries by the massacre of Dr Chalmers and his assistant Tomkins, was also alluded to, In the afternoon, the Rev Charles Jukes, missionary from Madagascar, who has for 36 years been working on the island, addressed the scholars of the Sunday schools. He also preached in the church at night. The first part of the service, in consequence of Mr Jukes suffering from a cold, was taken by Mr George Wellington. Mr Jukes gave a very interesting account of missionary work in Madagascar, and stated that six years ago there were 1,409 Christian places of worship on the island, Since then, the labour of the missionaries had been disturbeJ. The occupation of the island by the French had resulted in the persecu- tion of the native protestants by the French Jesuit</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>• r 1 ' t; OGG • .4.,</head>
        <p>• r 1 ' t; OGG • .4.,</p>
        <p>kg% 4 . . 14 or I • rco „ • 1... t,</p>
        <p>priests. The whole subject, he continued, was an ex- tremely painful one. They in Madagascar had learnt what many foolish people in England seemed to have forgotten—that the spirit of the Inquisition was by no means dead and if the Jesuits had the power they would persecute to-lay as they did when they burned our protestant forefathers. Toe action of the French G)vernncent had brought about a much better state of affairs quite recently and many of the buildings which were taken from the Protestants had been restored and religious, liberty was now allowed.—Co'lections were taken on behalf of the London Missio,ary Society. On Tuesday evening the annual missionary meeting was held, the Rev John Yonge presiding. The speaker was the Rev Thomas Haines, missionary from Belgaurn, . .</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he wished to call attencien to one or two matters in connec:iln witn the work of the Lon- don Missionary Society in that district. He found that last year, from the report he held in his hand, and which was submitted t, the directors in May last, that Lymin, Widnes and Warrington had raised about £B3 among them—Lvmm, £6 3i.; Widnes, £5 7s , and the rest had ' been uollected in connection with that chinch. During the 25 years he had been connected with the church it had raised about £2,100 for the society. (Applause.) Mr R W Murray, who was at one time superintendent of the Sunday school, and who was very much interested in the London Missionary Society promised to double the amount raised from Sanday to Sunday at the end of the year. The challenge was taken up in such a rnag- ' nifioent style tint he (the Chairman) was rather dis- posed to think, Mr Murray was a great deal surprised, and was not quite prepared t 3 double such an amount but he rose to the occasion, and one' year he had to give £7O to redeem his pre- mise. Leaving out those " fat" years of the Society so far as Wycliffe was concerned, and the Centenary year when a sale of work was held on behalf of the Centenary fund, he had calculated that Wycliffe had raised on an average about Lfil a year for the Society. In conclusion he asked them whether they thought that a church which generally raised an income for all purposes of four figures each year, was quite properly represented in foreign missionary work by a donatbn of only two figures. He expreessd the hope that the amount of their contribution would be increased:</p>
        <p>At the outset of his interesting address the Rev Thomas Haines aid he was glad to give information about his own station because Warrington had sent to his part of that great country a missionary, viz., Mr Dignum. (Applause.) He knew him we 1, and could testify that he was carrying on a good work for :the Lord Jesus Christ, For 31 years he (the speaker) had been a missionary in India, 21 years at Belare and the remainder of the time at Belgaurn. Though many of the people were not yet Christians be was sure they (was very thankful for being brought under the inflaence of Christian truth. It was very difficult indeed to induce the bigti castes to become Christ- ians. They became atheistical and agnosticel The value of missionary schools was alluded to, the speaker pointing out that here the Hindoos came into contact with divine ideas. A brief reference was made to the fearful famine in Belare in 1877-8-9. He said the Government were very kind to the starving people in gifts of money and kind—a kindness which could never be forgotten. It was pointed out that many children rendered orphans by the famine came under the influence of the missionary society, that thus, while the famine was very trying, it was in another sense a sort of blessing to Christian missions. The speaker mentioned that there were many of the root ideas of Christianity in Hhadooism, and wherever they raw anything good the missionaries must be perfectly prepared to acknowledge th..t good, and then point to Jesus Christ and Christianity as the perfection and com- pletion of these root ideas. It was very important that they should have as missionaries the right sort of men, men properly educated,because they had to work among people who, in a great measure,were well educated, and it was necessary for them to have also tip spirit of Jesus Christ in them, and to be full of the Gospel. The F rEm ker., referred to the need of lady missionaries and speaking of the good that might ba done by working among the women natives, said that in India as well as in this Christian land, the women were more spiritually minded than;the men. Before they got India to be a Christian country they must work among the women. His work in Belgaum bad been of an evangelistic character. He had been in charge of three churches, and he found that it 'took a long time for these people to get rid of their</p>
        <p>heathen ideas. The fliadoos were very p 'tient. required only two meals a day—and they could not e get that—and when the famine came on they suffer the pangsrof hunger and never utter a cry. . were two other movements going on in addiel° Christianity; but they need have no fear. The iiiivA; results of Christianity were greater than the results, and he believed the time was coming when would be converted. There were some who vrisse have the English language spoken throughout ladisr, he saw no reason for that as the natives had rPle' language. What they wanted was to give the. Gospel pure and simple, and then they would foree,, a form of government of their own,and under that ' Christianity would be the religion of India. A. hymn having been sung and prayer offere, meeting =eluded. Mr Frank Hickman presided 11" organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>SOUTH LANCASHIRE VOLUNTEERS IN SOUTH AFRIC Captain Appleton's Book.</head>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASHIRE VOLUNTEERS IN SOUTH AFRIC Captain Appleton's Book.</p>
        <p>Next week Captain Appleton, the officer commiriaj the Active Service Company (Ist South Lancashir°'''. merit) which served in tho present war from Jac 1900, to Jurae• 1901, will publish a book giving 116 and highly interesting account of the domgs of Oa pany during that eventful period. The local Voltly joined their regiment in the neighbourhood of smith in time to take part in finally clearing the:enemy, and this work, as well as the subJel, eying work of guarding lines of communicatiet raphically described. The company contained 112, iterary talent and—as might be expected seeing CP was drawn from Lancashire—much dry hunanat,k the bock gives a true re&amp;ction of both. are hundreds of high-:lass illustrations, inc," photos of the engagement in Botha' teresting points in Natal, the Transvaal and the v.'o River Colony, and photos of each officer and mal3l, company and of the Third Contingent, still front. There is also a photo of the late O'Leary, killed at the moment of the relief of smith, and of his grave on Pieter's Hill. In factlo log has been omitted that was calculated to rt,. bock complete and worthy a place in the literattg` will rank in future as South Lancashire historl.„, book, which covers over 200 pages, is to be publioo</p>
        <p>, 33 61 and (better bound edition) at 53. the printed is limited, so that these who are de!</p>
        <p>securiog a copy snould send order and P.O. at Captain Appleton "Guardian " Office War</p>
        <p>Lieutenantlewis, Ditton Lodge, Widnes ; or Dutton, Cowley Hill, S. Helen!.</p>
