        <p>ST. PETER'S SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>Opening by Mrs. Douglas Buckton.</p>
        <p>A sale of work in aid of parochial organizations we s opened in.S t. Peter' s schoolroom ,John-street,on Thursd a y afternoon. The room had been tastefully decorated and the stalls were laden with an abundance of usef u and ornamental wares, beautiful flowers Ssz., many of whichlwere quickly disposed of. The following were th e stall-holders :—Vicarage stall, Mesdames Percival , Taylor, Stanley and Edgar, Misses Young and Worrell; Church Army stall, Captain Powell, C.A, Miss Cheanev , Mesdames Grounds and Tomkinson ; refreshment stall, Mesdames, Bracecamp, Bamfortb, Lirigley, Beesley and , Yeomans, and Misses Allen and Bancroft ; flower stall, ' Misses Jenks and Webster, and Mrs Clayton ; sweet stall, Misses Barlow and Lingley. The sale was opened by Mrs Douglas Buckton in the presence of a good attendance. Amongst those present were the Mayoress (Mrs J C Parr) and Miss Parr, Mrs Beamont, Mrs John White, the Rector (Rev Canon Willis), the Revs B Per- cival (Vicar), W Bracecamp (curate), E C E Carleton, W Pollock Hill, A S Cameron, and A F White, Major J D Buckton, Mr Charles White and the Misses White Mrs Willis, Mrs Carleton, Mrs Rolling worth, CouncilloriJohn Roberts, Mr Peter Edgar and Mr P Wells. In opening the proceedings the Vicar thanked the I friends from outside the parisk for their presence and support. He said the parish was a very large and a very poor one and they depended to a great ex- tent upon outside assistance. He thanked all who had in any way contributed to the success of the sale. Speaking of the object of the sale the rev gentlemen said they required a considerable amount of money, but if they raised as much by that sale of work as they had done by previous ones they would be very grateful and thankful. They had many funds to which the proceeds could be devoted. Two of the organiza- tions which required support were the Curate's Fund land the Lay Agent's Fund and these funds swallowed up something like £220 every year. He now called upon Mrs Buckton to open the sale. Mrs RUCKTON, who was cordially received. said she was glad to hear that there were to be no raffles at the sale, as this was a point upon which she was rather strong. She hoped the amount realized would be quite as large, in fact larger than they would have raised if they had had raffling. (Applause.) Councillor JOHN ROBERTS proposed a vote Of thanks to Mrs Buckton, and said the Buckton family had been very kind to that parish. They had a large number of outside friends and he did not know what they should have done if these friends had not rallied round them when they were in need of subscriptions. The RECTOR of Warrington seconded and said the parish of St Peter's had been greatly indebted to Mr Buckton and family for pecuniary assistance and advice. He was sure they all regretted the approaching depar- ture of Mr Bracecamp,who during Mr Percival's illness had worked indefatigably for the good of the Church. He thought the best way they could show their appreciation of his work would be to try and raise whatever funds were necessary to provide for a successor. He was delighted to know that they had a lay worker from Mr Carlile's band (the Church Army) in the parish. After express- ing appreciation of the work of the Church Army, the Rector concluded by saying that St. Peter's parish deserved all the help which he was sure they would try to give it. (Applause). The motion was :carried with acclamation and the business of the day commenced. _ . _ _ . _</p>
        <p>The proceeds for the day amounted to £67 5s 8d including subscriptions. The sale will be continued to-day at 3 p.m.</p>
      </div>
