      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Xttters to the attor.</head>
        <p>Xttters to the attor.</p>
        <p>Ruhcorn School Board Election.—An</p>
        <p>Awkward Situation.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Affairs are now reaching their climax. In any future history of education in Runcorn the first election el the Twentieth Century will be mensorable, and mark a distinct era in the fight between the rival systems of therNational and the Sectarian. The town is literally flooded with liteaature. Statement s are met by counter- statements. Meetings have been held nightly in various parts of the town. One set of candidates have made charges, and been met by counter-charges from their opponents. Amidst the babel the ratepayer must be in danger of losing himself in a wild-rness of words. The Progressive candidates have endeavoured to lift the subject fram its mere local surrounding and deal with it on the lines of the question, " Which is best for the child and for the nation." But in vain. The interests of the Church party are too purely sectarian and in this election too utterly parochial, that they can- not rise above the one idea of defeating their opt) 1- nents by any means that election tactics will allow. They have been compelled even to fall back upon their cry of three years ago of " Down with the rates," with- out even the qualifying "Keep," of which Canon Wood made so much use at his first meeting. The mis- representation of the co t of the transfer of the Wesleyan Schools has been trotted out again and again in order to compel the ratepayers to yield to fear, what the Church candidates are utterly unable to induce by real facts and fair argu- ments. But Nemesis in the shape of Mr Fred J Norman has overtaken the Canon and his "facts for voters," With painful iteration and re-iteration Canon Wood has declared ttmt the transfer of the Wesleyan schools added 4d to the rates. Argument and appeal were alike wasted upon him. To his guns he stuck like the Canon he is As Shakespeare has it, however, he has been brought to "pause " in his career of misrepresentation. And his financial fables have been exploded in this wise. Mr Norman wrote a letter to Canon Wood pointing out how unsatisfactory it was that two members of the same Board each-possessed of the authentic documents,should confuse the public with such conflicting statements. And concluded " I, therefore, propose that you name some Chartered Accountant of standing to whom we will jointly submit the official figures, and if he will certify the accuracy of your statement I will pay the cost of such reference and give £25 to the Cottage Hospital, while if you fail to obtain such a certificate you stand mulct in the same penalty." On the very evening of the day on which he received this challenge Canon Wood admitted that he was wrong to the extent of id in the £. Did he seek to justify his prc- tracted misrepresentation of: the facts ? N. Dia he apologise to those more nearly concerned ? Ng. He entered into a confusing jumble of figures, wrong on their basis and therefore misleading in their result. Unless Canon Wood either accepts Mr Norman's challenge or withdraws his statement, which be has already acknowledged to be not the fact, be will I should imagine forfeit the confidence of the ratepayers who will know what construction to put upon his atti- tude.—Yours etc.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>IMPERIALIST.</head>
        <p>IMPERIALIST.</p>
        <p>Raffling at Bazaars.</p>
        <p>Sir,—From letters— and conversations I have had anent my recent letter on the subject of nanny at bazaars held for religious objects—and I would have your readers understand it is against raffling under the is of religion that my criticism has been directed—l find that the rationale of my obiection has not been understood. Bat, as when more fully explained, it has been generally accepted I shall be obliged if you will kindly allow me space to further elucidate a matter the importance of which it would be difficult to over-estim- ate. For gambling, which an eminent judge opines " leads to vicious inclinations, destruction of morals, abandonment of industry and honest employment, a loss of self-control and respect," has now assumed alarming ' proportions. Its evil and its growth in our midst will, I think, be generally admitted. That it should be strenu- ously opposed by the Christian Church will also be conceded. But lam asked is raffling really gambling? Can anyone, with any regard for the laws of logic, deny it ? " Raffles and sweeps are illegal. being nothing more than lotteries "—and lotteries are part of that gambling which is defined as "the practice of playing for a money-stake, games depending solely on chance." (Chambers' Encyc. vo'. v., p. 73.) The late Archbishop Magee defined it as " the purchase of chances." It is impossible then to deny that raffling—even in its least harmful form—is gambling. But the relation of raffling to the grosser forms of this vine may be very much what the child's petty pilfering from the maternal cupboard to the grosser forms of theft. If this petty domesti picking were the only form of theft, one might thinkat pedantic—in view of 'other great evi's in the world—to inveigh unduly against it. And lam prepared to admit, that if the comparative harmlessness of the riffle were the oroy form of gambling prevalent, tour objection to it might be considered equally Puritanic and petty—at any rate ro long as really serious evils remained to be ' undermined. But what should we think of a Sunday school in these days of prevalent dishonesty, which encouraged children to pilfer from their mother's larders ? Just what many of U 9 ;think of religious organisations which,in these days of prevalent gambling, sanction and encoarage raffling !</p>
        <p>But ie gambling—apart from its dire effects—really wrong ? May it not be considered in the light of in- dnleence in alcoholic drinks ? To condemn all use of alcohol appears to me to be Manichceism ; moral evil does not reside in material things ; every creature of God is good in itself. True. I am a total abstainer from alcoholic drinks as beverages, but on quite other grounds than those on which I am a total abstainer from every form and degree of gambling. It may not he the duty of every one to be an abstainer from alcohol. It is the duty of every one to be an abstainer from gambling. For wherein lies the sin of gambling ? I have seen many attempts' to answer this question. Perhaps that cf Herbert Spencer and the late Bishop Westcott—the two are practically identical—is, until re- cently, the most successful. The Christian Bishop and the Agnostic philosopher condemned gambling (1) be- cause the benefit received does not imply any equiva- lent effort put forth by the receiver, (2) because noth- ing has been added to the common weal, and (3, because the happiness of the winner involves the misery of the loser. But can we be quite satisfied with this—true as it undoubtedly is ? For (1) and (2) con- demn many a gift or the benevolent ; while what can be raid concerning (31 when two persons agree before- hand to accept the risk, and where the unhappiness is not evident ? This criticism I have always felt to be just. But recently—bow recently Ido not know—a much more satisfactory attempt has been made. Who is its author Ido not know and should be glad to be informed. To transfer property by chance is to deny the control of reason and conscience in the act, just as (the sin of intoxication apart altogether from its evil effects, consists in the suspension of reason in all acts. I have received ro little criticism on this point. But Ido not say that it is necessarily wrong to suspend the control of reason. Otherwise, sleep antestheties, intuitive or reflex acticn, and the " tow-op "of cricket elevens would stand con- demned. But every transfer of property involves moral obligation. To allow chance to be the sole or determin- ing principle in such transfer is to attempt to act out- side the scope of the moral law in that sphere where moral law must have its application. Chance' is un- moral, but to appeal to un-moral chance instead of to conscience 'where coascieace should 7,e heard is immoral. This is why gambliug can be classed with intoxication Both are immoral although nossibly not to the same degree. And this becomes the more apparent if we consider a very profound and noble definition of pro- perty given by a prominent Roman Catholic in America. " Property " says Dr Browne, "is communion with God in material things." If "the earth is the Lord's and the fulness thereof" if He is the absolute owner of everything, then man is in the posi- tion of a trustee. How dare he then stake God's property on the throw of dice or the drawing of a lot ? How dare he exclude the moral law from the transfer of property or its trusteeship and allow it to be determined by chance and luck ? The Church used to utter no uncertain voice in thie matter. At the Council of Eliberie it was decreed that "a Christian playing at dice or tables is not to be admitted to Holy Communion, but after a year's penance and abstention and his total amendment." We English Catholics are not one whit behind our Roman Catholic friends in regard for the great S. Cyprian, who eaid that "a common gamester may call himself a Christian, but he is not one." But what would a common gamester of Carthage have said had be seen the Archbishop presiding at a roulette table to provide himself with an Episcopal throne for his cathedral. What would he have said if he had been asked by the Archbishop himself to put in a lottery for a case of wine of Setia, Calea or Falerne ? Would be not have't,ewn tempted to say, Sir, you are a humbug! Raffling at a cricket Club b; zaar is wrong, but I shall leave protest for others there. A cricket club doee not exist to ostensibly further the cause of social righteousness. But the whole raison d'etre of a religious organisation at any rate where religion and morality are fused into one homogenous whole as should be in the case of any with any pretension whatever to the Christian name—is gone unless it'is engaged in com- bating those works of toe devil to destroy which the Son of God was manifested. I honour the Nonconformist conscience in being in the van in its attitude towards great' Social and national sins. Happily a more Sensi- tive Church conscience is being rapidly formed in these matter., as may be seen in the recent utterances of the Bishop of Liverpool and the Rector of Wigan on this very subject of raffling at religious bazaars- With the latter I most cordially agree and will conclude. "I do not think for a moment that all people who put into raffles in bazaars are moved by the gambling spirit, but it is not fitting that in • Church bazaar there should be encourage or lead anyone ianntyothtbinagt habitat coofugld possibly ambling which is at the present time One of the great curses of the country.—yours FRANCIS E. POWELL. St. James' Vicarage, Warrington, November 12th, 1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY NOVEMBER 22, 1901</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY NOVEMBER 22, 1901</p>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>Rumoured Assassination of</p>
        <p>the Queen of Servia.</p>
        <p>A Denial.</p>
        <p>A Reuter's telegram from Vienna states that a Servian official denial is given to the rumour that Queen Drage had been assassinated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>HUDDERSFIELD LADY</head>
        <p>A HUDDERSFIELD LADY</p>
        <p>COMPLETELY CURED OF CHRONIC INDIGESTION AND ANAEMIA, BY VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC. INDIGESTION AND ANAEMIA CORRECTLY EXPLAINED.</p>
        <p>Indigestion is the result of a weak stomach. The symptoms are,pain and fulness after eating, with a sore and tender feeling at the pit of the stomach and fre- quentiv pain under the heart, caused by wind on the stomach, consequent upon the food not being properly digested ; it sours and ferments. This condition toget- her with other causes, produces amernia. When your fool is properly digested, it assimilates and nourishes the body, makes blood, and gives health, and vitality, If it does not digest properly you become pale, thin, weak, languid, and unfit fer work ; suffering from headaches, poor appetite and sleeplessness. Veno's Seaweed Tonic strengthens the stomach and cures indigestion. It enables you to eat and digest your food hence you get strong. It produces natural refreshing sleep, clear the head and braiu and invigorates the whole system.</p>
        <p>A WONDERFUL CURE. AFTER DOCTORS FAILED.</p>
        <p>Mi 39 M. F. Haigh, Houses Hill, Kirkeaton, near Huddersfield. writes January 17th, to Mr. Veno.—l suffered terribly from indigestion for over five years. I had it so bad that it caused me sometimes to have colic and neuralgia at the stomach. I have always been under the doctors, and had to give no my employment. Sometimes I felt alright when taking the doctor's medicine, but as soon as I stopped taking it I would be as bad again. I never had above a week or two of pleasure until a friend told me of your Seaweed Tonic. I was so bad then that I could scarcely put one foot be- fore the other. On September 10th I got a small bottle 1 of your Seaweed Tonic, and I felt it had done me good. I then began to get large bottles. I have taken four altogether. I have had no need to take any since De- cember 21st, far which lam very glad. I think I never felt better in my life ; thanks to your Seaweed Tonic. I cannot praise it enough. I told a friend of mine about it, and it is doing her good. VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC _ _</p>
        <p>quickly and permanently cures all ailments arising from a diseased condition of the stomach, liver, kidneys, and blood. It is a veritable life invigorator, has marvellous tonic and strengthening power, and is the natural cure for constipation. You will feel better after the first dose. Price le 14c1 and 23 91 Caution :—When you ask for Veno's Seaweed Tonic or Veno's Lightning Cough Cure, see that you get it. Many dealers are in the habit of trying to coax you to take a cheap mixture of their own. You will do well to avid their shop. Go where you can get what you ask for. See that the name" Veno " is blown in every bottle. Sold by Boots Ltd., Warrington ; Peaks, Earlestown ; and Newton ; Weston and Co., Runcorn ; Wallbridge, chemist ; J W Jackson, chemists and druggists, S 5 Helens, and all chemists and medicine vendors every- where.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>---46.--- RUNCORN BOARD OF</head>
        <p>---46.--- RUNCORN BOARD OF</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above authority was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday. Present Mr A R Norman J.P., C.C., in the chair Messrs. I' Lea, T Reynolds, T Wood,J Douglas, the members of the Rural District Council with Mr W E Hough (assistant clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>TENDERS FOR THE FIRE -ESCAPES.</head>
        <p>THE TENDERS FOR THE FIRE -ESCAPES.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr T Wright;it was de-c.ided that the tender of the Iron Foundry Company Glasgow should be accepted for the erection of the fire escapes at a cost of £163 16!. .FIRE ESCAPES.</p>
        <p>- At the last meeting of the Board Mr J Moseley- Williams gave notice that he would move that the Guardians consent (1) to the erection at tie Workhouse of the Union of fire escapee in accordance with plans to be approved by the Local Government Board ; (2) to the expenditure for the purpose of £2,000 ; and (3) to the borrowing ef or part of the gum that may be necessary, the repayment with interest to be secured by a charge on the common fund of the Union ' • and (4) that the said sum be repaid by such equal half-yearly payments as reckoning principal and ioter6st together will repay the sum borrowed in twenty years. Admiral Jones Parry protested against the spending of the money, but he was not prepared to move an amend- ment to the resolution.</p>
        <p>Mr Limiter pointed out that Mr Dansey, the Local Government Board Inspector, knew more than the Council about the matter, and the escapes wore the result of his recommendation. The motion was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>EDUCATION OF WORKHOUSE CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION OF WORKHOUSE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Mr J Moseley-Williams moved that Eiizsbeth, Annie and Mice Edam be sent to one of the Branches of the Children's) Home and Orphanage for training at a cDst of 53 per week each. Mr Roylance seconded. _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr Talbot said he could not see why thrAe children should b 3 singled out of the house to confer a favour upon them by sending them away. Mr Woodward E t rong y supported the resolution. They were Wesleyan children and!would be sent to a Wesleyan home. A sum of 51 was not at all excessive. They paidlas much as 63 to,send Roman Catholic children away. Mr Linaker believed in sending the children away from the Workhouse. It was a step in the right direc- tion.</p>
        <p>---The Chairman said that the Wesleyans intended to devote either £50,000 or £lOO,OOO to the education of the Wesleyan children at present in the Union. They would all be taken out of the Unions some time next year and put into schools and this would save the rate- payers a certain amount of expense. The motion was passed. FINANCIAL STATEMENT. Mr Woodward reported that the payments for out- relief were : Runcorn £3O. Frodsham £22, The statement was approved. GIFTS OF BOORS.</p>
        <p>The master reported gifts of-boOks for the inmstes from Mr. Lswton, Moore Mr Spruce, Preston-'ti. Hill, and Mrs. Fletcher, Whitley.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS _FARE FOR THE INMATES.</p>
        <p>The Huse Committee recommended that the usual Christmas fare be provided for the inmates consisting of roast beef and mineral water for adults and half pint of milk for each child and bread and butter, currant and seed bread, tea and coffee for supper with the addi- tion of tobacco for smokers and oranges and sweets for the children.</p>
        <p>Mr Moseley Williams in moving the adoption of the minutes said that Mr Wood had promienl to send a mince pie to each of the inmates. Passed. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>MEETING OF RECHABIT'ES.—The annual tea and public meeting in connection with the " Sunbeam " tent of the Order of Rechabites were held in Ebenezer Chapel on Monday evening, when upwards of 100 members and friends were present. After tea, an entertainment was held in the chapel, under the presidency of Bro Sher- burne, who was supported on the platform by Bros W Dutton (Norley), the Rev J S Hughes, and W Wright (Warrington).—Bro William Spensley, T. 9., in s brief repart, stated that the " Sunbeam " tent, which is a branch of the Warrington Consolidated District, 1.0. R„ was instituted in August, 1393, and its membership has been steadily increasing up to the present time. Bro William Dutton (Norl ly), bettor known as 1" The Teetotal Bishop of Mid-Cheshire" (be having been instrumental in forming upwards of 60 branches of the Independent Order of Rechabites in Cheshire) delivered an address, full of pleasant reminis- cences of the early days of the movement. The vener- able brother, who is over 70 years of age afterwards sang, together with Mrs Dutton, " There is a better day , coming "and "The old family Bible." Bra Rev J s Hughes spoke of his long connection with the order, and expressed a desire that all young men should attach 11 themselves to that institution, the object of which was] to promote thrift and sobriety. A good programme has also been provided, consisting of instrumental selections violin and mandoline by Misses Asbbrook and Broom. bead, songs by Miss M Ross (Warrington), Miss Moss and Bro George MO9/1, and a humorous recital by Bro George Whitfield, also a dialogue by members of the juvenile tent, ea th item being well given. On the pro- , position of Mr Roylance (Moore), seconded by Bra Joseph Moss, CR., a vote of thanks was given to the chairman, speakers, artistes,and teamakers,bringing to a close a successful gathering Mrs Hughes and Miss Ross accompanied the various musical items. The GLEN-SPEY DISTILLERY. Situate in the finest Whisky-producing District of Scotland. is the property W..t A. Gilhey. This Whisky is made entirely from borne-grown Barley and is kept absolutely, unblended In his Majesty's Bonded Warehouses to mature, and t sold by W. .t A. Qilbey's agents in .every town a 35, ed per bottle,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A HAYDOCK COLLIER.</p>
        <p>Patrick Casey, a collier, Haydock, was charged at the Warrington Oczasional Court on Monday, be- fore Mr John White, with woundir g his wife, Eliza- beth Casey,on November 9th,by striking her on the head with a basin with intent to do her some grievous bodily harm. Evidence having been given to the effect that complainant was unable to atend the Court, Casey was remanded for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>POLICE CASES.</head>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>• At the Newton Police Court- on Friday morning, before Mr T J Gillespie, James Mitchell and Thomas Swift, both of no address, were charged with being drunk at Earlestown on Thursday, and were each sent to gaol for seven days in default of paying a fine of 23 6d and costs. On Monday at the same court, John Grabwell, Houghton-street,was charged with being drunk at Newton on Sunday and was fined 23 6d and costs. John Gavin, for a similar offence in Earlestown on Saturday was fined 2s 6d and costs.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER CITY MISSION, EARLESTOWN BRANCH.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Brunswick-street,Mission Hall, a pleasant Saturday evening was held in the Hall, when a varied concert was given to a large audience. The chief items on the programme were a number of phonographic selections by Mr W Dalton. These were most interesting and were enthusiastically received by the audience. Band selections, cornet, banjo, piccolo and vocal selec- tions were reproduced and heartily applauded. Songs were also given by Miss Corlett and Mr S Bring. GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The Hope of Earlestown Lodge meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, Wargravy-road, on Mon- day evening. Bro Williamson, PC T, presiding. One member was readmitted and 12 candidates were proposed for initiation. A number of visitors were present from the Unity Lodge, Leigh, and were received with honours. Bro Byland (Leigh) then presided, and the visitors provided the pro- gramme :—Address by Bro Rylands • recitation 'The Gate Keeper's Story," Sis Mrs Bright ; song Bro Rylands ; song, Bro Waterworn ; song, Sis Bylands ; song, Sis Bright. Afterwards all pro- ceeded to enjoy a more substantial programme in the form of a potatoe pie supper, provided by Sisters Woods, Grant, Hatton, and Singleton, to which ample justice was done.—A vote of thanks to the visitors, to which Brother Byland respon- ded, and proposed a vote of thanks to Earlestown brethren for their hospitality, which was heartily accorded by the visitors, brought a pleasant meet- ing to a close. WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONARY MEETING, EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting in aid of the Wesleyan For- eign Missionary Society, was held in the Wesleyan Chapel, Earlestown on Mondey evening. Mr G Burgess presided,and he was supported by the Revs R M Gawtrey, E C Bocock, and C E Dove. There was only a poor audience due no doubt to the in- clemency of the weather. The Rev C E Dove read the annual report of the society. He said the total income of the Wesleyan Foreign Missionary Society for the year 1900, including the balance of £l9 carried forward from last year, legacies Szc.., was £135,944, an increase on last year of .£1,804. The expenditure was £972 more than the income. From the Transvaal alone the society had lost in income between £5,000 and £6,000. Earlestown had con- tributed by collections from public meetings and Sunday services, .£8 103 3i, subscriptions, .£G 103 8d and from the Juvenile Association ..£l2 is 21, mak- ing a total of £27 2s id. Addresses were given by the Revs E C Bocock and R M Gawtrey, who had been a missionary at Buluwayo. EARLRSTOWN THURSDAY A.F.C.</p>
        <p>The fourth annual ball in connection with the above football club was held in the Town Hall,l Earle3town, on Wednesday night. The room was well filled, about 50 couples being present when the dancing commenced at 845 to the strains of Mr ' Frank Cunliffa's (St Helens) Band, Mr and Mrs George Owen leading off. The leading feature in the dancing was the " silveraine " dance, which was introduced towards the close of the programme and consisted of showers of silver rain falling from the top of the building on to the dancers. Twenty- one dances were negotiated, the proceedings termi- nating shortly after 3 a.m. The M.C.'s were Messrs H H Duke, H Walton, E Smith and L E Forster and t`hey effectually carried out their portion of the programme. The room was magnificently decorated by Mr Arthur Clarke and the catering was under. taken by Mr George Edwards, "Ship" Inn. The whole of the arrangements were carried out by a committee consisting of Messrs Pritchard (secre- tary), Dukes, Emerson, Singleton,Birkhead,Bennett, Goodburn, Morriss, Eastwood, Naylor, Galloway, Byrne, Barrow, Hodnett, Smith, Latham, Littler and Brown, EARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The 164th quarterly meeting of this society was held in the Co-operative Hall, on Monday evening. Mr D Forrester presided, and there was a record attendance of members, the ball being crowded. The report and statement of accounts showed the cash receipts to be £10,911 23 21 being an increase over last quarter of £175 19s 10d, and compared with the corresponding period last year. showed an increase7of £1,262 15s 3d. The net profits realised were .£1,681143 4A,d,which were disposed of by pay- ing's:dividend off 33 in the pound absorbing £1,656 adding £lO to the Workmen's Compensation fund, and the balance £l5 149 4id to the Contingent fund. During the quarter 51 new members had been admitted and 26 had withdrawn, leaving 1728 on the register, of whom 1311 had brought in checks. The committee, in their report, stated that no less than four events of a historic char- acter had trantpired during the quarter, each of considerable importance to the society, 'viz., open- ing of new stables, and new No. 3 branch shop, the completion of ten new cottages, and milk dealing successfully introduced as a new branch of busi- ness. The report was adopted. Messrs J G Mullins and J Owen were elected to the Committee of Management and Mr D Forrester was re-nominated for the position of President. Mr J Judson was appointed Delegate to the Wholesale Society's quarterly meeting. A donation of .£5 Sc was voted to the Southport Convalescent Home, and £3 3s to the Royal Infirmary. Manchester. This was all the business. DROWNING FATALITY AT EARLRSTOWN.</p>
        <p>... -- During the dense fog on Saturday eveninz a man named Michael Bryan, 40 years of age, of Hindley- road, Burtonwood, fell into the Sankey Canal at Earlestown, and although he was taken from the water alive, he subsequently died from the immer- sion. The deceased called at the Ship Inn,adjoining the Canal, about 8 o'clock, and asked to be supplied with a glass of whiskey. The landlady,Mrs Edwards, refused to supply him, owing to his condition, and ealledlher husband,Cwho requested Bryan to leave the house and go home. A few minutes later a man named Michael Queen heard a splash in the lock of the canal. He went into the Ship Inn and inform- Mr Edwards that someone had fallen into the water. Edwards and Queen, with the assistance of another man named Ratcliffe, procured a rake and I managed to get the deceased out of the water. Artificial respiration was commenced by Edwards and Ratcliffe, and Bryan was apparently brought round. Some time later,however, he had a relapse, and Dr Mouncey was sent for, but death took place at 10 o'clock before he arrived.—The iniuest was held at the Town Hall. Ear!estown on Wednesday morning, by Mr S Brighouse, coroner.—Bernard Bryan, brother of deceased, gave evidence of identi- fication and stated the deceased was 40 years old. Evidence was also given by Mr G Edwards,landlord of the Ship Inn, bearing out the above facts, and a verdict of " Accidental death," was returned, the jury adding a recommendation that the locks should be fenced.</p>
        <p>Spratt's Patent, Limited, have just placed on the market a new poultry spice. This preparation is in- tended for miring with the soft food, such as Spratt's patent poultry meal, at the morning feed. Ic no only assists the laying power of hens, but is very beneficial for chickens t ) fortify them against cold and damp in our variable climate. It can be had in Id., 3d., and 6i. packets.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER Tmz MOON POR BEST BOOTS IN LANOA SEUNB.—Adyt</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFT TONIC Is THE KING OF TONICS —63, SANKEY-STREET, WarrintOn.—(&amp;dvt.)</p>
        <p>BEST BOOTS nit ALL.—UNDER TEE CLOCK.—Ad w. T. WILLIAMS.—SPECIALIsT in MILLlNliltlt, DRBSSES and MANTLES.-74, SANKEy-STREET, and 1, SANICEY.STBEET, WarringlOn.</p>
        <p>STRANGR'S Al. CRYSTAL OIL, the beet &amp; safest Tamp oll.—J, S. GARLAND. 51 to 55 Bridge St, —Advt. ANTIQUE Wrought IRON and COPPER WARE. for wedding presents, eta—GARLAND, Bridge St,—Advt.</p>
        <p>TINDER THE CLOCK FOR GOOD BOOTS.—Adv TABLE, FLOOR, and RANGING LAMPS of every iescriptlon.—J S ISABLAlTD,lronmoageraßrldge St,Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDHNT.I</p>
        <p>Drowning cages in the canal at Newton Cammon locks, are becoming much too frequent, and it is a pity some means cannot be devised to prevent them. Not many weeks, since a man walked into the locks, and was only rescued after some con- siderable time, and when he was almost exhausted through clinging to the lock gates to keep his head above watcor. On Saturday night, during the fog another man walked into the locks at precisely the same point, and although be was got out alive, he died an hour or two afterwarde. In botla cases the men were intending to cross the canal by the swing bridge, but failing to locate the bridge,stepped into the water. The locks are utterly unprotected, and in the darkness, it is the easiest thing imaginable to walk into the water instead of on to the bridge No doubt it would be a difficult matter tn fence the 'sides of the locks, but surely something could be done to obviate the possibility of persons losing their lives in this fashion.</p>
        <p>The Hospital Scheme must surely have come t_ an untimely end. Nothing whatever has bee' heard of it for some time past, and altnoughn understand the Ladies' Committee are working hard for the projected bazaar in aid of the scheme, no steps appear to be taken by the Slale Committee, Some time ago a draft circular was submitted to the committee for approval, in order that it might be printei arid issued to the public, setting forth the aims and objects of the scheme, and although it was considered and one or two amendments sug- gested,no further move has been made. Lam think- ing another Town's meeting will ba necessary to form a thoroughly energetic and interested com- mittee, if the idea is to bear fruit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>FOOTBALL,</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL,</p>
        <p>The fog reigned supreme in thl3 neighbourhood on Saturday, consequently there was no football. Earlestown had arranged a friendly with Wigan United on the letters' ground. but it had to be can- celled, Tomorrow they should have made a journey to Workington to play a League fixture but Workington are down to play Southport Central in an English Cup tie, so that in all probability the local eleven will again have a day off. It is hoped, however, they will be able to fix up a match, in order to keep the team in form.</p>
        <p>Newton should have a visit from Bangor, to-morrow to play their combination fixture, and should the day be fine there will certainly be an excellent game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>ST. MARY'S AMATEUR DRAMATIC</head>
        <p>ST. MARY'S AMATEUR DRAMATIC</p>
        <p>SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Performances in Aid of the Nursing Association.</p>
        <p>With the laudable object of assisting the funds of the Warrington and District Nursing Associa- tion, the Amateur Dramatic Society in connection with St. Mary's Catholic Church has this week given three performances in Parr Hall, on the even- ings of Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday, to large audiences. Parr Hall does not lend itself easily to adaptation for stage plays, but the St. Mary's Amateurs have had some experience of the difficul- ties, and know well how to overcome them, as was evidenced by the way the pieces performed this weak were presented. The piece de resistance was " The Courier of Lyons," better known as "The Lyons Mail," a drama still regularly included in the repertoire of Sir Henry Irving and his Lyceum company. St. Mary's Amateur Dramatic Society have always aimed high, and do not seem content with the round of plays generally selected for amateur performances. %%e will not go so far as to say their" vaulting ambition o'er-reaches itself," but we do think that more modest aspira- tions would become them better and at the same time conduce to a better performance in the main of whatever was undertaken. 8t Mary's is but a young society and yet it essays tasks which would daunt the members of similar organisations with a record extending over a quarter of a century It would not be true were we to say that the society gave an adequate representation of " The Courier of Lyons." Few amateur societies could, any more than they could tackle "Robespierre " with any prospect of success from a histrionic standpoint. But disabusing one's mind of the critical feelings engendered by so ambitious a selection, we can honestly and sincerely say that the society pre- sented " The Crairier of Lyons" in a manner which did them infinite credit and which afforded a large amount of pleasure to the audiences nightly assembled, though the fact still remains that they would probably have performed something less exacting very much better. The following is the cast of " The Courier of Lyons." _ .</p>
        <p>Jerom Lesurques (the Innkeeper of Luisanb) Mr S RTIN Joseph Lesurques (his son) } W KILDUFF Dubose (the Assassin) Didier (Betrothed to Julie Lesurques) .........MR 0 NEWTON Lambert t Friends of Lesurques and t MR T JOHNSON Guerneau ( Brother Collegians of Courriol MR J N VERNON Jollquet (Walter at the Ina or Luisant) M.L9T&amp;R KILDUFF Danuenton (a Magistrate) Mr F V ECZERSLET ChOppard klorsedealer, alias '' The Irresistible "</p>
        <p>Mr G NEWTOif Courriol (a Fashionable) MR A BATE Foulnard (alias' The Chicken ”) MR H BOYLANCE Dumont (a Courier) MR J SUDELL Magloire (a Postilion) Mn J GLEESON Postmaster MR J GATLEY Ostler MitE BENEGHAN Officer MR J NEWTS Jeanne MISS AGNES KILDUFF Julie Lesurques (Betrothed to Didier) Miss CISSIE BUTtROW3 Louise (the Postmaster's Daughter) MISS KATE BROWNE Genuarmes MESSRS. CROSS &amp; SUDELL There must have been some difficulty in allotting twenty parts, but from the material at its command the Society did well in this respect. The principal character is a dual one, that of Joseph Lesurquea, and Dubose, between whom there is a striking re- semblance which forms the foundation for the drama and the events pourtrayed therein. Mr W Kilduff essayed the heavy task of wearing Irving's mantle, and, avoiding the obvious comparison, it must be admitted that he performed the doubly- exacting role' exceedingly well. He contrived to keep Lesurques and Dubose separate in voice and gesture, while at the same time he maintained the resemblance between them so as to dumbfound even the audience at times. He was best, in our opinion, as the rogue and assassin, Dubose, whose drunken ecstacies over the approaching execution of Les- urques for his own crime were finely pourtrayed in the final scene. The least convincing scene was the one in Act 111., when the magisterial examination takes place into the circumstances at- tending the murder of the Courier of Lyons, and when damning admissions are made apparently im- plicating the innocent Lesurques. There was:a lack of proper consternation on the part of the guilty, Mr A Bate as Courriol excepted, and a want of righteous indignation on the part of the innocent, which detracted from the impressiveness of the act. Mr Ryan made a good innkeeper of Luisant though rather too young looking for the father of Lesur- ques and grandfather of Julie. In the latter part Miss Cissie Burrows acted with taste and feeling, tb.:Jugh at times rather too subdued. As Jeanne, Dubose's victim, Miss Agnes Kilduff was convinc- ingly dramatic and bad one or two fine oppor- tunities which she made the most of. Choppard and Fouinard were a couple of grotesque scoun- drels whose eccentricities were fully brought out by Messrs G Newton and H Roy- lance. The other conspirator, Courriol, was cleverly represented by Mr A Bate who both looked and acted his part well throughout. Mr Eckersley made a good magistrate and praise is due to Master Kilduff for his lively pourtrayal o Joliquet, the waiter at the inn of Luisant. The other numerous characters were all well-sustained and the six scenes staged in a manner which con- veyed an excellent idea of the place and period of the story. Prior to the " Courier of Lyons " the humorous sketch "Damon and Pythias" was given as a curtain-raiser, the parts being well-taken by the Misses Delia Gleeson and Fanny Hayes, and Messrs Roylance, Kilduff, S Ryan and G Newton. The adventures of the two cousins created considerable amusement and formed a fitting prelude to the sterner play which followed. selections of music were played at intervals by an orchestra under the conductorship of Mr P Walsh, while the onerous duties of stage manager were performed by Mr W F Burrows ; of property master by Mr L Burrows ; of limelight manipulator by Mr J L Kertland. The hon. secretaries, Messrs M G Hearne and E A Connor are also entitled to ' commendation for the success which has attended their indefatigable efforts.</p>
