      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTFUCT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTFUCT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Council was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday :—Present : Mr J Knowles (in the chair), Mr r Woodward (vice-chair- man), Admiral Jones-Parry, Messrs T Wright, J Moseley Williams, C E Linaker, A El Talbot, C Roylance, J Bolshaw, J Spruce, bit El Bradford, G Pilling, J Whitlow, J Horton, E Bretberton, T Antwiss, T Baxter. E Clarke, J Mountield, R Billiagton, with Messrs W E Hough (assistant clerk), W Swinton and W Diggle (surveyors ) and J Farrington (nuisance inspector), and Dr Adam (medical officer of health).</p>
        <p>, - WHO PAYS? A QUEiTION OF RE MONSIBILITY.</p>
        <p>_ Local qovernment Board, Whitehall, S.W. 12th November 1901. Sir, I am directed by the Local Government Board to advert to your letter of the 26,h ult, with reference to the proposal of the Rural District Council of Runcorn to pay the sum of £3 123 41 in res- pect of expenses incurred in connection with the nursing of a case of enteric fever in the patient's own home, and to state that the Board have considered representa- tions contained in your letter, but that they must adhere to the decision already' conveyed to the District Council. lam, sir, your obedient servant Thomas Pitt, assistant secretary. The Clerk to the Rural District Council of Runcorn.</p>
        <p>It was decided to leave the rnatt.tr in the hands of the auditor and abide by his decision.</p>
        <p>THE WESTON ROAD. Mr Reginald Potts, clerk to the Cheshire County Council, wrote under date November 14th, to Mr Ash- ton, the clerk. as follows :—"lt,ferring to the applica- tion of your District Council to have the highway from the lopundary of the Runcorn Rural District to the Cross in the Parish of Weston (length 5 furlongs 140 yards) declared a main road, and to the inspection by a sat-committee of this Council of such highway on Wed- nesday 18-h S,ptember !as, I have to inform you that in the opinion of this Couocil there was no probable cause for nuch application and they, therefore, declined the same."</p>
        <p>The Clerk said that the matter had already been dealt with in committee. OBSTRt7CTING A FOOTPATH. - .</p>
        <p>Mr Reginald Potts, clerk to the Count--; Council,vrrete informing the Rural Council that the Parish Council of Moore had representel to them that a public footpath in that township had been obstructed, and that the Dis- trict Council had refused or failed to take proceedings hi the m tttsr.B4ore bringing suchlrepresent itions under the notice of the County Council he asked to be furnished with the views of the District Council. A further letter from Mr Potts was received, acknowledg- ing a letter from the clerk, and stating that he would be glad to know the result of correspondence with the Railway Company as to the removal of the obstruction. The Clerk said that the matter was still under consideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-11-22_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>RUNCORN VOLUNTEER CONCERT AND PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN VOLUNTEER CONCERT AND PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>On Friday evening the annual concert and prize dis- tribution in connection with the Runcorn Volunteers took place in the Drill Hall. There was a crowded audience.</p>
        <p>During the interval in the concert the prizes were dis- tributed by Mra. Maitland Wood. Detachment Prizes.-7 shots each at 200, 500 and 600 yards: Scores—Colour-Sergeant W 11 Harvey, score 79. prize £3; Corporal J Mort 74, £2 12s 6 Sergeant J H Rushton 74, £2 Os.; Copporal j Houghton 74 £1 176 6d; Lance- Sergeant F J Edwards 74, £1 10s. ; Private W Glegory 72, £1 Os; Sergeant W B Cooper, 71, £1; Lance-Sergeant J Gittens 70, 174 63 ; Private T P Mort 69, 154 ; Private S Joyneon 69, 12a ; Colour-Sergeant J Mort 68. 12s 63 ; Private 0 Garratt 63, 123 6d; Corporal J Oultram 63, 15$ ; Lance-Corporal T Hedgcock 62, 108 ; Sergeant W it Phoenix 59, 103 ; Private T A. Jones 58, 7e 63; Private J W Hulse 57, 7s 63; Private L J Hughes 53, be; Sergeant S D Matthews 55. 54 ; Lance-Corporal J Sadler 54, Os; Private W Outh- watt° 53, be ; Quartermaster J Poole 51, sa; Corporal J Marsh 51, 5s ; Corporal F Platt 60, Os; Private T Chamber- lain 49, 5s ; Private J Hargreaves 48, 5s ' - Private W Freeman 47, se; Lance Corporal C Glover 47, 5s ; Private H Gibbs 45, Se; Sergeant 83 Jeacock 44, 53._</p>
        <p>- Drill 'Coinpetaion.—F Company Lance-Sergeant Gittine, shooting and attendance, 641, prize ; Corporal J Mort 631, £1 153 ; Colour-Sergeant 3 Mort 611, £1 105 ; Ser- geant J Rughton 61. £1 53 ; Private S Johnson 571, 21 ; Private T Mort 561. 155; Corporal F Platt 561,128 6d;Sergeant W H Phcenix 551, lOs ; Private C Garratt 55, 7s 6d, iergeant D Mathews 541' ss. G Company :—Colour-sergeant W H Harvey 661, £2 ; Sergeant W B Cooper 641, £1 15e; Sergeant J F dwards 631, £1 10s ; Private W Gregory 611, £1 5s ; Corporal J Houghton 611, £1; Private J W Hulse 53, 15s ; Lance-Corporal J Sadler 521, 125 6cl ; Lance-Corporal T Hedoock, 501, 105 ; Private W Outhwaite 471, 73 61 ; Ser- geant F J Jeacock 471, 53. Recruits g3neral competition : —Private J. Higgins, 21 ; Private .1.1 Weston, 155 ; Private C Pendleton, 125 Gd; Private J Webb. 103 ; Private J Oeii, 7$ 61 ; Private C Coventry, 58 ; Private T Rowbottom, ss. Private J Brazen- dale, 56. . _ _</p>
        <p>Rstra series :—Corporal J Mort, 20 points ; Lance-Sergeant J F Edwards, 19; Sergeant J A Rushton, 18; Colour. Sergeant J Mort, 13; Lance-Corporal J Sadler, 18 ; Private W Gregory, 18; Private J W Hulse, 18; Private S Joynson, 18; Corporal G Ouhums' 17; Corporal J Houghton, 17 ; Ser- geant W It Phcenix, 17 ; Sergeant S D Matthews,l3 ; Private Garratt,l6 ; Quartermaster-Sergeant J Poole. 16 ; Sergeant F J Jeacock, 16 ; Private J Hargreaves, 16 ; Sergeant W B Cooper, 16 ; Lance-Corporal C Glover. 16; Private T P Mort, 15 ; Sergeant Instructor F Callagan, 15 ; Private T A. Jones, 15 ; Corporal F Platt. 15 ; Private W Oath wane, 14 ; Lance- Sergeant .7 Gittens. 14 ; Private H Shingler, 14 ; Private G Bold, 13 ; Private F Marshall, 13 ; Corporal J Marshall, 13 ; Private F Johnson, 13 ; Private T Chamberlain, 13 ; Lance- Corporal T Hedgcock, 13 ; Private J Higgins, 13 ; Lance- Corporal W Forster. 12 ; Colour-Sergeant H Harvey, 12 ; Private E Cowap, 12 ; Private C Coventry, 12 ; Lance- Corporal It Jones, 12 ; Private L G Hughes, 12 ; Private C Brulsh, 11; Lance-Sergeant W F Gaskell, 11. The following were presented with the Volunteer brig service medals :—Sergeant D Edwards, Lance-Sergeaat J Gittens and Corporal W Walker.</p>
        <p>18; Corporal nt W H r</p>
        <p>Sergeant S D Matthews carried off the Barclay cup for best shot in the detachment, together with attendance at drill. He bad 95 points for shooting and the highest possible attendances at drill.</p>
        <p>Lady Brooke's challenge cup for the best shot in the recruits, was won by Private J Higgins. The (Rowing were donors of the extra series prizes : Messrs W Shaw, jun. G Hill, T Plant, T Griffith,, H Rodick (Weston Point), Shingler (Weston Point), flallbrook, Grounds (Weston Point), C Biroh (Halton), Steele and Robinson, T Lea, L Jackson, H Ashbrook, W Waldgcose. mGo rHziasy, ‘svw oßrat lau k eltr. o sH. , ndilearntoante, ya Bellamy, Gregory. McCauley, W H Mack, Lewis and Company, Light- foot Bradleys, J Bate, W Aakerley, W P 0,3 tdutton,G LPeeaarasnond SIonA, Brookes, R bherat. Mesdames Britland, Waterworth, Turner, Philipps and A Hough. com- mence his remarks with an apology. Captain Aahton who had command of that detachment, had been sud- denly called away to Scotland. 1113 youngest brother wh had been ailing for sometime, had become worse eshk, and he received an urgent call on the previous day to o come at once. He was ssury further co say that the news that day was that there was no improvement. Be was sure they would all extend .with the detachment their sympathy with Captain Ashton in his present trouble. He knew it was one of Captain Ashton's proud moments when he appeared before them annually to give an account of the work of the Volunteer year. ITbat duty had been thrust into his hands suddenly, but he could not say that it was a great ple4Bore to him. The strength of the detachment for the last year WAS ' 194, this year it was 175 so that they had lost 19 during year. The aggregate strength was 232, so that 571 were needed to complete the establishment. They were also entitled to six officers, and at the present they had only two. Be once more appealed to the young men of Run- corn to help to complete the numbers. The strength of the detachment considering the population of Runcorn. was not much. This year the regiment had tbe honour Ito be selected to form part of the new defence, and they went from Runcore 140 strong out of their total:strength of 170. The brigade was composed of the Royal Welsh Fusiliers, south Lancashires and the 2nd Liverpool. They had fourteen slays training on Salisbury Plain. Met of them would remember that during that time it was thelhottest period of the year,so they could imagine what it was for the volunteers. The second week was very wet, and quite a change to the weather they had in the first iweek. It was a period of instruction and they learned a great deal. They had the honour of having a field day, General Buller bringing a big force down from Aldershot. The General complimented them very strongly on the way they carried out all the raulluyrrinougntdhetheev'irenoilnugntaeerhiCgohr P work. Be hoped that the young men of Runcorn woull class concert was givens in which the best elements of muaical culture were brought into combination. Votes of thanks brought the meeting to a close.</p>
      </div>
