        <p>St. Helens and the Pollution of Sankey Brook. The Local Government Board and the St Helens Corporation have been in opposition for a great number of years on the question of sewage dis- posal. The W arrington Corporation and the county authorities have complained of the St. Helens authority turning all their sewage absolutely un- treated into the Sankey Brook, which has for years been locally known as the " stinking " brook. The brook wanders on through Earlestown and Sankey to Warrington and the Mersey. and is everywhere a source of offence. The St. Helens Corporation have always urged that the large amount of chemi- cal drainage which goes into the brook serves to dis— infect and deodorize in a perfect manner the sewage.. Dr Robertson, who is now medical officer of Sheffield. practically proved this to the Local ..iovernment Board, The Corporation were obliged to admit that the sewage would be all the better foe- being precipitated and the residuum treated proper. ly, but on financial grounds, and to give time for other work to be carried out, the Corporation have postponed any effort to meet the wishes of the Local Government Board. Experiments have been made, and various places visited, with the object of deciding on the " system." The septic treatment had been tried, but is found too costly, and the Cor- poration are stated to have decided upon the old- fashioned system of precipitating the sewage by means of Jima. The necessary works it is expected will cost about £18,500, but in addition it is sug• Rested that a destructor will have to be erected. Application will be made. to the Local Government Board for borrowing powers.</p>
