        <p>But I also said that I should not defend H Roberts, he was quite capable of doing that for himself, neither &amp;hunk) I judge him on your tines. I cannot recall the exact words, but that I am certain is the substance. Mr Williams makes a good deal more of it than that and the sooner he is put right the better,—Yours faithfully, (Signed) F MONKS. --- Penketh House near Warrington, Bth November, 1901. Dear Mr. Monks. I have your yesterday's note, and while:l should be the last man to insist upon my own re- collection of words, used by a gentleman, as against my own, I must say I have a most clear and distinct re- membrance of what you said, and it was in effect that you and your son were both entirely with me in regard to the improper Municipal Trading by the Mayor, and other members of the Council, and considered it ought to be put a stop to, and that with regard to " Henry Roberts " (you used his name in this way) you had yourself on several occasions for years past, told him (" warned him" I think were the exact words you used) that he was in a wrong position. You then proceeded to say that I was entirely wrong in taking up the" Consolidation' question, and to state your reasons for so saying. Of course I was pleased to find that you and Mr. ' Fred were with DTI in regard to the improper trading (*8 I gather from your present letter you really are),l and I was naturally glad to be able to repeat your words to Mr. Norbury Williams when I saw him the next day. I have sent him a copy of your letter and of this. sincerely, (Signed) JAMES DODDS, F. Monks Esq.' J.P., Walton Old Hall. Ihe Wood-Blocks Tencier. Explanation from the Committee. We have been asked to publish the following : Re PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE AND TENDERS FOR BEST BED DEAL BLOCKS. In the issue of th; " Warrington Guardian " of November 9th, a letter is published signed " L Henshall." We do not desire to carry on any correspondence in the press re the above matter, but to prove the want of fair play and the I absence of truth in Mr. Henshall's letter we enclose you a copy of the tender received from R. A. Naylor. A full and complete explanation of the whole of the circumstances and conditions of these tenders will be given at the text Council meeting, but in the meae. time we think it desirable that the ratepayers shoul i know how false and misleading is Mr. Henshall's letter, and we shall he otaliged if you will publish this in the 1" Examiner" of Saturday next, It will thus be seen that R. A. Navlor's tender for the block specified is £9 10s per 1,000. Yours truly, ROBERT HEATON, Chairman. DAVID TINNION, Vice-Chairman, November 15, 190 L COPY OF TENDER RECEIVED FROM R. A. NAYLOR. 3019/01. Thomas Longdin, Esq., Borough Engineer's Office. Town Hall. Tender for 58,000 Wood Blocks, carriage paid to Warrington. We offer Red Deal Bloeks 9 by 6 by 3 thoroughly creosoted as below The spe:ial block as now being supplied to the Tramway contract and which are selected specially free from sap and defects and to the Engineer's strict specificatlon at 19; per 100 ; a good block to same size, at 18; ner 100 ; a block which is not so eloeely selected, at 16s 61 per 100 ; and we shall be pleased to have the order for your requirements. We can offer , several other sizes of paving blocks.—Youra truly, (Signed) R. A. NAYLOR.</p>
